(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m2-n93"]={"id":"n93","label":"Notes 9.3 · clearest slide-by-slide notes","revision":"ling406-modern-m2-n93-2","scripts":{"speech1-01":[],"speech1-02":[{"text":"**Speech perception** is the process of recovering a linguistic message from the sound of speech. Listeners take an acoustic signal that seems like a mess and retrieve a message as clear as “I can see you.”","children":["In the signal for “I can see you,” the energy of each sound runs into the next. Nothing in the signal cleanly separates letters or even words.","Real-time MRI of the **vocal tract** from USC SPAN reveals why. The tongue, lips and other articulators move without pause. The movement for one sound overlaps the movement for the next, so their acoustic effects overlap as well."]},{"text":"Listeners also extract **the same message from very different physical stimuli**. Voices and vocal tract sizes differ, so one word has different acoustic properties from each speaker. Listeners still hear the same word.","children":["Across **76 speakers**, each of ten vowels covers a broad range of first and second formant values.","Many of those ranges **overlap**. So one pair of formant values can be one vowel for one speaker and a different vowel for another speaker."]},{"text":"That is why recognizing speech means finding a **stable linguistic identity** inside enormous physical variation.","children":[]}],"speech1-03":[{"text":"A working premise is that listeners must **turn a highly variable, analog signal into something more abstract**. The language system can then operate on that abstract form.","children":["An **analog signal** varies continuously, so frequency, loudness and timing can each shift by tiny amounts.","A continuous signal has no built-in markers of where one speech sound stops and the next starts.","Many small changes leave the speech sound the same, so two physically different productions can be the same sound."]},{"text":"The abstract form is a **symbolic representation**. It identifies a language unit, such as a particular consonant or vowel, and sets the fine physical detail aside.","children":["The unit is the **phoneme**, a sound category that distinguishes words in a language.","/b/ in “bat” and /p/ in “pat” are different phonemes, because swapping them changes the word.","Many slightly different acoustic versions of /b/ all count as one phoneme."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** is thought to reflect this **“translation” from physical to symbolic**. Listeners are less sensitive to differences inside a category. They are more sensitive to equally large differences that cross a category boundary.","children":["A change stands out when it alters the identity the listener gives the sound.","A change of the same physical size can pass almost unnoticed when both sounds remain the same phoneme.","So categorical perception is one account of the translation, and other accounts exist."]}],"speech1-04":[{"text":"The **vocal tract** is the passage sound travels through in speech. It runs from the larynx through the throat and mouth to the lips. Its shape changes as the tongue, jaw and lips move.","children":[]},{"text":"Following **Ladefoged (1996)**, the shape of the vocal tract is related in systematic ways to **which resonant frequencies get amplified**.","children":["A **resonant frequency** is one that a space naturally strengthens. A tube of a given shape boosts some frequencies and weakens others.","Changing the shape of the vocal tract changes which frequencies get the biggest boost."]},{"text":"**Formants** are the resonant frequencies the vocal tract emphasizes. Every vowel has its own combination of formants.","children":["**For heed, hid, head and had**, the tongue moves from high and front toward low. Heed has its second formant far above its first. The two formants draw closer through hid, head and had.","**For hod, hawed, hood and who’d**, the tongue moves back and the lips round more. The amplified frequencies gather at the low end, so the first and second formants sit close together.","A vowel is defined by the **relationship among its resonances**. That relationship stays similar across speakers even when the exact frequencies differ."]},{"text":"A **formant transition** is a change in a formant’s frequency over time.","children":["As the mouth moves from a consonant into a vowel, the resonances glide from one set of values to another.","The steady part of a vowel gives information about the vowel. The moving part gives information about how the neighboring sound is made."]},{"text":"So changing the shape of the vocal tract changes the formants. The pattern of formants is what tells one vowel from another.","children":[]}],"speech1-05":[{"text":"A **spectrogram** maps acoustic energy over time and frequency. Each vowel has several bands of strong energy, and these bands are its **formants**.","children":["The recordings are Rob Hagiwara’s English vowel samples."]},{"text":"The words **bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood” and booed** all begin with /b/ and end with /d/. The consonants are the same, so the differences between the words come mainly from the vowels.","children":["**For bead**, the first formant is very low and the second formant is very high, above 2,000 Hz. The two formants are far apart.","**Through bid, bade, bed and bad**, the first formant rises and the second formant falls. The two formants move closer together.","**For bode, “bood” and booed**, the back rounded vowels, the second formant drops low, near 1,000 Hz or below. It sits near the first formant."]},{"text":"**F1** is the lowest formant and **F2** is the next one up. Both run through the whole vowel at the same time.","children":["Bead pairs a **low F1 with a high F2**. Booed pairs a **low F1 with a low F2**.","So the pattern of the two lowest formants gives most of what tells vowels apart."]}],"speech1-06":[{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** builds speech sounds from two parts. The **source** is the sound energy the voice produces. The **filter** is how the vocal tract shapes that energy.","children":["Separating the two parts makes it possible to change one while the other stays fixed.","The work is done in **Praat**, free speech analysis software from praat.org. Praat displays recordings, measures formants and resynthesizes sounds."]},{"text":"The recording is a speaker saying **/u/**, the vowel in “booed.”","children":["A **waveform** records changes in air pressure over time, so it captures timing and loudness.","A **spectrogram** breaks the same sound into its frequency components, so it captures the resonance pattern."]},{"text":"This /u/ has its **first formant at about 300 Hz**. Its **second formant is very low, around 900 Hz**.","children":["Both resonances run together through the whole vowel.","A low F1 and a low F2 together fit the back rounded quality of /u/."]},{"text":"If changing only the filter changes the vowel, the vowel’s identity must lie in the filter. The filter is the vowel’s pattern of formants. That is why these measured formants are the starting point for building new filters.","children":[]}],"speech1-07":[{"text":"**Praat can find formants automatically**. It follows each resonance through a recording and marks its frequency moment by moment. In this recording of /u/ it identifies **five formants**.","children":["Several resonances run at the same time, and each one is a frequency component of the same sound.","Automatic tracking measures the resonance pattern without anyone estimating frequencies by eye."]},{"text":"**Only the first three formants are usually important for phonemic processing**.","children":["The numbers give the order of the resonances from low to high. F1 is the lowest, F2 is next and F3 is third.","F1, F2 and F3 give most of the information that separates phonemes. The higher formants depend more on the individual voice."]},{"text":"The synthesis changes only **the first two formants**. In this /u/ they sit near 300 Hz and 900 Hz.","children":["These two formants hold most of the vowel information, so changing them is enough to turn one vowel into another.","F3 and the higher formants stay fixed. So any change listeners hear comes from F1 or F2 alone."]}],"speech1-08":[{"text":"The **source** can be **extracted** from the recorded /u/. Praat builds a model of the filter from the measured formant frequencies. That model is used to strip the filter’s effect from the recording.","children":["What remains is a **more-or-less “unfiltered” version of the voice**. It is the sound energy the vocal folds supplied.","It is only more or less unfiltered, because the separation is an estimate."]},{"text":"The extracted source **sounds like a buzz**.","children":["Its energy is spread across many frequencies, without the strong low formants of /u/.","The pitch survives, because pitch comes from vocal fold vibration.","The vowel quality disappears, because vowel quality comes from the filter."]},{"text":"The separation is **a little bit like doing a multiple regression analysis**.","children":["In multiple regression, one predictor’s contribution is estimated and removed so the rest can be examined.","Here the filter’s contribution is estimated and removed, and what is left is treated as the source."]},{"text":"So with the source isolated, new filters can be applied to it. Keeping one source while swapping filters makes the effect of the resonance pattern testable.","children":[]}],"speech1-09":[{"text":"A **filter** specifies which frequencies get more emphasis and which get less, so every filter is a resonance pattern. Three different filters can be applied to the one source.","children":["**The first filter** is just the first four formants from the original recording of /u/. Applying it should bring back the original vowel.","**The second filter** raises **the first formant a little**. The other formants stay roughly where they were.","**The third filter** raises **the second formant a lot**. F1 and the higher formants stay roughly in place."]},{"text":"Each filter changes one part of the resonance pattern instead of shifting every frequency together.","children":["In the original filter, F1 and F2 are both low, which fits /u/.","Raising F1 moves only the lowest resonance up.","Raising F2 moves only the second resonance up, by a much larger amount."]},{"text":"The **sound source stays the same** for all three filters. No new speaker or recording is needed. So any change in vowel quality comes from the filter.","children":["Comparing the outputs isolates what a change in the lowest resonance does and what a change in the second resonance does."]}],"speech1-10":[{"text":"**Multiplying the source by the filters gives new vowels**. The output sound is the source shaped by the filter.","children":["The source provides the raw sound energy.","The filter decides how much of that energy stays strong in each frequency region.","Each filter boosts a different set of frequencies, so each combination is a different vowel."]},{"text":"One source and three filters give three results.","children":["**With the original filter**, F1 and F2 stay low, so the result keeps the quality of /u/.","**With F1 raised a little**, the lowest resonance rises, so the vowel changes.","**With F2 raised a lot**, the second resonance rises far, so the vowel changes in another way. The back rounded /u/ moves toward a more front vowel."]},{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** separates two contributions that ordinary speech combines. The voice provides the sound, and the vocal tract resonances shape its quality.","children":["All three outputs share the same buzz, pitch and timing, yet they sound like different vowels.","So a researcher can change vowel quality by swapping only the filter. No speaker has to record each version."]}],"speech1-11":[{"text":"The vowel a speaker intends only **partly** determines formant values. Three other things also shape them.","children":["**Anatomical features** differ between speakers of the same language. Vocal tracts vary in length and shape, and a longer tract gives lower resonances overall.","**Different rates of speech** matter, because fast speech gives the articulators less time to reach their targets.","**Context** matters, such as the phones produced before and after a vowel. The tongue and lips are already heading toward the next sound or leaving the previous one. A **phone** is one particular spoken realization of a speech sound."]},{"text":"Across 76 speakers, each of ten vowels covers a wide range of formant values, and the ranges overlap.","children":["The same F1 and F2 values can be different vowels for different speakers.","So no single pair of frequencies defines a vowel for everyone."]},{"text":"Formant values nonetheless give a pretty good description of **vowel space**, especially for **a specific talker speaking carefully**. Vowel space is the layout of a language’s vowels by their formant frequencies.","children":["Within one careful talker, the vowels separate far more cleanly than across many talkers.","A **steady-state formant** is the resonance in the stable middle of a vowel, as opposed to the transitions at its edges.","So, all else being equal, **reasonable approximations of vowels** can be produced **by manipulating steady-state formants**."]}],"speech1-12":[{"text":"The **pattern playback synthesizer** turns a painted spectrographic pattern back into sound. Researchers paint bands on a transparent sheet wherever they want acoustic energy in time and frequency. The machine then plays the pattern aloud.","children":["Changing the painting changes the sound.","So researchers could build controlled, speech-like sounds. They did not have to rely on natural recordings, which vary in many uncontrolled ways."]},{"text":"The machine works by shining light through the painted pattern.","children":["A light source shines through a **cylindrical lens** onto a spinning **tone wheel**. The rings of the tone wheel modulate the light at many frequencies at once.","A lens and a **mirror** send that light onto the painted **spectrogram**. The painted areas pass along the matching frequencies.","A **light collector** and a **photocell** turn the light into an electrical signal.","An **amplifier** and a **loudspeaker** turn that signal into sound."]},{"text":"A researcher can alter a single formant band or transition and hear the result right away.","children":["Every other part of the pattern stays exactly the same. So any change in what listeners hear comes from the one painted change.","That is why series of patterns differing in tiny steps revealed how formant transitions signal consonants."]}],"speech1-13":[{"text":"**Stop consonants are distinguished by formant transitions**. The stop consonants **/b/, /d/ and /g/** are made by briefly closing the vocal tract and then opening it.","children":["The audible part of a /b/, /d/ or /g/ happens **while the jaw is opening**.","During that opening, the vocal tract changes shape quickly.","The changing shape makes the formants glide from the consonant’s position toward the vowel’s position. So the consonant’s identity lies in how the formants move."]},{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** used the **pattern playback synthesizer** to create speech-like sounds that replicated these formant transitions. Each painted pattern had two formants.","children":["**The lower formant** was nearly the same in every stimulus.","**The upper formant** started at a different frequency in each stimulus and then settled at the same steady vowel level.","In the early stimuli it started low and **rose** into the vowel. In the middle stimuli it was nearly **flat**. In the later stimuli it started high and **fell** into the vowel."]},{"text":"The fourteen patterns kept the vowel the same and changed only where the upper transition began.","children":["A rising transition was heard as **/b/**. A roughly flat transition was heard as **/d/**. A falling transition was heard as **/g/**.","So listeners’ responses could be tied directly to the transition, because only the transition differed."]}],"speech1-14":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** is a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property. This continuum **keeps F1 constant** and **varies the F2 onset frequency linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13.","children":["The **F2 onset frequency** is the frequency at which the second formant begins its transition.","Keeping F1 constant removes one possible difference between stimuli.","Varying the F2 onset linearly means it changes by the same step every time. So **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"The F2 transition shifts gradually along the series.","children":["**For the early stimuli**, F2 starts well below the vowel frequency, so the transition rises.","**For the middle stimuli**, the onset is higher, so the rise shrinks until the transition is nearly flat.","**For the later stimuli**, F2 starts above the vowel frequency, so the transition falls."]},{"text":"Every step is physically equal, so the continuum is a fair test of whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories.","children":["Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category, and other pairs straddle a boundary.","A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size.","If perception is categorical, steps within a category will sound alike and steps across a boundary will sound different.","So any advantage for boundary pairs comes from the boundary itself."]}],"speech1-15":[{"text":"An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond if perception worked according to one particular account. This simple version assumes that perception is **non-categorical**.","children":["Non-categorical perception tracks the acoustic signal gradually, with no sharp category boundaries.","The model is a baseline. Real data can be compared against it to see whether perception is more categorical than a gradual account allows."]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**. A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category.","children":["The endpoints of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**.","A stimulus farther from the /b/ prototype sounds less like /b/ and more like /d/.","**Stimulus 2** would be labeled **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**. It sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype."]},{"text":"So under this account, **labeling** changes smoothly.","children":["Responses for one category fade out in a straight slope while responses for the neighboring category fade in.","The /b/ responses reach 0 at stimulus 6, where the /d/ responses peak at 1. The /d/ responses fall back to 0 at stimulus 10, where the /g/ responses reach 1.","Two neighboring labels are equally likely halfway between their prototypes. No label changes in a sudden step."]}],"speech1-16":[{"text":"The same simple **ideal observer** can assume that perception is **linear**. Linear perception means equal acoustic differences are perceived as equally large. This leads to a second prediction, that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/.","**Discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are the **same or different**.","These are separate tasks, even when they use the same continuum."]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**.","children":["There are three categories, and listeners are choosing between labels.","So each category’s responses rise toward its prototype and fall away from it.","These peaks come from the structure of the labeling task itself."]},{"text":"Discrimination works differently under the linear account. Pairs are compared two steps apart, from **1 and 3** through **9 and 11**. Every pair has the same physical separation.","children":["If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, discrimination accuracy would also be constant.","So accuracy would stay level for every pair, at about 0.8, even where labeling changes.","A listener can be unsure which label to choose and still hear that two sounds differ."]},{"text":"So the two possible outcomes mean different things. A **flat** pattern would mean category boundaries play no role in hearing differences. A **peaked** pattern would mean the boundaries make some differences easier to hear.","children":[]}],"speech1-17":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** measured actual labeling and discrimination along the /b/, /d/ and /g/ continuum. Their results departed sharply from the non-categorical, linear predictions, and the pattern is called **categorical perception**.","children":["Listeners labeled each stimulus.","Listeners also judged pairs of stimuli that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart."]},{"text":"Labeling changed **sharply** from one consonant to the next.","children":["**Stimuli 1 through about 3** were labeled /b/ close to 100% of the time.","**Around stimuli 4 and 5**, /b/ responses dropped steeply and /d/ responses climbed to nearly 100%. /d/ stayed dominant through about stimulus 8.","**Around stimuli 9 and 10**, /d/ responses dropped steeply and /g/ responses rose to near 100%.","These switches are far more abrupt than the gradual slopes the ideal observer predicted. The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**.","children":["Accuracy peaked at the /b/ to /d/ boundary and again at the /d/ to /g/ boundary.","Pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance**, which is the level expected from guessing.","Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries."]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labeling data. They assumed listeners can tell two sounds apart only when they give them different labels.","children":["The predictions captured much of the real pattern.","Real accuracy was often a little higher than predicted, especially within categories.","So listeners keep some sensitivity to acoustic differences beyond the category labels alone."]}],"speech1-18":[],"speech1-19":[{"text":"Data from students in prior years tested **categorical perception** along a **beach to peach continuum**. The two words differ in their first consonant, /b/ or /p/.","children":["In the **identification** task, each listener labeled every stimulus from 1 to 9.","In the **discrimination** task, each listener judged pairs two steps apart, from stimuli 1 and 3 through stimuli 7 and 9."]},{"text":"**For the first listener**, identification stays on one word for the early stimuli. It then **switches to the other word** around the middle of the continuum.","children":["Responses are mixed only in a small region near the switch. The acoustic change is gradual, but the label changes within a narrow region.","Discrimination is best for the pair of stimuli 4 and 6 and the pair of stimuli 5 and 7. These pairs **straddle the identification boundary**.","Pairs well inside one category are hard to tell apart, even though their physical separation is the same.","The pair of stimuli 6 and 8 falls in between, because stimulus 6 itself received mixed labels."]},{"text":"**For the second listener**, the word label **switches later** along the continuum. Discrimination is best near that later boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"So different people can place their category boundaries at different points. For each listener, the best discrimination falls close to that person’s **own identification boundary**.","children":[]}],"speech1-20":[{"text":"The data from prior years support a few general points. The first is that **not everyone has exactly the same category boundary**.","children":["A sound near the beach to peach boundary for one listener can fall squarely inside a category for another listener.","A pair that straddles one person’s boundary may stay inside one category for someone else.","So when individual boundaries differ, averaging across the group can flatten the boundary peak."]},{"text":"**In general, discrimination is best for stimuli perceived as being in different categories**.","children":["Listeners may miss a physical difference between two versions of the same phoneme.","A difference of the same size is far easier to hear when the two sounds are heard as different words."]},{"text":"The discrimination experiment included **only “different” trials**, which made the lab doable in a short amount of time. Every pair held two physically different stimuli.","children":["The correct answer on every trial was **“different,”** if the listener was able to discriminate the two sounds.","Answering “same” meant missing a real difference.","Accuracy is the share of pairs heard as different. Someone who always said “different” would score perfectly.","So low accuracy on within-category pairs means listeners often failed to hear differences that were really there."]}],"speech1-21":[{"text":"More results from prior years compare a **bear to pear continuum** with the **beach to peach continuum**.","children":["**For bear to pear**, identification switches fairly clearly from one word to the other. Discrimination jumps between 0 and 1 from one pair to the next. It includes a miss on a pair that crosses the boundary.","**For another listener’s beach to peach data**, the responses fluctuate even more. Identification zigzags instead of switching once, and discrimination bounces between high and low values."]},{"text":"The one iron-clad law of experimental research is that **stuff happens**. Responses to a carefully built continuum can still bounce around.","children":["Identification records which word a listener chooses. Discrimination records whether a difference is detected. These two behaviors can vary in different ways across trials and people.","An isolated rise or drop in discrimination reflects noise in a few responses."]},{"text":"The experiment was not the best in two ways, and it could be done differently.","children":["Only “different” trials were used. So nothing measured how often listeners said “different” when the sounds were identical.","The short session gave each listener **few trials per pair**. So a single response could move accuracy a great deal.","So a better version would include trials with identical sounds and more trials for each pair.","The real test is whether better discrimination lines up with the place where identification changes, across the whole continuum."]}],"speech1-22":[{"text":"It is not obvious **how categorical perception would help**, or what the advantage is of being worse at hearing some differences between sounds.","children":["Categorical perception makes listeners worse at hearing differences within a category.","That can seem like a weakness, because the listener is throwing away acoustic detail."]},{"text":"The advantage is **stable word recognition**.","children":["Different productions of the same phoneme are always physically different, because of speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds.","If every small acoustic change were heard as a new identity, everyday versions of one word would sound like unrelated sounds.","Reduced sensitivity within a category lets many acoustic versions count as **one phoneme**."]},{"text":"Differences **between categories** stay easy to hear, so the distinctions that change words are kept.","children":["Variation inside a category is tolerated, because it leaves the word unchanged.","So ignoring small within-category differences frees the listener to focus on the differences that change meaning."]}],"speech1-23":[],"speech1-24":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** can be tested even though infants cannot name sounds. Infants react to novelty, so researchers watch for a behavior that changes when an infant notices something new.","children":[]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** comes from Janet Werker’s infant studies lab.","children":["An infant learns to turn toward a sound when the sound changes.","A correct turn earns an interesting visual reward, such as a moving toy.","After the infant learns this link, a head turn following a change means the infant detected the change."]},{"text":"The infant results on categorical perception come from **high-amplitude sucking**.","children":["The infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a recording device, and strong sucks make a sound play.","As the same sound repeats, the infant grows used to it and sucking slows. Growing used to a repeated sound is **habituation**.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change."]},{"text":"Both methods make perception testable before an infant can talk.","children":["The experimenter controls exactly which sound difference is presented.","So a head turn or renewed sucking reveals sensitivity to that difference without any labeling. This works even in infants who are months from saying words."]}],"speech1-25":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **4-month-old infants** using **high-amplitude sucking**. The sounds varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**. VOT is the delay between the release of a consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["Adults hear a short VOT as a voiced consonant such as /b/. Adults hear a longer VOT as a voiceless consonant such as /p/.","With a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing begins much later after the release than with a VOT of +10 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Infants heard one synthetic syllable over and over until their sucking slowed down. Then the sound either changed or stayed the same.","children":["**In condition 20D**, the VOT changed from **+20 to +40 milliseconds**. This 20-millisecond change **crossed** the adult boundary between two consonant categories.","**In condition 20S**, the VOT also changed by **20 milliseconds**, from −20 to 0 or from +60 to +80. Both sounds came from the **same** adult category.","**In condition 0**, the control group kept hearing the same sound."]},{"text":"In every group, sucking rose at first and then dropped as the sound became familiar.","children":["**In 20D**, sucking **recovered** sharply after the change.","**In 20S**, sucking kept falling with little recovery.","**In 0**, sucking kept declining steadily."]},{"text":"The two changed conditions were matched at 20 milliseconds, so the difference comes from crossing the boundary.","children":["Infants noticed a change that adults would hear as a new consonant. They did not notice an equally large change inside one category.","So even at four months, infants perceive voicing **categorically**."]}],"speech1-26":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** is the process in which experience tunes perception to the sounds of the surrounding language. Along the way, sensitivity to some distinctions from other languages declines.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested how age and experience affect infants’ ability to discriminate two **non-English contrasts**.","children":["The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two “ta”-like sounds that English does not use.","The **Salish contrast** is a distinction between “ki” and “qi” in Nthlakampx, a Salish language. English does not use it either.","The measure was the percentage of infants who reached a **discrimination criterion** at each age."]},{"text":"The English-learning infants did worse as they got older.","children":["**At 6 to 8 months**, about 95% met criterion for Hindi and about 80% for Salish.","**At 8 to 10 months**, about 67% met criterion for Hindi and about 52% for Salish.","**At 10 to 12 months**, about 20% met criterion for Hindi and about 10% for Salish.","**Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants** met criterion close to 100% of the time for the contrast from their own language."]},{"text":"These results are described as **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**.","children":["Young infants can detect these nonnative contrasts.","With experience of English, English-learning infants become much less likely to discriminate them by about their first birthday.","Infants who hear the contrast in their own language keep it, so the decline depends on language experience.","So contrasts the language uses are kept, and contrasts it lacks become hard to hear."]}],"speech1-27":[{"text":"The earlier findings feed **a very compelling narrative** about speech perception. The narrative makes three linked claims.","children":[]},{"text":"**The first claim** is that speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities that are a special property of the language system**.","children":["On this claim, the mechanism that sorts sounds into categories is specialized for language."]},{"text":"**The second claim** is that these abilities are **present very early in development**.","children":["The infant sucking and head-turn experiments suggest the abilities are in place long before children talk."]},{"text":"**The third claim** is that children **start off as “citizens of the world,”** able to hear **all** contrasts. They then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems to their specific language**.","children":["As “citizens of the world,” infants could learn any human language and are ready to hear the distinctions of every one.","Tuning means the contrasts of the surrounding language stay available while contrasts from other languages fade, as in the Werker and Tees results."]},{"text":"So the narrative covers both the **starting state** and **developmental change**.","children":["It proposes broad, language-specific abilities at birth.","It treats development mostly as narrowing from that broad start.","Each early result seems to fit. Infants discriminate categorically before talking. They hear contrasts from many languages. They lose nonnative contrasts with experience."]}],"speech1-28":[{"text":"The compelling narrative meets **some issues**. Three of them concern **categorical perception**, and one concerns **infant speech perception**.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception is **not limited to language**.","children":["It also appears with nonspeech sounds. So the claim that categorical perception is special to language is weakened."]},{"text":"Categorical perception is **not limited to humans**.","children":["Nonhuman animals also perceive speech sounds categorically. So the claim that categorical perception is special to humans is weakened."]},{"text":"Categorical perception is **task-dependent**.","children":["Results look more or less categorical depending on what the listener is asked to do.","Choosing between labels makes responses more categorical than a task that measures fine acoustic differences."]},{"text":"Infant speech perception is **not actually “universal.”**","children":["Some contrasts are harder for infants to detect than others from early on.","So development includes initial differences in sensitivity as well as changes driven by experience."]},{"text":"That is why these issues weaken the strong claim that every speech distinction starts out equally available in a mechanism devoted only to language.","children":[]}],"speech1-29":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** tested categorical perception with **non-speech sounds**. The sounds varied in **rise time**, which is how quickly a sound reaches full loudness after it begins.","children":["A sound with a **rapid rise time** sounds **plucked**, like a plucked string.","A sound with a **slow rise time** sounds **bowed**, like a bowed string.","Pluck and bow are labels for two kinds of sound onset. They are not phonemes."]},{"text":"The stimuli included **sawtooth waves**, **sine waves** and **speech syllables**. Each was made with short and long rise times, such as 10 and 70 milliseconds.","children":["**With a 10-millisecond rise**, the sound reaches full strength almost at once.","**With a 70-millisecond rise**, the sound builds up gradually."]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, listeners labeled the sounds and discriminated pairs that differed by 10 milliseconds.","children":["Short rise times were labeled pluck close to 100% of the time. Long rise times were labeled bow close to 100% of the time.","The label switched steeply around 30 to 50 milliseconds, in the middle of the continuum.","Discrimination stayed around 55 to 60% for most pairs, only a little above chance.","Discrimination **peaked near 75%** for the pairs around 30 to 50 milliseconds, right where the labels switched."]},{"text":"So the link between a label boundary and better discrimination appears for a sound distinction that has nothing to do with language. The physical continuum is gradual, yet the label changes over a narrow region and discrimination improves there.","children":[]}],"speech1-30":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** tested whether **infants** show categorical perception of **non-speech sounds**. They used **high-amplitude sucking** to measure whether a change in sound was detected.","children":["Infants heard one sound repeatedly until their sucking declined.","The sound was then shifted, and the change was compared across three conditions."]},{"text":"The three conditions were **no change**, a change **within categories** and a change **between categories**. In every condition, sucking declined before the shift as the sound became familiar.","children":["**After no change**, sucking stayed low.","**After a within-category change**, sucking also stayed low.","**After a between-category change**, sucking jumped back up. It then declined again as the new sound became familiar."]},{"text":"So infants responded more strongly to a non-speech change that crossed a category boundary than to a change within a category.","children":["This mirrors the pattern **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found for speech.","Infants cannot be using knowledge of words or phonemes when they respond to plucked and bowed sounds.","So their categorical response must come from more general auditory processing. The same processing could also underlie their categorical response to speech."]}],"speech1-31":[{"text":"Categorical perception of speech sounds has also been found in **chinchillas**. The evidence comes from **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** and the earlier work of **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**.","children":[]},{"text":"The animals were trained with **operant conditioning**, a form of learning in which consequences change how often a behavior occurs.","children":["Each chinchilla learned that either the **0-millisecond lag** stimulus or the **80-millisecond lag** stimulus was an **SD** for food.","**SD** stands for **discriminative stimulus**, which is a signal that a response will be rewarded.","The lag is **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"After training, the chinchillas were tested with the VOTs in between, along the **/ga/ to /ka/ continuum**. Human listeners were tested on the same sounds.","children":["Short VOTs were labeled /ga/ almost every time. Long VOTs were almost never labeled /ga/.","The switch happened over a fairly narrow range of VOTs, mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds.","The chinchillas’ labeling had the same steep, categorical shape as the humans’ labeling."]},{"text":"The study also varied the **burst** and the **aspiration noise**. The burst is the brief noise at the release of a stop consonant. Aspiration is the breathy noise that follows a voiceless release.","children":["Making the burst and aspiration louder shifted the boundary toward shorter VOTs, made the switch steeper, or both.","This happened for one chinchilla and for one human listener, so both species responded to these sound properties in a similar way."]},{"text":"So animals without human language can sort a speech continuum into categories. That argues against the view that categorical perception is special to human language.","children":[]}],"speech1-32":[{"text":"Some contrasts are **less salient than others** for infants. A **salient** contrast is one that is easy to notice.","children":["The contrasts tested here differ in **place of articulation**, which is where in the mouth a sound is made.","**In [ma]**, the nasal is made with the lips.","**In [na]**, the nasal is made with the tongue near the ridge behind the teeth.","**In [ŋa]**, the nasal is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate. [ŋ] is the sound at the end of “sing.”"]},{"text":"Researchers compared infants’ **looking time** on **same** trials and **change** trials. On same trials the syllable stayed the same. On change trials it switched to the other syllable. Longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change.","children":[]},{"text":"**For English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months**, the result depended on the contrast.","children":["For the native **[ma] and [na]** contrast, they looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 5.4 seconds on change trials. So they detected the change.","For the nonnative **[na] and [ŋa]** contrast, they looked about 4.5 seconds on same trials and about 4.2 seconds on change trials. So they did not detect the change.","English never uses [ŋ] at the start of a syllable."]},{"text":"**For Filipino-learning infants**, [na] and [ŋa] is a contrast in their own language.","children":["At **6 to 8 months**, they looked about 3.8 seconds on both kinds of trials, so they did not yet detect the change.","By **10 to 12 months**, looking rose from about 3.3 seconds on same trials to about 4.8 seconds on change trials."]},{"text":"So some contrasts start out hard for infants to hear, even when they belong to the native language. Experience with the language **strengthens** sensitivity to such contrasts over the first year. That is why development includes gains as well as losses.","children":[]}],"speech2-01":[],"speech2-02":[{"text":"**Klatt (1979)** describes a very old and very general model of speech perception. Recognition runs through a series of stages. Each stage transforms the output of the stage before it.","children":["The **speech waveform** enters the **peripheral auditory system**, which does a **spectral analysis**. The **cochlea** in the inner ear splits the sound into its frequency components.","**Acoustic property detectors** respond to particular properties of the sound.","**Phonetic feature detectors** estimate the probability of features such as voicing at each moment.","**Segmental analysis** combines the features into a sequence of speech segments.","**Lexical search** compares that sequence with stored words and produces **lexical hypotheses**, which are candidate words."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** fits this model. **Information is lost** somewhere between the **analog signal** from the cochlea and the **activation of phonemes**.","children":["Many physically different sounds become the same phoneme.","This **loss of information is helpful**, because a phoneme sequence can make contact with higher levels of representation.","One assumption follows from this model. To identify words, listeners must **first identify their constituent phonemes**."]},{"text":"Two problems challenge this simple view.","children":["The first problem is **cascaded processing**. Partial information moves on to the next level before the earlier decision is finished.","The second problem is **feedback**. A higher level, such as words, sends influence back down to a lower level, such as phonemes."]},{"text":"So the simple view, in which listeners identify every phoneme before finding the word, is challenged by both cascading and feedback.","children":[]}],"speech2-03":[{"text":"The **beach and peach continuum** is a familiar **voicing continuum**. The two words differ in their first consonant, which is /b/ in beach and /p/ in peach.","children":["These consonants differ mainly in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**. VOT is the delay between the release of the lips and the start of vocal fold vibration.","VOT gets shorter in equal steps along the continuum from “peach” to “beach”.","A **long delay** before voicing sounds like peach. A **short delay** sounds like beach."]},{"text":"In **identification**, one listener labels each stimulus as one of the two words.","children":["Stimuli 1 through 5 are labeled consistently as one word.","Stimulus 6 gets mixed labels, with a proportion near 0.33.","Stimuli 7 through 9 are labeled consistently as the other word.","The **category boundary** is the narrow region where the preferred label switches."]},{"text":"In **discrimination**, the same listener's results follow the boundary.","children":["Pairs inside one category are almost never heard as different.","Pairs 4 and 6 and 5 and 7 straddle the boundary, so they are heard as different every time.","Pair 6 and 8 is intermediate, near 0.33, and pair 7 and 9 falls back to 0."]},{"text":"So equal changes in VOT are **much easier to detect across the boundary** than inside one category. A step across the boundary changes the word. A step inside one category leaves the word unchanged.","children":[]}],"speech2-04":[{"text":"A **threshold model** is one answer to how categorical perception happens. In this model, **activity is only passed forward** from one level to the next when an activity **threshold** is crossed.","children":["A threshold is a set level of activity that must be reached before anything is sent onward.","Activity below the threshold can rise and fall without any effect on the next level."]},{"text":"In this kind of model, **categorical perception arises at phoneme identification**. Consider the voicing evidence for a sound between /b/ and /p/.","children":["**At the feature level**, the voicing evidence swings up and down between a **+voiced** threshold and a **−voiced** threshold.","**At the phoneme level**, /b/ and /p/ both stay silent while the evidence wavers below threshold.","When the evidence finally crosses the +voiced threshold, **/b/ switches on all at once**."]},{"text":"An ambiguous stimulus with mixed responses either crosses a threshold or does not.","children":["The word level receives a finished /b/ or a finished /p/.","The earlier uncertainty stays behind at the feature level.","Two acoustically different sounds that both end up as /b/ are identical by the time they reach word recognition."]},{"text":"That is why this **all-or-none switch** would produce categorical perception directly at the phoneme level.","children":[]}],"speech2-05":[{"text":"A **cascaded model** gives a different answer to how categorical perception happens. In this model, **activity is passed forward continuously** over time from one level to the next.","children":["Each level responds to whatever activity is present at that moment.","The next level responds while the earlier decision is still forming."]},{"text":"Feature activity and phoneme activity rise and fall together.","children":["When evidence for **+voiced** grows, **/b/** activation grows.","When the evidence shifts toward **−voiced**, activation shifts toward **/p/**.","When the voicing evidence is mixed, /b/ and /p/ each get activation in proportion to their evidence."]},{"text":"Graded phoneme activation then passes graded activation to words.","children":["A sound near the boundary **partly activates both beach and peach**.","Two sounds that both end up labeled /b/ can still differ in how much they supported /p/."]},{"text":"In this kind of model, categorical perception **might happen later on**. It might even be an **artifact of the task structure**. An artifact is a result created by the way something is measured, not by the process being studied.","children":["An identification task makes the listener click beach or peach, even if both words were partly active.","The answer looks categorical because the response choices are categorical."]},{"text":"So the processing before the final choice can keep fine-grained information about how ambiguous the sound was.","children":[]}],"speech2-06":[{"text":"**Making furniture** is a metaphor for **thresholded activity**. Each operation takes one specific input and turns it into one specific output.","children":["The **lumber mill** takes **trees** as input and produces **lumber**.","The **furniture factory** takes **lumber** as input and produces **unassembled furniture**.","The **IKEA customer** takes **unassembled furniture** as input and produces finished **furniture**."]},{"text":"Each step needs a **finished product** in a form it can use.","children":["Trees must become lumber before any furniture is made.","Lumber must become furniture parts before any assembly begins.","No part of the work at one stage passes forward until the whole job is done."]},{"text":"**Thresholded processing** depends on its inputs in the same way.","children":["A higher level waits until the lower level supplies the representation it needs.","So information moves forward **only as a completed product**."]}],"speech2-07":[{"text":"In the furniture metaphor, each **“layer”** of the system can only take a specific input. That input must be **transformed completely by the previous layer**.","children":["The factory has to wait for the shipment of lumber before manufacturing can start.","Many computer programs work this way. Each step finishes before the next one starts.","A lower stage can keep working while the higher stage sits idle and waits."]},{"text":"In speech recognition, a strict threshold account makes **word processing depend on an identified phoneme**.","children":["A partly resolved acoustic signal stays at the lower level until it is identified as a phoneme.","The word level receives only the finished segment."]},{"text":"This design also decides **what information is lost**.","children":["When different sounds become the same phoneme, their earlier differences never reach word recognition.","This helps the listener treat varied sounds alike.","The cost is that later stages never learn how uncertain the earlier stages were."]},{"text":"So in a thresholded system, each stage hands on only its finished output.","children":[]}],"speech2-08":[{"text":"A **food web** is a metaphor for **cascaded processing**. In a food web, the **“levels” continuously interact** with one another.","children":["The desert food web links desert plants, insects, kangaroo rats, small lizards, large lizards, scorpions, snakes, desert foxes and hawks. The links are who eats whom.","A drought that reduces the number of plants **immediately** affects the health of kangaroo rats.","The change in kangaroo rats then affects the snakes that eat them.","No population waits for the level below it to reach a finished state."]},{"text":"**Cascaded processing** has the same continuous dependence.","children":["Changing feature evidence changes phoneme activity as it happens.","Changing phoneme activity changes the support for words.","So each level responds to partial information as it arrives."]},{"text":"The food web also has clear **feedback loops**.","children":["Adding more hawks changes the populations of snakes, lizards and rats. Those changes then affect the hawks’ own food supply.","In a recognition model, **feedback** is influence that travels from a higher level back down to a lower level."]},{"text":"Cascading and feedback are different ideas. Cascading sends developing evidence **forward**. Feedback lets a higher level **change an earlier representation**.","children":[]}],"speech2-09":[{"text":"The central question is how **phoneme identification for word recognition** works. It could work like **thresholded processing**, as in the lumber mill. It could work like **cascaded processing**, as in the food web.","children":["In a **thresholded account**, word recognition begins only after a phoneme decision is finished.","In a **cascaded account**, a word can gain support while the phoneme is still uncertain."]},{"text":"The two accounts differ in how much developing acoustic evidence reaches candidate words. Two sounds can both be labeled **/b/** and still differ in how much they resemble **/p/**.","children":["**For a thresholded system**, both sounds become the same finished /b/. So pear should **not compete** with bear.","**For a cascaded system**, the more ambiguous sound passes along more /p/ support. So pear should **compete with bear for a while**."]},{"text":"Both accounts can predict the same final word. They differ in what happens before the final choice.","children":["That is why the deciding test measures **temporary support for a competing word** during recognition.","The evidence from eye movements favors the cascaded account."]}],"speech2-10":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** tested whether phoneme identification is thresholded or cascaded. They looked for **gradient effects of within-category VOT on lexical access**.","children":["**Lexical access** is the retrieval of a word from memory during recognition.","**Gradient** means that an effect changes by degrees.","**Within-category VOT** means differences in voice onset time among sounds that are all heard as the same phoneme."]},{"text":"Listeners identified sounds from a continuum between /b/ and /p/ in a **“visual world” display**. **Eye-tracking** recorded where they looked among the pictures while they heard speech.","children":["Each display had a **target**, such as pear, and a **competitor**, such as bear.","Each display also had two unrelated **fillers**, a lamp and a ship.","The target and the competitor differ only in their first consonant."]},{"text":"Eye movements happen quickly and continuously while a word is being recognized. Listeners look at objects as they think about them, often before they know they have decided.","children":["A listener can choose bear in the end after briefly looking at the pear.","A look at the pear means that pear was being considered."]},{"text":"So the number and timing of looks to the competitor measure **how active the competitor was** before the final click.","children":[]}],"speech2-11":[{"text":"Participants used the **mouse to select** whichever of the pictures was most consistent with what they heard, such as a “bear” or a “pear”.","children":["Each trial presented one sound from the VOT continuum with the four pictures.","The click records which word the listener finally settled on."]},{"text":"The clicks gave a **typical, step-like identification function**.","children":["**For VOTs from 0 to about 15 milliseconds**, /p/ responses stay near 0, so listeners almost always clicked bear.","**Between about 15 and 20 milliseconds**, /p/ responses jump up steeply.","**For VOTs from about 20 to 40 milliseconds**, /p/ responses stay near 1, so listeners almost always clicked pear.","The **category boundary** falls at **17.5 milliseconds**, plus or minus 0.83 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Judged by the clicks alone, perception looks fully **categorical**. A sound well inside the /b/ category and a sound just short of the boundary both produce bear.","children":["Taken alone, this sharp switch would seem to support the threshold model.","A click records only the word chosen at the end of recognition.","Eye movements record which other words received support before that choice."]},{"text":"So a sharp identification function can exist together with gradual differences in competition.","children":[]}],"speech2-12":[{"text":"**Eye movements reveal that ambiguity is gradient**. There are **more looks to the competitor close to the boundary**. Gradient means that the uncertainty varies by degree.","children":["The analysis holds the final response constant. It compares only trials with a bear click, or only trials with a pear click.","Every trial in a comparison ended with the same click. So differences in competitor looks cannot come from listeners changing their answer."]},{"text":"**For trials where the response was bear**, the measure is looks to the **pear**. The VOTs were 0, 5, 10 and 15 milliseconds.","children":["Looks to the pear rise after the word begins and peak around 400 to 600 milliseconds. Then they decline as bear wins.","A VOT near the boundary, such as 15 milliseconds, gets more looks to the pear, and they last longer.","A VOT of 0 milliseconds gets the fewest looks to the pear."]},{"text":"**For trials where the response was pear**, the measure is looks to the **bear**. The VOTs were 20, 25, 30, 35 and 40 milliseconds.","children":["A VOT of 20 milliseconds, just past the boundary, gets the most looks to the bear.","A VOT of 40 milliseconds, the clearest /p/, gets the fewest."]},{"text":"The effect of VOT is mainly on the **duration of activation**, which is how long the competitor stays active.","children":[]},{"text":"So the recognition system keeps track of how ambiguous each sound was. A threshold model would throw that information away.","children":[]}],"speech2-13":[{"text":"The gradient effect is clearest when competitor looks are compared across the whole **VOT** range. The **category boundary** sits in the middle of that range.","children":["**On the bear side** of the boundary, listeners clicked bear. The measure is how often they looked at the pear.","**On the pear side** of the boundary, listeners clicked pear. The measure is how often they looked at the bear."]},{"text":"Looks were measured in two time windows.","children":["The **early** window covers **300 to 1100 milliseconds** after the word begins.","The **late** window covers **1100 to 1900 milliseconds** after the word begins."]},{"text":"In both windows, competitor looks **increase as VOT approaches the boundary from either side**.","children":["**On the bear side**, late looks to the pear rise from about 0.02 at 0 milliseconds to about 0.045 near 15 milliseconds.","**On the pear side**, late looks to the bear are about 0.04 near 20 milliseconds. They fall to about 0.01 by 40 milliseconds.","Early looks are higher overall, but they follow the same pattern.","The rise is gradual and not sudden."]},{"text":"The effect appears in both windows, so uncertainty about the first sound affects looking throughout recognition.","children":[]},{"text":"So a sound can support the eventual winner while still supporting its rival. The closer the sound is to the boundary, the stronger that rival support is.","children":[]}],"speech2-14":[{"text":"The eye-movement finding is consistent with **activation flowing in a cascaded way from features to phonemes**. Changing features affect phonemes while recognition is still going on. Changing phonemes affect words in the same way.","children":["Even stimuli identified as /b/ **100% of the time** can vary in how strongly and unambiguously the **“voiced” feature** is activated.","A clear /b/ activates voicing strongly.","A /b/ near the boundary activates voicing only weakly."]},{"text":"**/b/ and /p/** share most of their features.","children":["Both are **bilabial**, which means the two lips form the closure.","Both are **stops**, which means the airflow is completely blocked and then released.","A stimulus that is obviously **+bilabial** and **+stop** supports /b/ and /p/ equally.","Only voicing separates them, so weak voicing evidence creates **momentary ambiguity between /b/ and /p/**.","That ambiguity is reflected in the **eye movements** while the participant decides where to click."]},{"text":"The **TRACE** model represents this cascade with three layers.","children":["The **feature layer** receives a **pseudo-spectral input representation**, a simplified version of the sound’s frequency content over time.","Its detectors are POW, VOC, DIFF, ACU, GRD, VOI and BUR. They stand for power, vocalic, diffuseness, acuteness, gradualness of onset, voicing and burst.","The **phoneme layer** has units for phonemes such as /b/, /p/, /r/ and /t/, repeated at every point in time.","The **word layer** has units for words, also repeated across time.","Activity passes up from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words. Units within a layer compete through **inhibition**."]},{"text":"So in TRACE, partial evidence about voicing reaches the phoneme and word layers before any decision is final.","children":[]}],"speech2-15":[{"text":"**Andruski and colleagues (1994)** gave evidence that **partial activation can reach all the way to the lexical level**. The lexical level is the level of stored words and their meanings.","children":["They found **semantic priming** even with **nonwords**. Semantic priming is the speed-up in responding to a word after hearing a word related in meaning.","The nonword “beace” primes “war”. Beace is peace with a voiced first consonant, and peace is related in meaning to war.","The nonword “ging” primes “queen”. Ging is king with a voiced first consonant, and king is related in meaning to queen.","In the **word to word** items, the altered prime is another real word. The word “bill” primes “drug” because bill is an altered form of pill."]},{"text":"Participants did a **lexical decision task**. They decided as quickly as possible whether a target was a real word.","children":["The primes were **unaltered**, altered by reducing VOT by **one third**, altered by **two thirds**, or **unrelated** to the target.","Reaction times faster than in the unrelated condition count as priming."]},{"text":"The reaction times differed by condition.","children":["**For unrelated primes**, responses were slowest, near 950 to 970 milliseconds.","**For unaltered primes**, responses were much faster, around 810 to 860 milliseconds.","**For one third of VOT removed**, responses stayed fast, around 825 to 855 milliseconds.","**For two thirds removed**, word to nonword items stayed fast, near 820 milliseconds. Word to word items slowed to about 890 milliseconds.","In word to word items, the altered prime also partly activates a competing real word, which reduces the benefit."]},{"text":"So **lexical representations can be activated even by incomplete or imperfect input**. Beace never becomes a real word, but it matches peace closely enough to partly activate it. That activation spreads to meaning and speeds the response to war, which fits **cascaded processing**.","children":[]}],"speech2-16":[{"text":"The bear and pear eye-tracking result is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing** in speech perception. A **thresholded model** has a hard time explaining it.","children":["The number of looks to the other member of the **minimal pair** increases gradually toward the category boundary.","A minimal pair is two words that differ in only one sound, such as bear and pear.","The increase holds even when the final response is the same. So it comes from graded activation within one category."]},{"text":"**In a thresholded model**, graded feature activity never reaches the phoneme level.","children":["Feature activation keeps swinging between +voiced and −voiced.","Phoneme activation stays flat until /b/ suddenly switches on.","So the phoneme level cannot know how close a sound came to the boundary.","Every token in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","Word-level competition would then be the same for every /b/. Nothing would produce gradually changing competitor looks."]},{"text":"**In a cascaded model**, graded information reaches the word level.","children":["More support for the competing phoneme passes along more support for its word."]},{"text":"That is why the gradual rise in competitor looks favors cascaded processing.","children":[]}],"speech2-17":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also includes **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**. Activity runs up from phonemes to words and back down from words to phonemes.","children":["**Lexical feedback** means that an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","An uncertain sound can partly activate a word. That word can then strengthen the reading of the sound that fits it."]},{"text":"So influence runs in both directions.","children":["Acoustic evidence changes which words are active.","Active words change the balance between possible phonemes while the phoneme decision is still forming.","This helps listeners recover from noisy or ambiguous input."]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**.","children":["**Cascading** passes incomplete evidence forward, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words.","**Feedback** sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","An interactive model can include both."]},{"text":"Some experiments motivate adding this kind of feedback. In them, knowledge of words changes how sounds are heard, as in the **Ganong effect**.","children":[]}],"speech2-18":[{"text":"**Ganong (1980)** looked at how **phoneme identification is affected by word knowledge**. The finding is called the **Ganong effect**.","children":["Ganong tested whether knowing which sound sequences are real words changes how an ambiguous sound is heard.","“Gisses and kifts for Valentine’s day” plays on the effect. It swaps the voicing of the first consonants in “kisses and gifts”."]},{"text":"Ganong made **word and nonword pairs** such as “corpse/gorpse”. One end of each continuum is a real word and the other end is a nonword.","children":["**For the /k/ interpretation**, the result is corpse, which is a familiar word.","**For the /g/ interpretation**, the result is gorpse, which is not a word.","The continua spanned the relevant phoneme boundary in **5 millisecond steps** of VOT."]},{"text":"The design combines **uncertain phonetic evidence** with a **difference in lexical status**.","children":["The rest of the word, “orpse”, is the same at every step. It tells the listener which beginning would complete a real word.","If word knowledge affects identification, an ambiguous first consonant should more often be heard as the sound that makes a word."]},{"text":"So the important test is in the **ambiguous middle** of the continuum. Word knowledge has the most room to act where the sound is ambiguous.","children":[]}],"speech2-19":[{"text":"A **late “correction” process** is one prediction for Ganong’s continua. In this account, **categorical perception functions as usual**, but there is a **bias to report having heard a word**.","children":["The listener first categorizes the sound with no help from word knowledge.","At the response stage, the listener leans toward the option that forms a real word."]},{"text":"Three kinds of continua are compared.","children":["**For word-nonword continua**, the voiced end forms a word.","**For nonword-word continua**, the voiceless end forms a word.","**For neutral continua**, neither end has a lexical advantage."]},{"text":"This account predicts a **general reporting bias**. A bias added after perception pushes responses toward the word by the same amount everywhere.","children":["The word-nonword responses are shifted up everywhere. At the long VOT end, voiced responses stay near 0.25 and do not fall to 0.","The nonword-word responses are shifted down. At the short VOT end, voiced responses start near 0.75 and not at 1.","So even a clearly voiceless sound would sometimes be reported as voiced when the voiced version makes a word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** is the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions.","children":["In this account, the lexical effect is **the same size at every step**, near 0.25.","So perception and word knowledge stay separate, and word knowledge only nudges the reported answer."]}],"speech2-20":[{"text":"A second prediction is that **word knowledge shifts the categorical boundary**. In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other.","children":["Near an ambiguous onset, the interpretation that forms a real word gets extra support.","So the effect changes categorization itself, not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"This account predicts that the lexical conditions differ only in the middle of the continuum.","children":["**At both ends**, the sound is clear, so every condition gives the same answer. Voiced responses start near 1 at short VOT and end near 0 at long VOT.","**Near the boundary**, the word-nonword condition crosses later and the nonword-word condition crosses earlier."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** forms a **peak**.","children":["It is near 0 at the endpoints and rises to about 0.35 for the items closest to the boundary.","The effect is **strongest for the items closest to the category boundary**. Those are the stimuli for which lexical information can help resolve ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","At the endpoints, strong acoustic evidence already settles the consonant, so word knowledge has little room to help."]},{"text":"So where the effect occurs matters. A **flat effect** points to a reporting bias. A **peaked effect** points to a shift in the boundary itself.","children":[]}],"speech2-21":[{"text":"**Ganong’s results** are strikingly consistent with the **second model**, which is the boundary-shift account.","children":["**At the short and long ends** of the VOT continuum, the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions give nearly the same answers.","**Near the boundary**, the two conditions separate the most.","The **lexical effect** rises from about 0.1 at the endpoints to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary."]},{"text":"A peak at the boundary rules out a simple reporting bias. Word knowledge matters most where the acoustic evidence is weakest.","children":["At the boundary, the word that fits tips the balance.","At the endpoints, the acoustic evidence decides the answer alone."]},{"text":"Ganong raised a number of possibilities for how this could happen.","children":["He focused on different kinds of **guessing strategies**.","A guessing strategy would let listeners use word knowledge only when they were unsure about the sound."]},{"text":"Later models emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","So the whole-word representation can affect how an ambiguous part of the word is interpreted during recognition."]}],"speech2-22":[{"text":"A **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model** tracks **phoneme-level and word-level activations** as the input unfolds.","children":["The input begins with a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**.","Then come /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/, which together make the word **plug**.","Activations are recorded at four moments, called frames."]},{"text":"**In the first frame**, only the ambiguous first sound has arrived.","children":["/b/ and /p/ are both weakly active at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2.","Word activation is still very low."]},{"text":"**In the second and third frames**, /l/ and the vowel arrive.","children":["Partial /b/ evidence supports words beginning with bl. Partial /p/ evidence supports words beginning with pl.","**Plug**, **blood** and **blush** gain activation together, because all three fit the beginning heard so far.","Phoneme activity for /p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"**In the fourth frame**, the final **/g/** arrives.","children":["The /g/ fits plug but does not fit the endings of blood or blush.","**Plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, while the alternatives fall away.","So **/p/ ends up more active than /b/** at the phoneme level."]}],"speech2-23":[{"text":"In the TRACE simulation, **activity accumulates for “plug”** as the input unfolds over time. The biggest gain comes when the **final /g/** arrives.","children":["The /g/ is highly inconsistent with “blood” and “blush”, which were also activated in Frame 3.","Blood and blush fit the early part of the input, so they were plausible candidates for a while.","Once /g/ arrives, they lose support because their endings do not match.","Plug wins because only plug matches the full sequence of sounds."]},{"text":"The model explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** in terms of **top-down processing**. Top-down processing means that higher-level knowledge influences lower-level analysis.","children":["**Activity from the lexical level produces feedback to the phoneme level**.","The feedback gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**.","Plug is the winning word, so /p/ receives extra support."]},{"text":"So the ambiguous first sound comes to be heard as /p/. Information that arrives at the end of a word can affect how an earlier sound is interpreted.","children":[]}],"speech2-24":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model is a version of the **Interactive Activation model** for **written word recognition**. That model comes from **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**, and many of the principles are the same.","children":["**Feature** units connect to **letter** units, and letter units connect to **word** units such as WEAR, WORK, WORD and WEAK.","Compatible units at different levels support each other.","Competing units at the same level inhibit each other.","Active words send support back down to their own letters."]},{"text":"Suppose a reader sees **W, O and R** clearly, while ink blots cover part of the last letter.","children":["The visible features of the last letter are consistent with both **R** and **K**.","W, O and R activate words such as WORK and WORD.","Only WORK fits the visible features of the last letter, so WORK becomes the strongest word.","WORK sends support back to K, so the ambiguous letter is **perceived as K**. This parallels plug supporting /p/ in speech."]},{"text":"A key issue in speech perception is that **the stimuli unfold over time**.","children":["A written word is all there at once. A spoken word arrives one sound after another.","TRACE handles this by repeating feature, phoneme and word units at every point in time.","So a candidate can fit the beginning, gain activation and then weaken when the ending conflicts."]}],"speech2-25":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** means that perception is shaped by **category boundaries**. Given the same physical difference, it is **harder to distinguish stimuli from the same category** than from different categories.","children":["Equal acoustic steps have different perceptual effects depending on whether they cross a boundary.","Cascading and feedback do not undermine this basic finding."]},{"text":"Two findings about the **lexical-phonetic interface** add to it.","children":["**Ambiguity about phoneme identity does have an impact on word recognition**. Sounds with the same label still differ in how strongly they activate a competing word.","**Word-level information seems to have an impact on phoneme categorization**. In the Ganong effect, word knowledge shifts the category boundary.","In TRACE, that shift happens through lexical feedback to the phoneme level."]},{"text":"So the word recognition system **cannot just sit there waiting** for the phoneme recognition system to finish its job. A lumber mill chain would work that way.","children":["**Spoken word recognition is more interactive** than this, more like the food web.","Partial information passes forward while uncertainty is still being resolved.","Active words send support back down at the same time.","So labels can be categorical while the processing behind them is graded."]}],"speech2-26":[],"speech2-27":[{"text":"**Spoken word recognition is also very fast**. **Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** used a visual world eye-tracking task to measure how quickly candidate words become active.","children":["Participants saw a grid of pictures and geometric shapes. They followed spoken instructions to move objects.","The display included a **beaker**, a **beetle**, a **speaker** and a **carriage**."]},{"text":"The pictures were chosen for their sound overlap with the target word beaker.","children":["**Beaker** is the **referent**, which is the target.","**Beetle** is a **cohort competitor**, because it shares the beginning of the word.","**Speaker** is a **rhyme competitor**, because it shares the ending of the word.","**Carriage** is **unrelated** and shares no sounds with beaker."]},{"text":"The measure is the probability of fixating each item over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the word begins. The spoken word ends a little before 400 milliseconds on average.","children":["**For beaker and beetle**, looks rise together from around 200 milliseconds, because both fit the beginning.","**For beetle**, looks peak near 0.2 around 500 milliseconds. Then they fall once the distinguishing sounds arrive.","**For beaker**, looks keep climbing, past 0.8 by about 800 milliseconds and close to 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","**For speaker**, looks rise later and stay smaller, with a peak near 0.1.","**For carriage**, looks stay near zero."]},{"text":"So recognition begins **while the word is still unfolding**. Words that match the input become active, and they drop out when they stop matching.","children":[]}],"speech2-28":[{"text":"The eye-tracking data can be compared with a **TRACE simulation** of the same task. The comparison is between real fixation probability and the fixation probability that TRACE predicts over 1,000 milliseconds.","children":["**For the referent**, the predicted and real looks both rise steadily from about 300 milliseconds. Both approach 1 by 1,000 milliseconds.","**For the cohort competitor**, both rise early, peak near 0.17 to 0.2 around 500 milliseconds, and then fall.","**For the rhyme competitor**, both rise later and stay small, with a peak near 0.1.","So the TRACE predictions closely match the eye movements."]},{"text":"The **granularity** of the simulation is fine. It predicts activity moment by moment, and not only the final choice.","children":["**Eye movements give an idea of the changes in “activity”** for different competitors over time.","That is why it is possible to **see cognitive processing before a final response is issued**."]},{"text":"This parallels the **graded effects of category goodness** in the categorical perception task.","children":["Sounds that are better or worse examples of a category produce different amounts of competitor activity.","This happens even when the final label is the same."]}],"sentence-01":[],"sentence-02":[{"text":"“The horse raced past the barn fell” is a grammatical English sentence that usually feels broken on first reading. The opening words push the reader toward the wrong structure.","children":["Most readers first take **raced** as the **main verb**. Then “the horse raced past the barn” seems to be a complete event in which the horse did the racing.","When **fell** arrives, that structure has no place for it. The sentence seems to fall apart."]},{"text":"The sentence describes **two events**. Someone raced the horse past the barn, and the horse then fell.","children":["The intended meaning is “The horse that was raced past the barn fell”.","In that reading, “raced past the barn” tells which horse is meant. **Fell** is the main verb, and it says what happened to that horse."]},{"text":"Putting back the missing words **disambiguates** the sentence.","children":["Adding “that was” makes the modifying relationship explicit. Raced can no longer be taken as the main verb.","Understanding a sentence depends on assigning relationships among its words, not only on knowing what each word means."]},{"text":"Researchers have used this sentence again and again to study how readers build structure as words arrive. That is why it could be called **the sentence that launched a thousand experiments**.","children":[]}],"sentence-03":[{"text":"“The horse raced past the barn fell” is almost as famous as “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”.","children":["“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously” is grammatical but meaningless.","“The horse raced past the barn fell” is grammatical and meaningful, but it is very hard to process."]},{"text":"The horse sentence is the classic case of the **“garden path” effect**. A **garden path sentence** leads the reader into one interpretation early. Later words reveal that this reading is wrong.","children":["The name comes from the expression “to lead someone down the garden path”, which means to mislead them.","Raced looks like the main verb. Fell then conflicts with the structure already built.","The reader has to go back and reinterpret “raced past the barn” as “that was raced past the barn”."]},{"text":"Garden paths happen because readers build structure **word by word**.","children":["Readers choose one analysis before the sentence is complete.","If they waited until the end of the sentence, they would never be led astray."]},{"text":"A dinosaur comic jokes about the horse sentence. One dinosaur insists nobody would ever say it. The other replies that farmers could say it if their horses kept falling over.","children":["The joke works only because the sentence really is grammatical, even though it sounds wrong."]},{"text":"So **grammaticality and processing difficulty are different things**. A sentence can have an acceptable structure and still be very hard to follow as it unfolds.","children":[]}],"sentence-04":[{"text":"**Syntactic explanations** of the garden path effect start with a **parser**. The parser is a mental process that builds a **syntactic tree** for each sentence we hear or read.","children":["A syntactic tree records how words group into phrases and how those phrases relate to one another."]},{"text":"**In the correct tree** for the horse sentence, the subject is a large noun phrase, or **NP**. It contains “the horse” plus a reduced clause meaning “that was raced past the barn”.","children":["“That” and “was” are silent in the actual sentence. The clause also has an empty subject position.","Inside the clause, raced takes the prepositional phrase, or **PP**, “past the barn”.","The main verb phrase, or **VP**, contains only **fell**."]},{"text":"**In the failed parse**, “the horse” is an ordinary NP. The main VP is “raced past the barn”, with raced as the main verb.","children":["**Fell** is then left with nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down."]},{"text":"The parser runs inside a cognitive system with **limited memory** and **time constraints**.","children":["Words arrive quickly, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence before building structure.","It can keep only a limited number of structures available at once."]},{"text":"That is why an account of parsing has to explain how the parser handles an ambiguous sentence under these limits.","children":[]}],"sentence-05":[{"text":"The **grammatical version** of the horse sentence is much more complex syntactically than the failed parse. It has **many more nodes**.","children":["A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","The correct tree needs extra nodes for the embedded clause and for the silent “that” and “was”.","The failed parse makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes."]},{"text":"**“Minimal attachment”** is a strategy in which the parser **initially prefers smaller trees**.","children":["The parser fits each new word into the existing structure with the fewest possible new nodes.","Fewer nodes means less structure to build and hold in memory at that moment, so the strategy saves space."]},{"text":"In the horse sentence, minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb**, because that analysis needs the fewest nodes.","children":["The reading where “raced past the barn” modifies horse needs an embedded relative clause. So the parser passes it over at first.","When fell arrives, it has no place in the small structure. The parser has to switch to the larger structure and rebuild the tree."]},{"text":"So the smaller tree lowers the immediate load, but it leads the reader down the garden path.","children":[]}],"sentence-06":[{"text":"**“Late closure”** is another parsing strategy in this framework. It is a bias to **keep the current clause “open” as long as possible**.","children":["Keeping a clause open means adding incoming words to it instead of closing it and starting a new structure.","So new words attach to the phrase currently being built."]},{"text":"**Frazier (1987)** contrasts two structures for “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”. The phrase “with the dynamite” wants to associate with the big **verb phrase** starting with “blew open”.","children":["**In the preferred structure**, the PP attaches to the **VP**. This is **verb-phrase attachment**, and it makes the dynamite the **instrument** the burglar used to blow open the safe.","**In the unpreferred structure**, the PP attaches to the **NP** “the safe”. This is **noun-phrase attachment**. It makes the phrase describe which safe was blown open, the one that had the dynamite."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, but they express different relationships.","children":["Late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the incoming phrase joins it.","Minimal attachment prefers the same attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than building an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite”."]},{"text":"So both strategies favor the VP attachment. For this sentence the preferred parse is also the sensible one, so readers reach the right meaning easily.","children":[]}],"sentence-07":[{"text":"Late closure can be seen at work in a sentence that needs the **“unpreferred” parse** to be sensible. The sentence is “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels”.","children":["Jewels are an implausible tool for blowing open a safe.","The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that contained the jewels**.","That meaning requires attaching “with the jewels” to the noun phrase “the safe”, which is the unpreferred NP attachment."]},{"text":"Careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements** find that people are **slower** to read sentences where the sensible reading requires the unpreferred parse.","children":["Reading time measures how long readers spend on each part of a sentence.","Eye movement recordings track where readers look and when they jump back to reread earlier words.","The extra time appears at the point where the parse has to change."]},{"text":"Readers **momentarily entertain the idea** that the jewels were used to blow open the safe. Then they have to **retrace their steps**.","children":["The parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase, just as it did for the dynamite.","The meaning of jewels conflicts with the instrument reading, so the parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase."]},{"text":"So the final meaning is easy to understand, but reaching it carries a measurable **processing cost**.","children":[]}],"sentence-08":[{"text":"This syntactic explanation rests on a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure** of the sentence. It parallels the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception.","children":["In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","In the same way, the parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without consulting meaning or context.","Meaning is worked out afterward from the finished structure."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**.","children":["It wants to make the simplest tree possible, which is **minimal attachment**.","It accomplishes this partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**.","Both preferences limit how much structure must be built and held in memory at once."]},{"text":"This is a very efficient way of dealing with ambiguity. The parser **ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem**.","children":["The parser commits to one likely structure and builds only that one. Most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra effort is needed.","When a later word does not fit, the structure has to be revised. That revision is the source of garden path difficulty."]},{"text":"So garden paths are the price of an efficient strategy.","children":[]}],"sentence-09":[{"text":"A **purely syntactic explanation** has a problem. Syntactically “difficult” sentences are **not always difficult to parse**.","children":["A **reduced relative clause** is a relative clause with “that was” or “that were” left out, as in “the horse raced past the barn fell”.","Published research articles contain many reduced relatives. In each example here, the verb that introduces the reduced relative is written in capitals."]},{"text":"The examples come from four articles.","children":["Trueswell, Tanenhaus and Garnsey (1994) wrote “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a MORPHOLOGICALLY AMBIGUOUS VERB … will be temporarily ambiguous”.","Britt, Perfetti, Garrod and Rayner (1992) wrote “Referential information PROVIDED by the discourse is of no help”.","Frazier and Rayner (1987) wrote “Recent research REPORTED by FERREIRA AND CLIFTON (1986) has demonstrated that syntactic processing is quite independent and that the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence is little influenced by the semantic information ALREADY ANALYZED”.","Altmann and Steedman (1988) wrote “The pattern of results OBTAINED with global reading times is reflected by differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase”."]},{"text":"Each capitalized verb begins a reduced relative with the same structure as the horse sentence. **But these are not impossibly difficult sentences**. Readers understand them easily, with no garden path.","children":["In “recent research reported by Ferreira and Clifton”, reported describes research that someone reported. Research is not the one doing the reporting."]},{"text":"**MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994)** argue that **semantic factors and statistical regularities interact with “parsing” from very early on**.","children":["Semantic factors concern meaning.","Statistical regularities are patterns in how often words appear in particular structures.","So particular words and their familiar uses can support a reduced relative reading from the start, which makes the sentence easy to read."]}],"sentence-10":[{"text":"The collection of reduced relative examples is **wonderfully passive-aggressive**. Many of the examples are drawn from papers that use the difficulty of parsing reduced relatives as evidence for an **autonomous parser**.","children":["An autonomous parser builds syntactic structure without help from meaning or context.","The authors of those papers argued that reduced relatives are hard to process.","Yet they wrote reduced relatives in their own articles, and readers understood them easily."]},{"text":"In each example, the capitalized verb modifies the noun before it.","children":["In “a noun phrase FOLLOWED by a morphologically ambiguous verb”, followed modifies noun phrase.","In “the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence”, assigned modifies analysis.","In “differences LOCATED on the critical disambiguating phrase”, located modifies differences.","Ferreira and Clifton (1986) wrote “A person PRESENTED with the evidence EXAMINED by the lawyer may interpret the evidence as a theme, not an agent, because examined requires an animate agent”. Presented modifies person, and examined modifies evidence."]},{"text":"These examples were **not the only reduced relatives** in those articles.","children":["Reduced relatives appeared at a rate of about **one per journal page** in this sample of articles.","So reduced relatives are common in ordinary scientific writing."]},{"text":"Readers attach each modifier to the correct noun easily in context. That is why a formally complex structure can be easy to understand in a supportive context.","children":[]}],"sentence-11":[{"text":"When we read “Recent research reported by…”, several factors besides syntax come into play.","children":["**Semantics** matters because research is an **inanimate** object, which means it is not a living thing. Research is unlikely to be the subject of a verb like report. So it is read as the thing that was reported.","**Lexical statistics** matter because “reported” occurs frequently in reduced relatives. Lexical statistics are learned patterns of how often particular words appear in particular structures.","Lexical statistics and **“world knowledge”** matter together. In scientific papers, “reported” occurs very frequently in reduced relatives, especially when following the word “research”. World knowledge here means knowing how people actually write in that setting."]},{"text":"These and many other factors **interact with the syntax of the sentence very early on**.","children":["They act from the first moments of reading.","They help decide which structure is chosen in the first place."]},{"text":"Two sentences with identical grammar differ in difficulty.","children":["**For the burglar**, the sentence is “The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today”. Burglars are likely to be arrested, so arrested is easily read as a reduced relative describing the burglar.","**For the policeman**, the sentence is “The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today”. Policemen are likely to do the arresting, so arrested is first taken as the main verb. Then “was charged” causes a garden path."]},{"text":"So the difference in difficulty comes from what we know about burglars and policemen, not from the grammar.","children":[]}],"sentence-12":[{"text":"**“World knowledge” can be very local**. It can come from the visual scene in front of the listener as well as from general knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"**Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested this with a visual world task.","children":["Participants saw real objects, which were **a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple sitting on a towel**.","They heard the instruction “Put the apple on the towel in the box”. They carried it out while their eye movements were recorded.","The unambiguous comparison instruction was “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”."]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” would temporarily seem like the end of the clause “put the apple on the towel”.","children":["In that reading, “on the towel” names the **destination**, the place the apple should go.","In the intended reading, “on the towel” tells **which apple** to move, and “in the box” is the destination."]},{"text":"People **do look at the empty towel** while planning their movements, as though they are considering it as a target.","children":["**For the ambiguous instruction**, a typical sequence of looks goes from the apple to the empty towel and then to the box. The look to the apple comes near 1,000 milliseconds, and the look to the box comes near 2,000 milliseconds.","**For the unambiguous instruction**, the looks go from the apple straight to the box."]},{"text":"So the listener recognizes every word correctly but briefly takes the location phrase as the destination. That briefly changes the planned action.","children":[]}],"sentence-13":[{"text":"A **two-referent context** is a display that contains **two apples**. One apple sits on a towel, and the other sits elsewhere. The empty towel and the box are still present.","children":["Now “the apple” alone does not say which apple to move.","The phrase “on the towel” becomes useful, because it picks out one apple from the two."]},{"text":"In this context, participants usually look first at the apple on the towel and then at the box.","children":["Looks to the empty towel are much less common.","The **sequence and timing of eye movements**, measured relative to the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the ambiguous and unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"The ambiguous instruction is processed just like the explicit instruction with “that’s on the towel”.","children":["With two apples present, the listener needs to know which apple, so a phrase that picks one out is expected.","Listeners treat “on the towel” as a description of the apple from the start. The destination reading never takes hold."]},{"text":"So the same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context is different.","children":[]}],"sentence-14":[{"text":"With **two apples** in the display, it becomes **felicitous** to give more information about which apple the speaker means. Felicitous means appropriate and natural in the situation.","children":["**With one apple**, there is no need to say which apple. So “on the towel” seems like extra information and is easily taken as a destination.","**With two apples**, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is to identify the right apple."]},{"text":"So people **do not look at the empty towel as much**. The measure is the proportion of trials with eye movements to the incorrect destination, the empty towel.","children":["**In the one-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on about **55%** of trials. Unambiguous instructions led to almost none.","**In the two-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on only about **17%** of trials. That is close to the roughly 12% for unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"The words of the instruction were identical in both contexts. So the visual context alone changed how often listeners were misled.","children":["This is an effect of **referential context**, which is the set of objects a phrase could refer to.","Referential context influences which grammatical relationship listeners assign while the sentence is still being heard."]}],"sentence-15":[{"text":"The Spivey results are **a big problem for purely syntactic accounts**. Different parses of the same sentence are favored under different **semantic and pragmatic conditions**, and the effect happens **very early**.","children":["**Semantic** conditions concern meaning.","**Pragmatic** conditions concern how language is used in a situation, such as whether a speaker needs to specify which object is meant."]},{"text":"The same ambiguous sentence misleads listeners often with one apple and rarely with two apples.","children":["With one apple, it led to looks at the empty towel on about 55% of trials. With two apples, it led to them on only about 17%.","The words are identical in both conditions. Only the visual situation changes, and it changes the parse the listener adopts first."]},{"text":"The difference appears in **eye movements during the sentence**, before the action is carried out. So the eye movements capture the parse while it is being built.","children":["If context only corrected the parse afterward, listeners in both contexts should first look at the empty towel.","Those looks drop with two apples, so context must have shaped the **first parse**."]},{"text":"That is why a parser that always starts with the same structure cannot predict these results.","children":[]}],"sentence-16":[{"text":"Researchers have more recently used **mouse movements** to study these questions. **Farmer and colleagues (2007)** tracked the path of the computer mouse while participants carried out spoken instructions. The display was a version of the apple and towel display.","children":["It is not just eye movements but **the actual execution of arm movements** that syntactic ambiguity can influence. The influence depends on how the ambiguity interacts with the **visually defined context**.","Mouse tracking records the whole path of the hand. So a competing interpretation can pull the movement toward another location before the hand reaches its target."]},{"text":"**In the one-referent context**, the hand moved from the apple toward the box. On ambiguous instructions, its path **curved toward the empty towel**, compared with the path on unambiguous instructions.","children":["The two paths separated most in the middle of the movement.","The hand still ended at the correct place."]},{"text":"**In the two-referent context**, the ambiguous and unambiguous paths were nearly identical.","children":["The second apple makes the location phrase useful for picking out an apple. So the phrase is no longer taken as a destination."]},{"text":"The researchers also measured the **Euclidean distance** between the ambiguous and unambiguous paths at each moment. Euclidean distance is the straight-line distance between two points.","children":["With one apple, the distance rose to a peak of about 200 pixels around 40% of the way through the movement.","With two apples, it peaked lower, at about 140 pixels, and earlier, around 30%."]},{"text":"So comprehension and action planning interact while both are underway. A competing interpretation bends the path of the hand even when the movement ends correctly.","children":[]}],"sentence-17":[],"sentence-18":[{"text":"**The** is a **determiner**, which is a word that introduces a **noun phrase**. When the parser meets “the”, it expects a noun to follow. An adjective or other modifier may come first.","children":["“The” does not name anything by itself.","It limits which kinds of words can come next, so the parser can anticipate the shape of the phrase."]},{"text":"The expectation is about a **grammatical category**, not about one particular word.","children":["Many different nouns could follow, such as pizza, dog or book.","An adjective could also come first, as in “the hot pizza”.","The parser predicts the form of the phrase while its meaning is still open."]},{"text":"So each word sets expectations for the next word. Words that fit the expectation are processed smoothly, and words that break it cause difficulty.","children":[]}],"sentence-19":[{"text":"**Pizza** is a **noun**, and it completes the noun phrase begun by “the”. It is the **head of the noun phrase**, the central word that the rest of the phrase is organized around.","children":["“The” combines with “pizza” to form “the pizza”. That phrase can now serve as the subject of the sentence.","With a complete noun phrase in place, the parser expects a verb or verb phrase. The verb would tell what the pizza did or what happened to it."]},{"text":"Recognizing the word makes two kinds of information available.","children":["Its **category**, noun, tells the parser how it can combine with other words.","Its **meaning**, a kind of food, sets up expectations about plausible events, such as being eaten or baked."]},{"text":"So both grammatical expectations and meaning-based expectations guide how the next words are processed.","children":[]}],"sentence-20":[{"text":"**Was** is a past-tense form of the verb **be**. It can act as an **auxiliary**, which is a helping verb that combines with another verb to mark grammatical information such as tense or voice.","children":["In “the pizza was eaten”, was helps form a **passive** with the participle eaten.","In “the pizza was in the oven”, was links the subject to a phrase giving a location."]},{"text":"When “was” arrives, the sentence could continue in several ways. It could describe where the pizza was, what it was like or what was done to it.","children":["A location phrase would follow with a preposition.","A passive would follow with a participle such as eaten.","An adjective could also follow."]},{"text":"The grammatical role of was depends partly on what comes next. That is why the parser has to keep the developing structure available while more words arrive.","children":[]}],"sentence-21":[{"text":"**In** is a **preposition**, which is a word that expresses a relationship such as location or containment. It begins a **prepositional phrase**.","children":["“In” does not name the location by itself.","It sets up the expectation of a noun phrase that supplies the location, as in “in the oven” or “in the box”."]},{"text":"After “the pizza was”, the word “in” narrows the possible structures. The sentence is now heading toward a statement about where the pizza was.","children":["The parser expects a noun phrase to complete the prepositional phrase.","The expectation is grammatical, so the next word must be able to start a noun phrase. Words like “the”, “an” or a noun would fit."]},{"text":"So understanding the phrase requires combining the preposition with the noun phrase that follows it.","children":[]}],"sentence-22":[{"text":"**The** begins a new **noun phrase** inside the prepositional phrase “in the …”. It starts the phrase that will name the location.","children":["“The” does not supply the location by itself.","A noun is still needed, possibly with modifiers before it, as in “in the hot oven”."]},{"text":"The parser now strongly expects a **noun**. The expectation concerns a **word category**, not just a sensible topic.","children":["Whatever comes next must work grammatically as part of a noun phrase.","Knowing the sentence is about pizza makes words like oven or box likely. The grammatical requirement holds regardless of meaning."]},{"text":"So the grammatical frame is now very specific. Any word that cannot be part of a noun phrase will clash with the expectation.","children":[]}],"sentence-23":[{"text":"**Eaten** is the **past participle** of the verb **eat**. It is a verb form, not a noun, so it cannot complete the noun phrase that “in the” has started.","children":["“In the eaten” puts a verb form exactly where a noun is required.","This is a **word-category violation**. A word of the wrong grammatical category appears in a position that demands a different category."]},{"text":"A category error differs from a strange but grammatical meaning.","children":["**For “The pizza was in the ocean”**, the meaning is odd but the grammar is fine, because ocean is a noun.","**For “The pizza was in the eaten”**, no unusual situation can fix the sentence. The problem lies in the grammatical type of the word, so the structure itself breaks."]},{"text":"That is why such sentences are useful for studying the brain. Brain responses to category errors and meaning errors can be compared to see whether grammar and meaning are processed differently.","children":[]}],"sentence-24":[{"text":"The **early left anterior negativity**, or **ELAN**, is an **ERP** response elicited by **word class violations**.","children":["An **ERP**, or **event-related potential**, is electrical brain activity recorded at the scalp. It is averaged over many trials and timed to an event such as the start of a word.","A **word class** is a grammatical category such as noun, verb or preposition."]},{"text":"Classic ELAN sentences contain word-category violations.","children":["In “The pizza was in the eaten”, a participle appears where a noun is expected.","In “The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem”, the preposition **of** appears where a noun is expected after “Max’s”."]},{"text":"The ELAN has three main features.","children":["It is **early**, appearing about **100 to 200 milliseconds** after the critical word.","It is **left and anterior**, which means it is strongest over the left front of the scalp. That location raises the question of whether it is specific to language.","It occurs **specifically for word category violations**, such as a noun when a verb is expected, or a preposition in place of a noun."]},{"text":"Incorrect sentences produce an early negative response over left frontal sites soon after the critical word. Correct sentences do not.","children":["So the ELAN was taken as evidence for a very fast syntactic analysis that checks word categories before meaning is processed."]}],"sentence-25":[{"text":"With visually presented words, **the ELAN came from visual cortex**. Visual cortex is the region at the back of the brain that processes what we see. It sits in the **occipital** lobe.","children":[]},{"text":"**Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)** reported this finding. Their paper is titled “Early occipital sensitivity to syntactic category is based on form typicality”.","children":["They compared “The beautiful princess …” with “The beautifully princess …”. Princess is a **bimorphemic noun**, which is a noun that ends in an identifiable noun suffix.","Beautiful is an adjective, which correctly predicts a noun. Beautifully is an adverb, which predicts a verb or an adjective, so princess is unexpected.","The visual response was **larger** for “The beautifully princess …” at about 120 to 130 milliseconds after the noun appeared."]},{"text":"The finding suggests that a prediction can **feed back down to the earliest parts of the visual system**.","children":["When we expect a verb, for example, the prediction covers visual patterns that are consistent with the way verbs are written. A predicted category implies likely visual forms, such as typical word endings.","The very early response to the error might reflect a **mismatch** between that prediction and what finally appears on the screen."]},{"text":"This raises a question. Does this work only for items that are **morphologically marked** as nouns or verbs, or also for **open-class** and **closed-class** words?","children":["A word is morphologically marked when a part of it, such as a suffix, signals its category.","Open-class words are content words such as nouns and verbs, which keep gaining new members. Closed-class words are function words such as prepositions and determiners, which form a small fixed set.","The early effect appears for nouns with a noun-marking morpheme and for nouns with a typical noun form. It does not appear for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues."]},{"text":"So the early visual response depends on how much a word’s written form looks like its category.","children":[]}],"sentence-26":[{"text":"Many English words can function as either a **noun** or a **verb**. The rest of the sentence decides which.","children":["**For perches as a noun**, the sentence is “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches seem easy to install”. Perches means the bars that birds sit on, and seem is the verb.","**For perches as a verb**, the sentence is “Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches comfortably in the cage”. Perches describes what the bird does.","**For needs as a noun**, the sentence is “The teacher told the principal that the student needs were not being met”. Needs means the student’s requirements.","**For needs as a verb**, the sentence is “The teacher told the principal that the student needs to be more focused”."]},{"text":"Recognizing the word form alone does not settle its grammatical role. The **continuation** after the ambiguous word resolves it.","children":[]},{"text":"Words also differ in **phonological form typicality**. This is how much a word’s sound pattern resembles the sound patterns of typical nouns or typical verbs.","children":["A corpus analysis of **3,158 words** measured each word’s mean distance in phonological feature space from all nouns and from all verbs. A small distance means the word sounds similar to that category.","Nouns tend to sound closer to other nouns, and verbs tend to sound closer to other verbs.","Some words go against the trend. There are **noun-like nouns** and **verb-like verbs**, but also **verb-like nouns** and **noun-like verbs**."]},{"text":"So a word can sound like one category while actually belonging to the other.","children":[]}],"sentence-27":[{"text":"In **form typicality**, **“perches” is noun-like** and **“needs” is verb-like**.","children":["These are learned statistical tendencies in how words sound.","Both words can still appear in either role."]},{"text":"**For perches**, the noun-like form matches one sentence and clashes with the other.","children":["In “the bird perches seem easy to install”, perches is a noun, which matches its form.","In “the bird perches comfortably in the cage”, perches must be a verb, which goes against its form."]},{"text":"**For needs**, the pattern is reversed.","children":["In “the student needs to be more focused”, needs is a verb, which matches its form.","In “the student needs were not being met”, needs must be a noun, which goes against its form."]},{"text":"Each word appears once as a noun and once as a verb. So the same word form can be compared in a matching use and a mismatching use.","children":["The comparison separates what a word’s form suggests from what the grammar of the sentence requires."]},{"text":"So the four sentences set up a test of form typicality. If form typicality guides parsing, the mismatching uses should be harder to process, even though all four sentences are grammatical.","children":[]}],"sentence-28":[{"text":"Reading is **slower when a word’s required category conflicts with its form typicality**.","children":["The measure is reading time at **disambiguation** minus reading time at the **point of ambiguity**. The times are adjusted for word length.","The point of ambiguity is the ambiguous word itself, such as perches or needs.","Disambiguation is the point where the continuation makes the intended category clear, such as “seem” or “comfortably”.","A larger increase from the point of ambiguity to disambiguation signals more difficulty."]},{"text":"**For noun-like homonyms** such as perches, noun-resolved sentences added only about **50 milliseconds**. Verb-resolved sentences added much more, about **120 milliseconds**.","children":["So a noun-like form is harder to resolve as a verb."]},{"text":"**For verb-like homonyms** such as needs, noun-resolved sentences added about **110 milliseconds**. Verb-resolved sentences added almost nothing, under 10 milliseconds.","children":["So a verb-like form is harder to resolve as a noun."]},{"text":"The ambiguous word stays the same in each pair, and only the continuation changes.","children":["The extra cost appears when the continuation demands the category that does not match the form."]},{"text":"So parsing uses learned statistics about how word forms sound, along with the structure the sentence finally requires.","children":[]}],"sentence-29":[{"text":"The **ELAN effect** for nouns where a verb is expected is limited to certain cases. It appears only when **syntactic** or **statistical form-based** information is **physically available** in the word.","children":["Syntactic information means **morphemes** that indicate noun-ness. A morpheme is the smallest meaningful part of a word, such as a suffix that marks a noun.","Statistical form-based information means **“nouniness”**. This is how much a word’s form resembles typical nouns, even without a noun suffix."]},{"text":"The early response measured is the **M100**, a brain response that peaks about 100 milliseconds after a word appears. Three kinds of nouns were compared.","children":["**For bimorphemic nouns**, the pair was “The beautiful princess …” and “The beautifully princess …”. Princess has a noun-marking suffix, and the unexpected version produced a significantly larger response.","**For typical nouns**, the pair was “The tasteless soda …” and “The tastelessly soda …”. Soda has a statistically noun-like form, and the unexpected version again produced a significantly larger response.","**For neutral nouns**, the pair was “The cute infant …” and “The cutely infant …”. Infant has neither a suffix nor a typical noun form, and the two versions did not differ."]},{"text":"So **no effect** was observed for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues. An abstract category mismatch alone is not enough to produce this earliest visual response.","children":["An expected category generates predictions about what the next word will look like.","The early response appears only when the arriving word carries physical evidence that conflicts with the prediction."]}],"sentence-30":[{"text":"Sentence processing can be summed up in **four sentences**, and each one carries one idea.","children":[]},{"text":"**For “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”**, the idea is that **syntax is independent of semantics** to some extent.","children":["In the limit, a sentence can be grammatically correct but meaningless.","Words can fill a grammatical structure without describing a sensible event."]},{"text":"**For “The horse raced past the barn fell”**, the idea is that syntactic structure is often highly **ambiguous**, or actually **vague**. We do not notice except where things fail.","children":["Reading times for “preferred” and “unpreferred” structures suggest that syntactic processes **take shortcuts to save memory**. They do not activate every possible parse of a sentence.","When the shortcut picks the wrong structure, the reader is led down the garden path."]},{"text":"**For “Put the apple on the towel in the box”**, the idea is that **context, pragmatics and other factors** interact with syntactic processing from very early on.","children":["With two apples in view, listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple, not as a destination.","So the visual situation shapes the parse while the sentence is still unfolding."]},{"text":"**For “The bird perches were comfortable”**, the idea is that the very earliest syntactic effects observable in the brain are driven in part by **form typicality**. They are not necessarily driven by **morphosyntactic** structures, which are grammatical structures signaled by word parts and word order.","children":["These effects seem to be related to **predictions about the physical forms of words**.","Perches has a noun-like form, so its use as a noun here matches the expectation its form creates."]}],"sentence-31":[],"aphasias-01":[],"aphasias-02":[{"text":"**Aphasia** is a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage. In the **1870s**, German neurologists including **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** began making **more systematic observations of aphasia**.","children":["They ascribed specific losses of function to **“disconnections” between “centers.”**","A **center** is a brain region that stores one kind of language representation.","A **disconnection** is damage to the pathway linking two centers, so information can no longer travel between them.","This was reasoning about aphasia before Chomsky."]},{"text":"**Lichtheim’s “House” model** arranges the centers and pathways in the shape of a house.","children":["**A** is the center for **sound images**, the stored sound patterns of words.","**M** is the center for **movement images**, the stored motor plans for saying words.","**B** is the center for **concepts**.","The **acoustic pathway a** brings heard speech into A. The **motor pathway m** carries plans from M out to the speech muscles.","Seven numbered sites mark different forms of aphasia. Each site is damage at a particular center or pathway."]},{"text":"Each damage site predicts its own pattern of loss.","children":["For a break between **A and B**, the word’s sound arrives but cannot reach its concept. So the person might lose the ability to **understand spoken words**.","For a break between **A and M**, a heard word cannot pass directly to the plan for saying it. So the person might lose **verbatim repetition**."]},{"text":"So this kind of **“box and arrow” model** is foundational in **aphasiology**, the study of aphasia, and in modeling typical language function.","children":["The **interactive activation model** of word recognition has the same basic form, with levels of representation connected by pathways.","This history comes from Eling and Whitaker (2009)."]}],"aphasias-03":[{"text":"The **“Geschwind-Wernicke model”** links language functions to particular brain regions and the pathways between them. **Norman Geschwind** revived the box-and-arrow approach of Wernicke and Lichtheim in the **1970s**.","children":["**Broca’s area** sits toward the front of the left hemisphere, in the frontal lobe.","**Wernicke’s area** sits farther back, in the temporal lobe.","The **arcuate fasciculus** is a bundle of nerve fibers that connects Wernicke’s area with Broca’s area."]},{"text":"Two major grooves serve as landmarks.","children":["The **central sulcus**, also called the **fissure of Rolando**, separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe behind it.","The **lateral sulcus**, also called the **fissure of Sylvius**, separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes above it."]},{"text":"In the century between Wernicke and Geschwind, the field of **linguistics evolved dramatically**.","children":["Linguists developed a theoretical model in which **syntax was independent** of other aspects of language. Syntax had **its own computational principles**.","That opened the door to theorizing about **dissociations** and **double dissociations** of language functions in more sophisticated ways."]},{"text":"In a **dissociation**, one ability is impaired while another is spared.","children":["In a **double dissociation**, one patient loses the first ability and keeps the second. Another patient has the reverse pattern.","A double dissociation is strong evidence that the two abilities depend on **separate systems**.","So once syntax, word knowledge and meaning are treated as separate parts, a patient can be impaired in one part while the others work."]}],"aphasias-04":[{"text":"**Fromkin (1997)** describes the classic profiles of the two main aphasias.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Broca’s aphasia**, speech has **word-finding pauses**, **loss of grammatical morphemes**, and quite often **disturbed word order**.","children":["A **grammatical morpheme** is a small word or word part that marks a grammatical relationship, such as “the,” “is” or past-tense “-ed.”","Comprehension of everyday conversation **gives the impression of being generally good**. But **controlled testing reveals considerable impairments**."]},{"text":"**Agrammatism** is difficulty producing or understanding grammatical structure. It is almost a synonym for Broca’s aphasia.","children":["Still, **some patients with lesions in Broca’s area are not agrammatic**.","And **some agrammatic patients would not be classified neurologically as Broca’s aphasics**.","So Broca’s aphasia names a pattern of behavior. That pattern does not always follow damage to Broca’s area."]},{"text":"For **Wernicke’s aphasia**, speech is **fluent** with **good intonation and pronunciation**.","children":["It has **many word substitutions**. Some are related in meaning to the intended word and some are not.","It has **neologisms**, which are invented words, and **phonological errors**, which are errors in the sounds of words.","These patients also have **comprehension difficulties**.","Fluency describes how smoothly speech flows. It is separate from whether the speech carries a coherent message."]},{"text":"So Broca’s patients keep meaningful words but lose the small grammatical pieces that connect them. Wernicke’s patients keep the grammatical frame but fill it with wrong or invented words.","children":[]}],"aphasias-05":[{"text":"An attractive **dichotomy**, a two-way division, contrasts the two aphasias.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Wernicke’s aphasia**, speech is **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**. **Syntax is roughly intact**.","children":["“Jabberwocky” is Lewis Carroll’s poem of grammatical sentences filled with nonsense words.","Asked about his health, one patient said “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves.” Kriendler and colleagues (1971) reported this answer.","Asked about his poor vision, another patient said “My wires don’t hire right.” The sentence frame is normal, but the words do not express the problem with his eyes.","Asked if he was a doctor, an aphasic physician said “Me? Yes, sir, I’m a male demaploze on my own. I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go.” **Demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient called a **fork** “a need for a schedule” and a **spoon** “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"For **Broca’s aphasia**, speech is **“telegraphic.”** There is **difficulty with word order and grammatical particles**, with **relative sparing of important content words**.","children":["Asked what brought him to the hospital, one patient said “Yes … ah … Monday … ah … Dad and P.H. (the patient’s name) and Dad … hospital. Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital. And, er Wednesday … nine o’clock and eh Thursday, ten o’clock … doctors. Two doctors … and ah … teeth. Yeah … fine.” Badecker and Caramazza (1985) reported this answer.","Content words such as Dad, hospital, doctors and teeth carry pieces of the story.","The grammatical words and endings that would connect them are largely missing."]},{"text":"So the two patterns look like mirror images. Wernicke’s patients have **fluent grammar with wrong words**. Broca’s patients have **meaningful words with little grammar**.","children":[]}],"aphasias-06":[{"text":"The dichotomy can be stated by what is damaged. For **Wernicke’s aphasia**, there are **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon**. For **Broca’s aphasia**, there are **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words.","**Activation** makes a candidate word available.","**Inhibition** suppresses competing words that should not be chosen. So weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"This description is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. Paul Broca described **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan.”**","children":["Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, “tan.”","He **seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses**.","He **seemed to understand what was being said**."]},{"text":"Broca’s description raises three questions.","children":["It is critical that Leborgne’s other senses were intact. Intact senses mean the loss was specific to speech, which makes it a **dissociation**.","A “capacity for articulate speech” seems a more plausible function for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness,” a phrenology term for love of one’s children.","Examining Tan today would mean measuring his deficits at the **levels of description used in modern linguistics**, such as phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic."]},{"text":"So there is a confusion. **Broca’s actual patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca’s aphasics** today.","children":["They were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**, apparently because of **motor control problems**.","They would be classified as having **apraxia of speech**, a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak.","Apraxia of speech can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"aphasias-07":[{"text":"The dichotomy treats Wernicke’s aphasia as a lexicon problem and Broca’s aphasia as a grammar problem. It is interesting partly because it **aligns well with a sharp distinction between the grammar and the lexicon** in late 20th-century formal linguistics.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences.","The **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky’s formulation of the computational problem of language learning**.","children":["We recognize a new item as a sentence **not because it matches some familiar item** in any simple way.","We recognize it because **it is generated by the grammar** that each person has internalized.","So a sentence never heard before can be understood, because the listener can work out how the grammar builds it."]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also gave **compelling demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics**.","children":["Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has ever occurred in an English speaker’s past experience.","For “colorless green ideas sleep furiously,” the meaning makes no sense, but the word order follows English grammar. So it is **grammatical**.","For “furiously sleep ideas green colorless,” the same words come in an order the grammar does not allow. So it is **not grammatical**.","Both sentences are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless. So the difference between them comes from **grammar as a separate system**."]},{"text":"That is why the aphasia dichotomy lines up with this split. Wernicke’s aphasia would be a disorder of the lexicon, and Broca’s aphasia would be a disorder of the grammar.","children":[]}],"aphasias-08":[{"text":"The dichotomy also fits the **speech production model**, especially the difference between the **functional level** and the **positional level**.","children":[]},{"text":"The model has several levels of processing.","children":["At the **message level**, which is conceptual, the speaker is choosing a story angle and arousing concepts of referents and event structure. A **referent** is the person, object or event being talked about.","At the **functional level**, the first linguistic level, the speaker is arousing **word meanings and semantic roles**. A **semantic role** is the part a participant plays in an event, such as the doer or the receiver.","At the **positional level**, the second linguistic level, the speaker is getting **word forms in grammatically appropriate order**.","The **phonological encoding level** supplies the sounds of the words.","The **speech gesture level** handles **articulation**, the physical movements of speaking."]},{"text":"At the functional level, **lemmas have an arbitrary relationship to their referents**.","children":["A **lemma** is a word’s entry in the lexicon, linking its meaning to its grammatical properties.","Arbitrary means nothing about the concept determines the word. There is no reason a dog must be called “dog.”","This is the lexical side of language."]},{"text":"At the positional level, **syntactic trees and affixes are the product of abstract syntactic processes**.","children":["An **affix** is a word part added to a stem, such as “-ed” or “-ly.”","This is the grammatical side of language, built by rules."]},{"text":"One objection is that **HOUSEBOAT** and **DOGHOUSE** have meanings that are clearly not arbitrary, because they are built from parts.","children":["The reply is **speech error evidence** that **derivational morphology** is done at the positional level.","**Derivational morphology** builds new words from existing parts, such as adding “-ly” to make an adverb.","In the error “easy enoughly,” the speaker meant “easily enough,” but “-ly” attached to the wrong word.","The suffix moved as a separate piece. So a grammatical operation at the positional level added it."]}],"aphasias-09":[{"text":"Popular theories of **sentence processing** say **the syntactic structure of a sentence is computed independently of meaning** as we listen or read.","children":["Under these theories, an autonomous parser builds the structure first. Meaning is worked out afterward."]},{"text":"**Isolating these “parsing” mechanisms requires carefully constructing stimulus materials**.","children":["One such sentence is “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite.”","For the **preferred** structure, “with the dynamite” attaches to the **verb phrase**. So the dynamite is the instrument.","For the **unpreferred** structure, it attaches to the **noun phrase** “the safe.” So the phrase tells which safe.","The words are the same in both structures and only the attachment changes. So such sentences can test parsing without changing word meanings."]},{"text":"One possibility is that **Broca’s patients generally understand what is being said even though they have a core grammatical deficit**.","children":["In ordinary conversation, word meanings, familiar events and context all point toward the same message.","A listener can use these cues to understand without fully computing the grammar.","So a patient can succeed without parsing if meaning and plausibility point toward the right answer."]},{"text":"That is why testing a parsing deficit requires sentences whose grammatical relationships cannot be guessed from the most likely event.","children":["**Reversible sentences**, where either person could plausibly do the action, force the listener to use grammar."]}],"aphasias-10":[{"text":"**None of this theory was available to Broca in the 1860s**. For him, it was interesting enough to **dissociate “articulate speech” from comprehension**.","children":["Leborgne could produce only one syllable, yet he seemed to understand what was said to him.","So speaking and understanding could come apart. That was the key finding at the time."]},{"text":"The modern question is different. The goal is to establish that there are patients whose **difficulty is more generally with syntax**.","children":["A **syntactic impairment** concerns the organization of grammatical relationships.","Such an impairment should affect both producing and understanding sentences."]},{"text":"The way to do it is to **test comprehension**.","children":["A comprehension test lets the patient respond without speaking. So motor problems are removed from the test.","If the patient still struggles to understand grammatical relationships, the deficit **goes beyond articulation**."]},{"text":"So Broca asked whether producing speech and understanding speech could be separated. Modern researchers ask whether the difficulty lies in grammar itself, which requires testing comprehension of specific structures.","children":[]}],"aphasias-11":[{"text":"It was interesting to test whether **Broca’s patients have comprehension deficits** because their speech alone cannot tell what is damaged.","children":["**Broca’s aphasia is almost always accompanied by some level of motor dysfunction**.","So agrammatic speech might **merely reflect an attempt to communicate despite motor limitations**.","If speaking takes great effort, a patient might drop the small grammatical words and keep the most informative content words."]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson** made a related point. **We are observing what the individual can do despite the impairment**.","children":["A brain injury that leads to agrammatic speech does not literally mean that **the grammar itself is injured**.","Caramazza and Zurif (1976) cite a version of this idea from Lenneberg (1973). If anterior damage spares tacit knowledge of language, the telegraphic output could come from a neuromuscular problem.","On that account, the strain of speaking is so great that the patient **speaks asyntactically in order to economize effort**."]},{"text":"**Chomsky’s distinction between competence and performance** also applies.","children":["**Competence** is a person’s underlying knowledge of language.","**Performance** is the use of that knowledge in speaking or understanding. Memory limits, distraction or motor problems can disrupt it.","When motor deficits obviously interfere with performance, **comprehension may provide a clearer window into competence**."]},{"text":"That is why **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** tested comprehension in Broca’s patients.","children":["For the motor explanation, comprehension of grammatical structure should be normal.","For the grammar explanation, comprehension should also suffer.","Both explanations predict telegraphic speech, so only comprehension tests can tell them apart."]}],"aphasias-12":[{"text":"**Psycholinguistic tests** are needed to observe grammatical difficulties in comprehension. This is because **in conversation, there are lots of redundant cues to what a speaker intends**.","children":["**Redundant** cues are several different sources of information that all point to the same meaning.","“Man bites dog” is the newspaper example of an unexpected event."]},{"text":"“The dog bit the man” offers **many ways to get the right meaning**.","children":["A listener can form the **most likely combination of DOG, BITE and MAN** into an event representation, **with or without syntax**. Dogs bite people much more often than people bite dogs.","The sentence follows **canonical word order**, which is agent, then verb, then undergoer, or subject, then verb, then object.","The **agent** is the doer of the action. The **undergoer** is the one the action happens to. In English, the first noun is usually the agent.","A lot of comprehension also happens in a **discourse context**, the surrounding conversation, which clarifies a great deal."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients’ comprehension might depend on these **compensatory heuristics**.","children":["A **heuristic** is a mental shortcut. A compensatory one makes up for a weakness elsewhere.","A patient might assume the most plausible event, or assume the first noun is the doer.","Either shortcut gives the right answer for “The dog bit the man” without any grammatical analysis."]},{"text":"That is why the test sentences must defeat the shortcuts, as in “The dog that the man is biting is angry.”","children":["Here the **man** is doing the biting, even though the dog comes first and dogs are more likely biters.","So only working out the grammatical relationships gives the right answer."]}],"aphasias-13":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif** devised a list of exactly this kind of sentence, along with a set of **control sentences**. The materials have four groups of eight sentences each.","children":[]},{"text":"For **control sentences**, group A, the sentence is simple and in canonical order.","children":["Three of them are “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle.”"]},{"text":"For **center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints**, group B, meaning makes the roles obvious.","children":["A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause placed in the middle of the main clause.","Three of them are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round.”","Apples cannot eat boys, so meaning alone tells who did what."]},{"text":"For **reversible center-embedded sentences**, group C, either participant could plausibly do the action.","children":["Three of them are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy.”","Meaning gives no help, so only grammar reveals the roles."]},{"text":"For **improbable center-embedded sentences**, group D, the event goes against expectations.","children":["Three of them are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny.”","Common sense points to the wrong answer. Worms do not usually eat birds, but here the worm is the eater."]},{"text":"In every embedded sentence, the first noun is acted on and the doer is inside the relative clause. So the structure stays the same across groups B, C and D while the help from meaning changes.","children":["A listener who relies on meaning should succeed on groups A and B and fail on groups C and D.","A listener who parses normally should succeed on all four."]}],"aphasias-14":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task**. Participants heard a sentence and chose which picture matched its meaning.","children":["Accuracy reveals whether the listener understood who did what to whom, without requiring a spoken answer.","Healthy listeners and patients with Broca’s, conduction and Wernicke’s aphasia heard all four sentence types.","The types were control, semantically constrained, reversible and improbable sentences."]},{"text":"For **healthy controls**, accuracy was **more or less equally good on all stimulus types**, near the maximum score.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Wernicke’s patients**, accuracy was **poor overall**.","children":["Their scores were low even on the simple control sentences.","This is probably a result of their **comprehension deficits**, which affect understanding of words as well as grammar."]},{"text":"For **Broca’s patients**, there was a particular difficulty keeping **“who did what to whom”** straight when **syntax conflicted with common sense** or with the probable semantic roles for nouns.","children":["Their scores were **high** on control and semantically constrained sentences, where meaning supplies the answer.","Their scores **dropped** on reversible and improbable sentences, where only grammar can reveal the roles."]},{"text":"So this **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca’s aphasia impacts syntax specifically**.","children":["The deficit appeared in understanding as well as speaking, which places it in the grammar itself.","The paper’s title is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes.”","Broca’s patients lose the **algorithmic** process of grammatical analysis. They keep the **heuristic** shortcuts based on meaning."]}],"aphasias-15":[{"text":"A more **specific theory of the “parts of language”** opens up **new ways of exploring structure-function relationships**.","children":["A **structure-function relationship** is a link between a brain structure and the function it supports.","Before such theories, aphasias could only be described in broad terms."]},{"text":"Researchers no longer have to just divide aphasics into those with good and poor comprehension, or those who are more or less fluent. They can **isolate problems with particular aspects of language**.","children":["One patient might know word meanings well but assign the wrong roles in complex sentences.","Another patient might produce a fluent grammatical frame filled with words that miss the intended message.","A single fluency rating would hide the difference between these two patients."]},{"text":"The key division is **the grammar versus the lexicon**.","children":["**Grammar** concerns the rules that relate words to one another in a sentence.","The **lexicon** concerns stored words and the ability to retrieve the right ones."]},{"text":"Each kind of deficit makes its own prediction.","children":["For a **grammar deficit**, there is trouble with reversible sentences but not with word meanings.","For a **lexical deficit**, there is trouble with word meanings and naming but not with grammatical structure."]},{"text":"So controlled tasks can test whether a particular operation is impaired and relate that impairment to a particular brain region.","children":[]}],"aphasias-16":[{"text":"There are **problems with this dichotomy**. **Dick and colleagues** had participants listen to **four types of sentences** and **indicate which animal is doing the action**.","children":["Two pictures appeared on a computer screen, one of a dog and one of a cat.","Participants pressed a button on a response box to choose the animal.","The event was always a dog biting a cat, but the sentence structure changed."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the doer first.","children":["For an **active** sentence, the wording is “The dog is biting the cat.”","For a **subject cleft**, the wording is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat.”","A **cleft** sentence splits a simple sentence with “It’s … that” to put focus on one part. In a subject cleft, the focused noun is the doer.","For both types, choosing the first animal mentioned gives the right answer."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the receiver of the action first.","children":["For an **object cleft**, the wording is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” The focused noun is the one being bitten.","For a **passive**, the wording is “The cat is bitten by the dog.” The receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","For both types, choosing the first animal mentioned gives the wrong answer."]},{"text":"So a listener who always picks the first animal succeeds on actives and subject clefts and fails on object clefts and passives. Only a listener who **parses the grammar** succeeds on all four.","children":[]}],"aphasias-17":[{"text":"When **aphasia patients and healthy aging controls** did this task, a pattern emerged **much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped by aphasia type using the Western Aphasia Battery.","children":[]},{"text":"Each group had its own profile across active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive sentences.","children":["For **elderly controls**, accuracy was close to 100% on all four types.","For **anomic** patients, whose main problem is word finding, accuracy was about 89% on actives and subject clefts. It dropped to about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives.","For **conduction** aphasia patients, accuracy was about 75% on actives and subject clefts. It fell to about 42% on object clefts.","For **Wernicke’s** patients, accuracy was low on every type, around 43% to 50%."]},{"text":"For **Broca’s aphasia patients**, there was **particular difficulty with the syntactically complex stimuli**, the object clefts and passives.","children":["They scored about 61% on actives and 65% on subject clefts.","They dropped to about 42% on object clefts and about 54% on passives.","These are **noncanonical** structures, meaning they depart from the usual doer-first order."]},{"text":"Their performance was **overall not so great**, however.","children":["Even on simple active sentences, Broca’s patients were far below the elderly controls.","So the pattern combines a **general drop** in performance with an **extra cost** for complex structures.","The groups differ in both overall accuracy and the size of the drop for complex sentences."]}],"aphasias-18":[{"text":"The same task was given to **healthy undergraduates** under **conditions that stress the language system** in different ways.","children":["The stress conditions included **speeding up the speech**, adding noise, and **asking people to remember lists of numbers as a secondary task**.","**Compressed** speech is speech played faster than normal.","A **low-pass filter** removes the higher frequencies from speech, which makes it sound muffled.","**Auditory digits** and **visual digits** are number lists that participants had to remember while listening, which uses up memory resources."]},{"text":"For **single-stress conditions**, accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts.","children":["Object clefts suffered most. With noise, accuracy fell from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts.","Passives also dropped under noise, to about 91%."]},{"text":"For **dual-stress conditions**, which combine two stressors, the effects grew larger.","children":["With low-pass filtering plus compression, accuracy was about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts.","It dropped to about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives."]},{"text":"The general rule is that **object cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all manipulations**. This is the same pattern seen in Broca’s aphasia.","children":["These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammatical knowledge.","So stressing their processing system is enough to produce an aphasia-like profile."]},{"text":"That is why the comprehension deficits in Broca’s aphasia **may be a “performance” issue after all**.","children":["Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so any reduction in resources hurts them first.","The patients may still have the grammatical knowledge, or competence, but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"aphasias-19":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked whether **Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits are language-specific**. Patients had to **match an action to an object**.","children":["For the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing had a boy pantomiming an action, such as licking.","Participants chose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","For the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words instead of by pantomime."]},{"text":"Controls were nearly perfect in both versions, and aphasic patients made more errors in both.","children":["For the non-linguistic version, controls scored **98.5%** and aphasic patients scored **93.4%**.","For the linguistic version, controls scored **99.4%** and aphasic patients scored **87.5%**.","So **Broca’s aphasics had difficulty with this task whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**."]},{"text":"Accuracy also depended on the type of wrong choice offered, called the **distractor**.","children":["For **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and patients scored 93.3%.","For **semantic** distractors, which are related in meaning to the correct object, controls scored 99.2% and patients dropped to **86.3%**.","For **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and patients scored 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, such as a different object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The non-linguistic version removes language from the input but keeps meaning in the task.","children":["A pantomimed action has to be understood and linked to the right object, just as a verb is linked to its object.","A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on the non-linguistic version.","So difficulty on that version points to a **broader problem with action meanings**, shared with language."]}],"aphasias-20":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of this task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area**.","children":["Lesion maps mark the brain areas linked to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**.","The maps cover three horizontal slices of the brain. Colored regions mark where damage was associated with poorer performance.","The hottest colors, red and yellow, mark the strongest relationships.","The strongest associations fall in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca’s area."]},{"text":"The task did not require producing or parsing language.","children":["Participants only had to interpret a pantomimed action and choose an object.","They did not have to produce a sentence.","They did not have to work out the grammar of a spoken instruction."]},{"text":"So damage to Broca’s area **affects more than syntax**.","children":["Patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved.","Broca’s area supports action understanding as well as grammatical processing.","That is why any account of Broca’s area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"aphasias-21":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues** asked whether **Wernicke’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits are language-specific**. Patients had to **match environmental sounds to pictures**.","children":["For the **nonverbal** version, a speaker played an environmental sound, such as a cow mooing. Participants chose between pictures, such as a cow and a sheep.","For the **verbal** version, the speaker played a spoken phrase, such as “cow mooing.” Participants chose between pictures, such as a cow and a violin."]},{"text":"The groups differed in overall accuracy.","children":["For **controls**, accuracy was 99.2% verbal and 98.1% nonverbal.","For patients with **right hemisphere damage**, accuracy was 99.1% and 98.2%.","For **anomic** patients, accuracy was 98.6% and 96.7%.","For **Broca’s** patients, accuracy was 96.3% and 95.2%.","For **Wernicke’s** patients, accuracy was **90.0% verbal** and **91.3% nonverbal**. So **Wernicke’s patients were particularly bad at this task**."]},{"text":"**No significant difference was observed between the verbal and nonverbal versions**.","children":["Wernicke’s patients struggled as much with an animal call as with a spoken phrase.","So the deficit goes beyond understanding spoken words.","Their difficulty concerns **linking sounds of any kind to their meanings**, whether the meaning arrives through language or through ordinary sounds."]}],"aphasias-22":[{"text":"**Deficits in both verbal and nonverbal sound-picture matching are associated with Wernicke’s area**.","children":["Lesion overlays mark where damage occurred in patients who were impaired on each version.","**Eight** patients were impaired in the **nonverbal** version.","**Ten** patients were impaired in the **verbal** version.","The warmest colors, red and yellow, mark areas damaged in the largest number of patients."]},{"text":"The two groups look very similar.","children":["For both, the region of greatest overlap is the **posterior temporal area of the left hemisphere**, around Wernicke’s area.","So the verbal and nonverbal impairments come from damage to the **same general region**."]},{"text":"The two versions differ in their input but share a demand.","children":["A spoken phrase conveys the meaning through words.","An animal call conveys the meaning without language.","Both tasks require connecting meaningful sounds to what they represent."]},{"text":"So damage around Wernicke’s area disrupts that connection in both cases. That is why Wernicke’s area appears to support **meaningful sound processing in general**, with language as one important case.","children":[]}],"aphasias-23":[{"text":"How language-specific the deficits in aphasia are is **still a controversy**.","children":["Some patients are especially vulnerable to complex grammatical structures. That suggests some aspects of language are handled in specialized ways.","The same patients can also struggle with action meanings or environmental sounds in tasks with no language. That suggests language shares brain systems with other kinds of meaning.","An explanation has to account for both kinds of finding.","If aphasia deficits were purely linguistic, language would appear to have its own dedicated brain systems."]},{"text":"What is clear is **how the terms of the debate have evolved**. The field now relies on three kinds of tools.","children":["The first is **relatively sophisticated tests that tap specific aspects of language**, such as reversible and improbable sentences, cleft sentences and nonverbal matching tasks.","The second is **quantitative analyses of behavior**, which measure accuracy across sentence types, patient groups and stress conditions.","The third is **quantitative analyses of structure-function relationships**, which relate patterns of brain damage to performance across many patients."]},{"text":"So the field has come **a long way from the 19th century**.","children":["Wernicke and Lichtheim drew boxes and arrows from a small number of individual cases.","Modern research can test which specific operations are impaired. It can map those impairments onto damaged tissue across large groups of patients."]}],"aphasias-24":[],"aphasias-25":[{"text":"**Voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping** formalizes **structure-function relationships** in aphasia using MRI data. It relates the location of brain damage to the severity of symptoms across many patients.","children":["The method comes from Bates, Wilson, Saygin, Dick, Sereno, Knight and Dronkers.","A **voxel** is a small three-dimensional unit of an MRI brain image, like a 3D pixel.","For each voxel, patients with damage there are compared with patients without damage there.","If the damaged patients score much worse, that voxel is associated with the symptom.","Colors from blue for weaker to red for stronger mark how strong the association is."]},{"text":"The **“symptoms”** are **defined very broadly**.","children":["For **fluency deficits**, the strongest associations are in the front part of the left hemisphere.","For **comprehension deficits**, the strongest associations are farther back, in the posterior temporal region."]},{"text":"So data from a large number of patients is consistent with a **rough double dissociation**.","children":["**Posterior regions** are associated with **perception**, or comprehension.","**Anterior regions** are associated with **production**, or fluency.","Each patient contributes a damage pattern and a continuous score. So the method turns many individual cases into one statistical map linking brain location to symptoms."]}],"development1-01":[],"development1-02":[{"text":"**Nature and nurture in child language** is the question of how much language comes from built-in capacities and how much comes from experience. **Nativist positions** grew out of **Chomsky's critique of Skinner**.","children":["**Skinner** was a behaviorist. He explained behavior, including language, with general learning mechanisms such as reward and punishment.","**Chomsky** argued that those mechanisms could not explain how children acquire grammar."]},{"text":"For Chomsky, the important feature of language is its **generativity**. Generativity is the ability to produce and understand an unlimited number of new sentences.","children":["Most sentences a person hears or says are new.","So acquiring a language means **identifying the abstract grammar** that produces the sentences of the language."]},{"text":"The general learning mechanisms studied by behaviorists were **not up to this task**, for two reasons.","children":["Grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are **not \"similar\"** in any straightforward way. A tiny change can make a sentence ungrammatical. Two grammatical sentences can look completely different.","The context for language acquisition does not look like **operant conditioning**. Operant conditioning shapes behavior with rewards and punishments that follow it. Children are not reliably rewarded for grammatical sentences or punished for ungrammatical ones. So the **reward and punishment contingencies do not hold**."]},{"text":"Nativist approaches assert two things instead.","children":["Children have **innate knowledge of what language is like**, called **Universal Grammar**. Universal Grammar is a built-in set of principles that limits which grammars a child will consider.","Children use **language-specific acquisition processes**. These are learning mechanisms built specifically for language."]},{"text":"Chomsky argued for this position by writing, \"The fact that all normal children acquire essentially comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable rapidity suggests that human beings are somehow specially designed to do this, with data-handling or 'hypothesis-formulating' ability of unknown character and complexity.\"","children":["Every typical child reaches a complex grammar quickly, even though children's experiences differ widely.","That is why nativists say innate limits narrow the possible grammars, while experience decides which language the child ends up with."]}],"development1-03":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** is the pattern in which listeners hear differences between speech sounds in terms of categories. Early theories treated it as a **language-specific** and **species-specific** phenomenon.","children":["Language-specific means it was thought to happen only for language.","Species-specific means it was thought to happen only in humans.","At the time, **only humans** were known to show categorical perception, and **only for speech sounds**."]},{"text":"The classic evidence comes from a synthetic consonant continuum of fourteen stimuli. The upper formant transitions change in equal steps from one stimulus to the next. The lower formant stays the same.","children":["In **labeling**, listeners switch sharply from **/b/** to **/d/** around stimuli 4 and 5. They switch from /d/ to **/g/** around stimuli 9 and 10.","In **discrimination**, accuracy **peaks near those boundaries**. Accuracy falls inside each category. This holds whether the pairs are one, two or three steps apart."]},{"text":"Categorical perception is a compelling example of how similarity between stimuli is defined in **linguistic, rather than physical, terms**.","children":["Two sounds in the **same category** can sound almost identical.","Two sounds on **opposite sides of a category boundary** can sound clearly different.","This happens even when the two pairs differ by the same acoustic amount."]},{"text":"So what counts as similar depends on the language categories. That is why categorical perception was taken as a sign of a specialized speech system.","children":[]}],"development1-04":[{"text":"Infant studies measure behaviors that change when an infant notices something new. **Head turns** and **sucking** reveal what an infant hears without the infant saying anything.","children":[]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** trains an infant to turn toward a sound when it changes. It is used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab.","children":["The infant sits on a parent's lap. An experimenter keeps the infant's attention forward with toys.","When the sound changes, a correct head turn earns an interesting visual reward, such as a lighted, moving toy.","Once the infant has learned this link, a head turn after a sound change means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** uses the infant's natural interest in something new.","children":["The infant sucks on a pacifier connected to a pressure recorder. Strong sucks make a sound play.","As one sound repeats, the infant gets used to it, so sucking slows down. Getting used to a repeated sound is called **habituation**.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has **noticed the change**."]},{"text":"Neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound. The experimenter controls exactly which acoustic difference is presented. So both methods test whether infants hear the same distinctions that adults hear.","children":[]}],"development1-05":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **4-month-old infants** with **high-amplitude sucking**. The sounds varied in **voice onset time (VOT)**. VOT is the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["With a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later after the release than with a VOT of +10 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable repeatedly until their sucking declined. Then the sound either changed or stayed the same. There were three conditions.","children":["In **20D**, the two sounds differed by 20 milliseconds of VOT, +20 versus +40 milliseconds. They came from **different adult phonemic categories**.","In **20S**, the two sounds also differed by 20 milliseconds, either −20 versus 0 or +60 versus +80 milliseconds. They came from the **same** category.","In **0**, the **control** condition, the same sound continued throughout."]},{"text":"In every condition, sucking first rose above the baseline rate and then declined before the change.","children":["**For 20D**, sucking **jumped back up** after the change, to around 50 responses per minute.","**For 20S**, sucking showed **little recovery** and kept falling.","**For the control**, sucking kept declining, down to about 15 responses per minute."]},{"text":"The two changed conditions were matched in acoustic size, so the difference comes from crossing the category boundary. The control establishes what happens with repetition alone. So infants responded to a change that **crossed an adult phoneme boundary** but not to an equally large change **within a category**.","children":[]}],"development1-06":[{"text":"Eimas's result was **a big deal** because of what categorical perception was thought to do. Categorical perception was considered the **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format** that later linguistic processes could use.","children":["A **symbolic** format represents a sound as a category, such as /b/.","Finding this ability in infants suggested that the starting point for language processing was already in place."]},{"text":"**Four-month-olds** are really little.","children":["Their **gross motor milestones** include manipulating objects with two hands and holding their head and chest up while prone. Prone means lying face down. Gross motor skills are large body movements.","Their **color vision** and general **visual acuity** are still \"in progress.\" Visual acuity is the sharpness of vision.","They are just starting to produce **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines. They cannot yet produce speech sounds well."]},{"text":"This is very, very early in development for an apparently **language-specific** ability to emerge.","children":["Infants sort speech sounds into categories before they can produce them.","They do this while their other abilities are still immature."]},{"text":"So speech categorization ran far ahead of other skills. That is why the result **seemed like evidence for innateness**.","children":[]}],"development1-07":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** for speech categories is the decline in sensitivity to sound distinctions that the child's language does not use. At around **six months**, English-learning infants discriminate speech contrasts they have **never heard before**. As development goes on, the ability to perceive these contrasts **declines**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants on two non-English contrasts.","children":["The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two \"ta\"-like sounds.","The **Salish contrast** is a distinction between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language.","They measured the percentage of infants at each age who reached a **discrimination criterion**."]},{"text":"The percentage fell with age for English-learning infants.","children":["**At 6 to 8 months**, about 95% discriminated the Hindi contrast and about 80% discriminated the Salish contrast.","**At 8 to 10 months**, about 67% and about 52% did.","**At 10 to 12 months**, about 20% and about 10% did.","**Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants** discriminated their native contrast close to 100% of the time."]},{"text":"This is **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**.","children":["Without experience of a contrast, English-learning infants lose much of their ability to hear it by about their first birthday.","Infants who hear the contrast in their language keep it, so the decline **depends on language experience**."]},{"text":"Perceptual narrowing is related to categorical perception through the native categories.","children":["As infants form the categories of their native language, sounds that the language groups together become harder to tell apart.","So a nonnative distinction inside one native category comes to be heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"development1-08":[{"text":"A **very compelling narrative** was built from these findings. It has three parts.","children":["Speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a **special property of the language system**.","These abilities are present **very early in development**, as the infant studies suggest.","Children start off as **\"citizens of the world.\"**"]},{"text":"A citizen of the world can hear **all contrasts**.","children":["This means children have **innate, language-general knowledge** about speech. Language-general knowledge applies to the speech sounds of every language.","So a newborn is ready to learn any human language.","Children then **rapidly tune** their perceptual systems to the specific language in their environment."]},{"text":"In this narrative, the role of learning is to **select** among the many possible innately specified phonetic contrasts.","children":["Learning narrows down the **inventory** to the contrasts that are meaningful in the child's native language. The inventory is the full set of phonetic contrasts the child can perceive.","Learning removes the contrasts that the native language does not use.","Learning keeps the innate categories that the native language needs."]},{"text":"So learning is a process of elimination, and the native language decides which innate categories survive.","children":[]}],"development1-09":[{"text":"The citizens-of-the-world view has **some problems**. Three of them concern **categorical perception**.","children":["Categorical perception is **not limited to language**. It also occurs for nonspeech sounds.","Categorical perception is **not limited to humans**. Animals without language also perceive speech sounds categorically.","Categorical perception is **task-dependent**. Results look more or less categorical depending on what the listener is asked to do. The task could be naming a sound or judging whether two sounds differ."]},{"text":"The fourth problem concerns **infant speech perception**, which is **not actually \"universal.\"**","children":["Infants do not start out able to hear every contrast equally well.","Some contrasts are hard for young infants to detect, even contrasts that occur in their own language."]},{"text":"Each problem targets a different part of the narrative.","children":["The nonspeech and animal findings question whether categorical perception is special to language.","The task effects question how categorical perception really is.","The uneven infant sensitivity questions whether all contrasts are innately available."]},{"text":"So a full account must explain contrasts that improve with experience as well as contrasts that are lost. That is why learning must **build categories** as well as remove them.","children":[]}],"development1-10":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** tested categorical perception with **non-speech sounds**. The sounds varied in **rise time**. Rise time is how quickly a sound grows to full loudness after it starts.","children":["A **short rise time** makes a sound like a **plucked** string.","A **long rise time** makes a sound like a **bowed** string."]},{"text":"The stimuli were **sawtooth waves**, **speech syllables** and **sine waves**. Each kind was made with a 10-millisecond rise time and a 70-millisecond rise time.","children":["With a 10-millisecond rise, the wave reaches full size almost immediately.","With a 70-millisecond rise, the wave grows gradually."]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, listeners identified each sound as pluck or bow. They also discriminated pairs that were 10 milliseconds apart.","children":["**For short rise times**, pluck responses are near 100%.","**For long rise times**, bow responses are near 100%.","The labels **switch steeply** between about 30 and 50 milliseconds.","Discrimination stays near 55 to 60% for most pairs. It **peaks near 75%** for pairs around 30 to 50 milliseconds, where the labels switch."]},{"text":"Pluck and bow are labels for kinds of sound onset, not speech categories. So a label boundary goes with better discrimination for a distinction that has nothing to do with phonemes. That is why categorical perception can arise in **general auditory processing**, without a mechanism specific to language.","children":[]}],"development1-11":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** tested whether **infants** show categorical perception of **non-speech sounds**. They used high-amplitude sucking.","children":["Infants heard one sound repeatedly until their sucking declined.","The sound was then changed, and three conditions were compared."]},{"text":"The three conditions were **no change**, a change **within categories** and a change **between categories**. In all three, sucking started near 20 per minute and declined to about 12 before the change.","children":["**After no change**, sucking stayed near 12.","**After a within-category change**, sucking also stayed near 12.","**After a between-category change**, sucking **jumped back up** to about 18. It then declined again as the new sound became familiar."]},{"text":"So infants responded more strongly to a non-speech change that **crossed a category boundary** than to a change within a category.","children":["This is the same pattern Eimas and colleagues found for speech.","Adults show categorical labeling and discrimination for plucked and bowed sounds. Infants show categorical discrimination for nonspeech changes.","Neither group needs language knowledge to show the pattern."]},{"text":"That is why early categorical perception reflects a **general auditory ability**, not only a part of speech.","children":[]}],"development1-12":[{"text":"**Chinchillas** also show categorical perception of **speech sounds**. This finding comes from **Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)** and the earlier work of **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**.","children":[]},{"text":"The animals were trained with **operant conditioning**. Operant conditioning is learning in which a behavior becomes more likely when a reward follows it.","children":["Each chinchilla learned that either the **0-millisecond lag** stimulus or the **80-millisecond lag** stimulus was an **SD** for food.","SD stands for **discriminative stimulus**, a signal that a response will be rewarded.","The lag is the **voice onset time**. Humans hear a 0-millisecond lag as /ga/ and an 80-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"After training, the animals were tested with **intermediate VOTs** along the /ga/-/ka/ continuum. Human listeners heard the same sounds. The measure was the proportion of stimuli labeled /ga/.","children":["**For short VOTs**, /ga/ responses stay near 1.","**For long VOTs**, /ga/ responses fall to near 0.","The fall is steep and happens mostly between about 30 and 70 milliseconds.","The chinchillas' labeling has the **same steep, categorical shape** as the humans' labeling."]},{"text":"**Increasing the intensity of the burst and aspiration noise** shifted the category boundary to a **shorter VOT**, made the switch steeper, or both. This held for one chinchilla and one human listener.","children":["The **burst** is the brief noise at the release of a stop. **Aspiration** is the breathy noise that follows a voiceless release.","So both species responded to these acoustic properties in a similar way."]},{"text":"Animals without language sort a speech continuum into categories. That is why categorical perception of speech is **shared with other species**.","children":[]}],"development1-13":[{"text":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found that **some contrasts are less salient than others for infants**. A **salient** contrast is one that is easy to notice.","children":[]},{"text":"The contrasts they tested use **nasal consonants** in the **onset**, which is the start of a syllable. The consonants differ in **place of articulation**, which is where in the mouth the sound is made.","children":["**/m/** is made with the lips.","**/n/** is made with the tongue behind the teeth.","**/ŋ/**, the final sound in \"sing,\" is made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate."]},{"text":"Infants heard **same** trials, where the syllable did not change, and **change** trials, where it switched. Longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change. The infants were English-hearing and 4 to 5 months old.","children":["**For the native [ma]-[na] contrast**, they looked about 3.3 seconds on same trials and about 5.4 seconds on change trials. So they **noticed** the switch.","**For the nonnative [na]-[ŋa] contrast**, they looked about 4.5 seconds on same trials and about 4.2 seconds on change trials. So they **did not notice** the switch."]},{"text":"A place contrast for nasals in onset, **/na/-/ŋa/**, is **not readily perceived** by four-month-olds, independent of language experience. In contrast, **/ma/-/na/** is **relatively salient**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the difference lies in the sounds themselves, not in experience. That is why the idea that every contrast starts out equally available runs into trouble.","children":[]}],"development1-14":[{"text":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010)** also tested infants in a **Filipino** language environment. In Filipino, [na] and [ŋa] are different syllables, so Filipino-learning infants hear this contrast regularly.","children":[]},{"text":"Between **6 and 10 months**, exposure to the contrast **increases its salience**.","children":["**At 6 to 8 months**, Filipino-learning infants looked about 3.8 seconds on both same and change trials. So they did **not yet notice** the switch.","**At 10 to 12 months**, looking rose from about 3.3 seconds on same trials to about 4.8 seconds on change trials. So they **noticed** the switch.","English-hearing infants at 4 to 5 months did not respond to this contrast, although they did respond to [ma]-[na]."]},{"text":"So it **cannot be that all speech contrasts are available innately**.","children":["Sensitivity to [na]-[ŋa] is weak at the start, even for infants whose language uses it.","Hearing the contrast over the first year makes it easier to detect."]},{"text":"**Exposure to contrasts in the environment is necessary for acquisition** in some cases.","children":["Learning can strengthen a contrast as well as preserve it.","That is why development includes building sensitivity to hard contrasts, not only losing sensitivity to unused ones."]}],"development1-15":[{"text":"The **emergentist approach** is the alternative to the nativist approach. Emergentist approaches assume that **associative learning mechanisms** are sensitive to **statistical regularities** in the environment.","children":["**Associative learning** is learning that two things go together because they repeatedly occur together.","**Statistical regularities** are patterns in how often things occur and which things occur together.","On this view, language structure emerges from general learning mechanisms working on these patterns in rich input."]},{"text":"Classical conditioning is the simplest case of associative learning. It can be described with three neurons, a **bell** neuron, a **food detector** neuron and a **salivation** neuron.","children":["The food detector already connects strongly to salivation, so food produces salivation.","When the bell repeatedly occurs with food, the **synapse** between the bell neuron and the salivation neuron is **strengthened**. A synapse is a connection between neurons.","After enough pairings, the bell alone can produce salivation."]},{"text":"This can extend beyond conditioning to support **pattern recognition**. Pattern recognition is identifying a recurring structure, such as a speech sound, across many varied examples.","children":["The same strengthening could link acoustic features that tend to occur together.","Repeated co-occurrence in speech could then build categories that were never specified in advance.","In conditioning, one event comes to predict another. In pattern recognition, many features come to predict a category."]},{"text":"So infants could learn speech categories by tracking which sounds occur together and how often. They would not have to start with a full set of built-in categories.","children":[]}],"development1-16":[{"text":"The **American vowels** are an example of statistical regularities. When **Kuhl and others** talk about learning the **statistics of distributions** in the environment, they mean patterns like these. A **distribution** describes how often each value occurs across many examples.","children":[]},{"text":"A **vowel space** is determined by the **first and second formants**. Each production of a vowel has one first formant value and one second formant value.","children":["Many productions of one vowel cluster together in one region of the space.","The regions of different vowels **overlap** heavily.","So a single pair of formant values can belong to more than one vowel.","That is why speech categorization is difficult. Listeners must learn where each vowel is centered and how far it spreads."]},{"text":"Maybe we learn the vowels of our language by **extracting the statistics** of these distributions.","children":["Over many exposures, the listener tracks where the productions of each vowel cluster.","The categories then reflect the shape of the input."]},{"text":"A **\"prototypical\" /a/** is then the **mean** (or perhaps the **mode**) of the distribution of all the /a/ sounds we hear.","children":["The mean is the average of all the values.","The mode is the most frequent value."]},{"text":"So the prototype comes from experience. That is why it can differ across languages and listeners.","children":[]}],"development1-17":[{"text":"**Maye and colleagues (2002)** tested whether infants really learn categories from distributions. Infants **listened passively** to sounds from a **[da]-[ta] continuum** of eight stimuli that differed in **voice onset time**. Passive listening means the infants heard the sounds with no task or reward.","children":[]},{"text":"Two groups heard different distributions of the same continuum.","children":["**For the bimodal group**, stimuli 2 and 7 were presented most often, 16 times each, and the middle stimuli were rare. Two peaks give **evidence for two different categories**.","**For the unimodal group**, stimuli 4 and 5 in the middle were presented most often, and the ends were rare. One peak gives **evidence for a single category**."]},{"text":"At test, infants listened to **stimuli 3 and 6** in **alternating** or **non-alternating** trials.","children":["In alternating trials, the two stimuli switched back and forth. In non-alternating trials, one stimulus repeated.","If infants treat 3 and 6 as different sounds, the two kinds of trials should hold their attention differently."]},{"text":"**Only infants pre-exposed to the bimodal distribution** were sensitive to the difference between the two kinds of trials.","children":["**Bimodal six-month-olds** looked 5.66 seconds on alternating trials and 6.41 seconds on non-alternating trials. **Bimodal eight-month-olds** looked 5.45 and 6.15 seconds.","**Unimodal six-month-olds** looked 4.85 and 4.53 seconds. **Unimodal eight-month-olds** looked 4.98 and 5.20 seconds. So unimodal infants looked about equally long on both."]},{"text":"So the infants in the unimodal condition acted as if they had learned to treat stimuli 3 and 6 as **members of the same category**. That is why a few minutes of exposure to a distribution supports **statistical learning** of speech categories.","children":[]}],"development1-18":[{"text":"Experiments suggest that **social interaction is important for learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"First, **nine-month-old American infants** were exposed to **Mandarin Chinese** in twelve 25-minute sessions.","children":["**For live exposure**, a Mandarin speaker played and read with the infants in person.","**For auditory or audiovisual exposure**, infants heard or watched Mandarin on audio or television.","A control group had English exposure.","Afterward, infants were tested on a **Mandarin phonetic contrast** with the **head-turn technique**."]},{"text":"Only live exposure led to phonetic learning.","children":["Infants with **live** Mandarin exposure scored about 65% correct, above the English control group at about 56%.","Infants with **TV or audio** Mandarin exposure scored about the same as controls, close to chance.","So the same exposure is **more effective in person** (and interactive) than as a video."]},{"text":"Second, **eight-month-old infants** received social feedback from their mothers in response to their vocalizations.","children":["**For the contingent condition**, mothers responded right after the infant vocalized. **Contingent** means the response depends on and follows the infant's behavior.","**For the yoked-control condition**, mothers responded on a schedule copied from another infant, so the responses were **noncontingent**.","A 30-minute familiarization phase came first, then a 10-minute baseline, a 10-minute social response phase and a 10-minute **extinction** phase. In extinction, the responses stopped."]},{"text":"The measure was the proportion of syllables among the infants' vocalizations.","children":["At baseline, both groups produced about 0.05 to 0.08.","During social response, the contingent group rose to about **0.32**, while the noncontingent group stayed near 0.05.","During extinction, the contingent group stayed higher at about 0.2, against about 0.1 for the noncontingent group.","So **contingent social feedback increased the quantity and complexity** of infants' vocalizations."]},{"text":"Social interactions contingent on the production of different sounds seem to work much like a **reinforcing stimulus**. A reinforcing stimulus makes a behavior more likely when it follows that behavior.","children":["One idea is that humans find social interaction with other humans **innately reinforcing**.","Another idea is that we are innately good at using **social cues** to learn from other people."]}],"development1-19":[{"text":"**Statistical learning with constraints** is learning from patterns in the input that is guided and limited in several ways. **Constraints** can mean a lot of things.","children":[]},{"text":"The first kind limits **which stimulus properties can be contrastive**. A **contrastive** property is one that distinguishes words, such as voicing in /b/ versus /p/.","children":["**Speaker** identity and **speech rate** change the sound a great deal. They do not change which word is said, so they are **not contrastive**.","A learner who tracked every statistical pattern would form categories for different voices and speeds.","So learning has to focus on the properties that matter for meaning."]},{"text":"The second kind is **based on earlier learning**.","children":["What a learner has already acquired shapes what is easy to learn next.","Once native categories are in place, new distinctions that cut across them become harder to notice and learn.","Constraints of this kind are central to **sensitive periods**, in which earlier experience limits later learning."]},{"text":"The third kind comes from **social and interactive contexts** for learning.","children":["The same sounds are learned more effectively in live, responsive interaction than from a recording.","Contingent responses from caregivers shape the sounds infants produce."]},{"text":"So learning depends on the patterns in the input, on what the learner already knows and on the social situation. That is why learning is fast and focuses on the distinctions that matter.","children":[]}],"development2-01":[],"development2-02":[{"text":"**Word segmentation** is the job of finding where one word ends and the next begins in continuous speech. Speech is **messy** because **there are no physical boundaries between words** in the signal.","children":["In “I hope it’s in my desk,” the sound energy of each word flows straight into the next word.","No silent gap separates the words from one another."]},{"text":"The pauses that do occur come from **closures**. A **closure** is a moment when the vocal tract is fully blocked, as for /p/, /t/, /d/ and /k/.","children":["Very little sound escapes during a closure, so a brief silence appears.","Closures are **as likely to happen within words as between words**.","In “hope” and “it’s,” the silences fall inside words or at their edges, so they do not match the word boundaries.","A listener who treated every silence as a boundary would split some words and merge others."]},{"text":"Listeners are still **really good at finding words** in this “sea of sounds.”","children":["Adults hear separate words with no effort, because they can lean on the words they already know.","The **harder question** is **how we learn to do this**. Infants have to discover the words in the stream before they can learn what those words mean."]},{"text":"So silence does not mark the words, and infants have to find them some other way.","children":[]}],"development2-03":[{"text":"Listeners have **several cues to word boundaries**. No single cue works for every word.","children":[]},{"text":"Some words **appear in isolation**, as in “Hello,” “No,” “Please,” “Go” and “Stop.”","children":["A word that is a whole utterance has obvious boundaries.","**Not all words** are heard alone, so this cue covers only part of the vocabulary."]},{"text":"**Phonotactics** is the set of patterns for which sound sequences a language allows. **Some sequences of phonemes are very unlikely to occur within words**.","children":["In “wordboundary,” the sequence /db/ almost never occurs inside an English word, so it suggests a break between word and boundary.","In “speechcode,” the sequence /tʃk/ suggests a break between speech and code in the same way.","“Movement” calls for caution. Its /vm/ sequence might suggest a break, but movement is one word."]},{"text":"**Stress** is the emphasis that makes one syllable louder, longer or higher in pitch. **In English, most words are stressed on the first syllable**.","children":["**Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)** first familiarized **7.5-month-old infants** with one word. Then the infants heard passages.","The kingdom passage kept using “kingdom,” as in “Your kingdom is in a faraway place.” The device passage kept using “device,” as in “Your device can do a lot.”","For infants familiarized with **“kingdom,”** which has first-syllable stress, there was a **preference for the kingdom passage**.","For infants familiarized with **“device,”** which has second-syllable stress, there was **not the same kind of preference**.","An infant who starts a new word at every stressed syllable finds kingdom easily but cuts device in the wrong place."]},{"text":"So in connected speech, **stress may help signal the beginnings of words**. It will **not work in every language or for every word in English**, because languages put stress in different places.","children":[]}],"development2-04":[{"text":"**Statistical learning** means picking up patterns in how often things occur together. **Transitional probabilities** give the learner one more cue to word boundaries through statistical learning.","children":[]},{"text":"The phrase “pretty baby” has one pair of syllables inside a word and one pair across two words.","children":["In English words, the syllable **/ti/** is **very strongly predicted by the syllable /prɪ/**. After “pre,” the next syllable is very often “ty,” because pretty is a common word.","Syllable pairs **across words** are less predictable. Many different words can follow “ty,” so “ba” is poorly predicted."]},{"text":"The comparison can be written with frequencies of occurrence.","children":["The frequency of “pretty” divided by the frequency of “pre” gives the chance that “pre” is followed by “ty.”","The frequency of “tyba” divided by the frequency of “ty” gives the chance that “ty” is followed by “ba.”","The first value is **far larger** than the second."]},{"text":"Syllables inside a word predict each other **strongly**. Syllables that straddle two words predict each other **weakly**.","children":["Pairs inside a word recur together again and again.","Pairs that cross a word boundary recur much less reliably, because each word can be followed by many others."]},{"text":"So a learner who counts how often each syllable follows each other syllable can find the joints in speech.","children":[]}],"development2-05":[{"text":"The **transitional probability** from syllable A to syllable B is the likelihood that B follows A. That likelihood is **greater within words than between words**.","children":["Inside a word, the same syllables always come in the same order, so the probability is high.","At a word boundary, almost any word can come next, so the probability is lower.","A drop in transitional probability is a good guess for a word boundary."]},{"text":"The cue is **probabilistic**, so it works most of the time but not every time.","children":["“What’s that in the road a/head?” can be heard as “a head” or as “ahead.” The sounds are identical, so the meaning of the whole sentence decides the boundary.","“I scream you scream we all scream for ice cream” plays on “I scream” and “ice cream” having almost the same sounds. One sequence can be split into words two ways."]},{"text":"Transitional probabilities tip the odds toward one way of splitting the stream. They work alongside stress, phonotactics and words heard alone.","children":["When the cues agree, segmentation is easy.","When the cues clash or are missing, as with “a head” and “ahead,” listeners fall back on context."]},{"text":"So transitional probability is a cue to a boundary, and it is not a marked boundary.","children":[]}],"development2-06":[{"text":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996)** tested whether **transitional probabilities** alone are enough to identify words. They separated **statistical information** from every other cue, so the stimuli were **tightly controlled**.","children":[]},{"text":"Each control removed one cue.","children":["The speech was **monotone**, with no changes in pitch, duration or amplitude. Amplitude means how loud the speech is. Without those changes, stress could not mark where words begin.","Every syllable was a **simple CV syllable**, which is one consonant followed by one vowel. No unusual consonant clusters occurred, so there were **no phonotactic cues** to word boundaries.","The syllables were **presented continuously** with **no pauses**, so silence could not signal a boundary."]},{"text":"The researchers built an **artificial language** from a set of **three-syllable “words,”** such as tokibu, gikoba and gopila.","children":["The words were joined in random order into one long stream.","The only clue to where each word began and ended was the transitional probability between syllables."]},{"text":"So infants who find the words in this stream must be using transitional probabilities alone, because every other cue was removed.","children":[]}],"development2-07":[{"text":"The stream of the artificial language has **no breaks at all**. It runs like “tokibugikobagopilatipolutokibu” and “gopilatipolutokibugikobagopila” and “gikobatokibugopilatipolugikoba.”","children":["The infant hears exactly this, with no spaces and no pauses.","**Four words** are buried in the stream. They are tokibu, gikoba, gopila and tipolu.","Each word recurs many times in random order."]},{"text":"The syllables still follow **regular patterns**.","children":["Inside **tokibu**, “to” is always followed by “ki.” Then “ki” is always followed by “bu.”","After “bu,” the stream may go on with “gi,” “go” or “ti.” The next syllable depends on which word comes next."]},{"text":"Some syllable pairs are **fully predictable** and others are not.","children":["A learner who keeps track of which syllables follow which can discover the difference.","The order of the syllables is the only signal of where words begin and end."]},{"text":"So everything needed to find the words is in the statistics of the stream.","children":[]}],"development2-08":[{"text":"Marking each of the four words separately makes its position in the stream visible to a reader. With one color for each word, tokibu is red, gikoba is green, gopila is pink and tipolu is purple.","children":["**Tokibu**, **gikoba**, **gopila** and **tipolu** each appear many times.","The infant gets no such marking. The infant hears only one unbroken run of syllables."]},{"text":"Each word keeps the **same inner order** every time, but its **neighbors keep changing**.","children":["“To-ki-bu” always comes in that order.","Tokibu may be followed by gikoba, gopila or tipolu.","So transitional probabilities are **high inside** each word and **lower across word edges**."]},{"text":"The word edges are exactly where **transitional probability falls**.","children":["The signal itself contains no spaces.","The learner notices where predictability drops."]},{"text":"That is why the learner can recover where one word ends and the next begins.","children":[]}],"development2-09":[{"text":"A test for an effect of **transitional probabilities** has to rule out plain familiarity. After a long stream of syllables, infants are trivially more familiar with sequences they have heard than with ones they have not.","children":["Infants could prefer heard sequences only because they are familiar.","That preference would not involve transitional probabilities at all."]},{"text":"**Two additional details** remove that possibility.","children":["**Half of the words were repeated more frequently** than the other half.","The familiarity test compared words against **“part-words”** that were **matched for frequency of occurrence** with real words.","A **part-word** is a three-syllable sequence that spans a word boundary. It joins the end of one word to the start of the next."]},{"text":"Take the stream “tokibu gikoba gopila gikoba tokibu gopila tokibu gikoba gikoba tokibu.”","children":["The word **GOPILA** and the part-word **BU-GIKO** each occur **twice**, so they are equally frequent.","For **BU-GIKO**, the probability of GI given BU is **2 out of 4**. BU occurs four times, at the end of every tokibu, and GI follows only two of them.","For **GOPILA**, both transitional probabilities are **2 out of 2**. GO is always followed by PI, and PI is always followed by LA."]},{"text":"So frequency is equal and only predictability differs. Any difference between gopila and bu-giko must come from transitional probabilities.","children":[]}],"development2-10":[{"text":"The test produced a **novelty effect**. Infants **looked longer in the direction of the “part-word” stimuli**.","children":["Words got about 6.8 seconds of looking. Part-words got about 7.6 seconds.","The difference was statistically significant."]},{"text":"Longer looking at part-words is a **novelty preference**. Infants tend to look longer at whatever seems new.","children":["The words had grown familiar during listening, so they were less interesting.","The part-words felt less familiar, even though infants had heard them just as often."]},{"text":"Frequency was equal for words and part-words. Only their transitional probabilities could separate them.","children":["The infants noticed that the syllables in part-words do not reliably go together.","After a brief exposure, infants pulled units out of a continuous stream using only how predictable the syllables were."]},{"text":"So the infants **had learned the statistical regularities of the speech stream**. They were **not merely picking up on simple frequency of occurrence**.","children":[]}],"development2-11":[{"text":"A **referent** is whatever a word refers to. **Identifying word referents** is the problem that comes after segmentation.","children":["Children can now “find” the words in speech. They still have to learn what the words mean.","A child may hear “cat” while looking at a cat.","Recognizing the sound pattern “cat” is one achievement. Knowing that it names the furry animal is a separate one."]},{"text":"**Segmentation** delivers a recurring chunk of sound, but it gives **no information about meaning**.","children":["Any scene holds many objects, parts, properties and actions.","The learner needs more evidence to tell which of these the speaker has in mind."]},{"text":"Children learn what a word means by **linking its form to the speaker’s intended meaning**.","children":["The child gathers that evidence from the situation in which the word is used."]},{"text":"So finding a word and learning its meaning are two separate achievements.","children":[]}],"development2-12":[{"text":"**Induction** is reasoning from particular examples to a general rule. In word learning, the rule is the meaning of a word. The **inductive problem of early word learning** is that a single experience fits many possible meanings.","children":[]},{"text":"A child hears a new word while watching a cat.","children":["The word might name a category, such as **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**. These are categories at different levels.","The word might be **FELIX**, the name of this particular cat.","The word might name a property, such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**.","The word might name an action, such as **WALKING**.","The word might name a part, such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings together make up the **hypothesis space**.","children":["Each candidate fits what the child sees.","Nothing in one scene singles out the meaning the speaker intends."]},{"text":"This is a form of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**.","children":["A linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by.","“Gavagai” might mean rabbit. It might also mean undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else."]},{"text":"Children meet this problem with every new word and still learn words fast. So something must narrow the possibilities. Joint attention, cues to the speaker’s intention and default strategies do that narrowing.","children":[]}],"development2-13":[{"text":"**Joint attention** is sharing an attentional focus with another person. It is one way children acquire word meanings.","children":["Two people look at the same object.","Each person knows that the other is attending to it too."]},{"text":"**Gaze following** is looking where someone else is looking. It **emerges around 9 months of age**.","children":["An adult looks over at an object.","The infant follows the adult’s eyes to that object."]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**.","children":["The infant grasps that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific.","If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent."]},{"text":"So joint attention shrinks the **hypothesis space**, which is the set of meanings a word might have. It ties the word to what the speaker is attending to.","children":[]}],"development2-14":[{"text":"**Joint attention optimizes word learning**. **Baldwin and colleagues** tested this with two groups of infants who heard a novel word while playing with an object.","children":[]},{"text":"The two groups differed in who said the word.","children":["In the **joint attention group**, an adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with.","In the **no joint attention group**, a voice from behind a screen labeled the object the baby was playing with."]},{"text":"The two conditions matched closely.","children":["In each one, the word came while the infant was focused on the same object.","They differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object."]},{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group**. They did **not** learn it in the no joint attention group.","children":["The hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment. So the difference in learning came from the visible, engaged speaker.","Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough."]},{"text":"So infants need evidence that a speaker means the word to refer to that object.","children":[]}],"development2-15":[{"text":"An **intentional cue** is a signal of what the speaker means to refer to. Children use **intentional cues from the speaker** to narrow the **hypothesis space** for new words.","children":["**Eye gaze direction** is one such cue.","When a speaker looks toward one toy, the direction of the eyes marks the object of the speaker’s attention."]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to infants of **16 to 17 months** and infants of **18 to 19 months** in two conditions.","children":["In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on while saying the new word. Both cues pointed to one toy.","In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket while saying the new word. That toy was different from the infant’s toy."]},{"text":"The discrepant condition pulls the infant’s focus apart from the speaker’s focus.","children":["Infants who attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy.","Infants who follow the speaker’s gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket."]},{"text":"So comparing the two conditions reveals whether infants treat the speaker’s gaze as evidence of meaning.","children":[]}],"development2-16":[{"text":"**Gaze direction** helps a child work out which object a speaker is naming when several objects are present.","children":["A speaker who looks at one object while saying a new word ties the word to that object.","The child can follow the speaker’s eyes to the **intended referent**."]},{"text":"The speaker’s focus can be **different from the child’s own focus**.","children":["Following the speaker’s focus means using information about someone else’s attention.","The child can then attach a new word to an object it was not looking at when the word was spoken."]},{"text":"The sound of the word gives no clue to its meaning. Gaze still narrows the possible meanings.","children":["The child combines the new word with evidence of what the speaker intends.","Following gaze rests on understanding that a speaker’s eyes point to what the speaker means."]},{"text":"That is why gaze gives a far **more specific mapping** than linking the word to everything in view.","children":[]}],"development2-17":[{"text":"**Intentional cues from the speaker** include **affective and behavioral cues**. **Affective cues** are emotional expressions. **Behavioral cues** are the speaker’s actions.","children":["An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was searching for.","An action, such as reaching for something, can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"**Akhtar and colleagues** found that infants **used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":["The adult’s emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one meant.","Infants linked the new word to the object that the adult’s expression picked out."]},{"text":"Several objects may be present when a word is spoken. The speaker’s reaction can single out one of them.","children":["For a **satisfied** expression, the speaker has found the desired object.","For a **disappointed** expression, the object is not the one the speaker meant."]},{"text":"So word learning involves grasping the goal behind the interaction.","children":[]}],"development2-18":[{"text":"A speaker’s **emotional reaction** works as an **intentional cue**. It can pick out the intended object from several candidates.","children":["Satisfaction or excitement can tie one object to what the speaker was trying to find or do.","The learner can use that tie to interpret a new label."]},{"text":"**Intention is conveyed through more than gaze direction**.","children":["Facial expressions and actions also reveal what a speaker means.","A word may be said near several objects that are not all equally likely referents."]},{"text":"The helpful information lies in **how the adult responds to each object**.","children":["Connecting the label to that response shrinks the word’s **hypothesis space**, the set of meanings it might have.","Infants who use emotional reactions are following the speaker’s goals. They understand that the speaker is searching for something and that the word names what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"So the word attaches to the object that meets the goal. Objects that disappoint the speaker drop out.","children":[]}],"development2-19":[{"text":"**Word learning strategies** are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**. A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":["These strategies **function to narrow the hypothesis space for new words**.","They give children a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The **whole object assumption** is one such strategy. It leads a child to expect a new word to name **a whole object**.","children":["The child does not expect the word to name a part of the object, its color, its texture or something it is doing.","A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will favor “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"The assumption rules out many unlikely meanings at once. It also fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":["It is only a starting preference, so later evidence can override it.","Children do eventually learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way."]},{"text":"So the whole object assumption speeds learning by removing many unlikely meanings before the child considers them.","children":[]}],"development2-20":[{"text":"**Mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**, is another word learning strategy. It says that, **as a default, objects can have only one name**.","children":["The implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","Mutual exclusivity **emerges during the second half of the second year**, roughly between 18 and 24 months."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view.","children":["The child hears a new word.","The dog already has a name, so the child takes the new word to mean the parrot.","No gaze cue or emotional cue is needed for this choice."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity combines with the **whole object assumption**.","children":["The whole object assumption says a new word names an object.","Mutual exclusivity says it names an object that has no name yet."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity is a default. So later learning can override it when an object turns out to have two names.","children":[]}],"development2-21":[{"text":"With **mutual exclusivity**, a child who hears an unfamiliar word gives it to an object that has **no known name yet**.","children":["An object the child can already name is an unlikely target for a new word.","The choice comes from comparing the objects in view."]},{"text":"**Prior vocabulary** determines which objects count as already named.","children":["Two children in the same scene can guess differently if they know different words.","The strategy builds directly on earlier learning."]},{"text":"Vocabulary growth feeds itself.","children":["A child who knows many names can rule out many objects.","The unnamed objects left over become the likeliest referents for new words."]},{"text":"So every word the child knows helps narrow the meaning of the next new word.","children":[]}],"development2-22":[{"text":"Children start putting words together in ways that are hard to tell apart. In production, it is difficult to tell what is going on when children produce only one, two or three words at a time.","children":["Very different processes can produce the same short utterance."]},{"text":"**Menn** distinguishes among four kinds of early utterances.","children":["**“Just a bunch of relevant words”** means the child strings together words that fit the situation, with no grammar linking them.","**Formulas** are phrases memorized as whole units. The child does not know they have separate parts.","**Word combinations** are separate words that the child joins productively to make new combinations.","**Stable constructions** are consistent patterns the child uses to build utterances. They are **not necessarily adult-like**."]},{"text":"A child might say “Up!” and “Pick you up!” and “Mommy up!” in the same kind of situation.","children":["“Pick you up!” sounds more advanced, but it may be a formula copied whole from adult speech.","“Mommy up!” is shorter, but it may be a real combination of two separate words.","Utterance length alone cannot tell which process produced each one."]},{"text":"A **full description of the context** in which an utterance is produced is **necessary but often not sufficient** to distinguish among these.","children":["Context reveals what the child intends and how the utterance is used across situations.","Even with complete context, the source of a given utterance can remain uncertain."]},{"text":"So a short utterance alone does not tell you whether a child is really combining words.","children":[]}],"development2-23":[{"text":"**Comprehension** and **production** develop at different rates. Even children at the **one-word stage** in production can use **basic transitive word order** in comprehension.","children":["In the one-word stage, a child speaks only in single words.","A **transitive** sentence has a verb with a subject and an object.","English transitive order puts the actor first and the one acted on after the verb."]},{"text":"**Gertner and Fisher** describe how children do this with the **structure-mapping account** of early **syntactic bootstrapping**. Syntactic bootstrapping means using sentence structure to help learn meaning.","children":["The input sentence is “The boy is tickling the girl!” and the child builds a **partial sentence representation** from it. It records the verb tickle with boy as the first noun and girl as the second noun.","The observed scene is a boy tickling a girl. From it, the child builds **conceptual representations** of possible events.","One event is giggle with the girl as agent. Another is tickle with the boy as agent and the girl as patient. Two more are play with the boy as agent and play with the girl as agent.","An **agent** performs an action, and a **patient** is affected by it.","A **one-to-one mapping between nouns and participant roles** lines up the two nouns with the two participants in the tickling event. Two nouns fit an event with two participants."]},{"text":"So comprehension can get ahead of production. A child who says only single words can still use noun order to tell who did what. Matching nouns to the participants in a scene also helps the child figure out what the verb means.","children":[]}],"development2-24":[{"text":"A **generalization test** checks whether children have really learned the **transitive structure**. If they have, they will **generalize it to new verbs**.","children":["A child who has only memorized particular sentences will be lost with an unfamiliar verb.","A child who knows the structure can use word order to interpret any verb."]},{"text":"The test sentence is “The duck is gorping the bunny.” The child has to select which of two novel actions it could depict.","children":["**“Gorp”** is invented, so the meaning of the verb gives no help.","The child needs to know that the first noun is the doer and the second noun is the one acted on."]},{"text":"**Gertner and colleagues (2006)** ran this test with **two-year-olds**.","children":["Children first heard “Hey look! The duck’s gonna gorp the bunny!” before any scene appeared.","Two scenes then appeared side by side. In one scene, a person dressed as a duck pushed a person dressed as a bunny in a wagon. In the other scene, the bunny acted on the duck.","Children heard “The duck is gorping the bunny! See?” with the scenes. After a short break they heard “The duck gorped the bunny! Find gorping!” and the scenes appeared again.","The two-year-olds **looked significantly longer at the duck acting on the rabbit** than at the reverse. So they understood the sentence from noun order alone."]},{"text":"Children at this age typically have a **mean length of utterance (MLU)** of **around 2**. MLU is the average number of **morphemes** in each utterance, and morphemes are meaningful words or word parts.","children":["Their three-morpheme utterances might be like “Duck gorp bunny.”","That is why their own speech is short and incomplete while they already understand the full transitive structure."]}],"l1-critical-01":[],"l1-critical-02":[{"text":"**Critical periods** and **sensitive or “optimal” periods** describe how the timing of experience affects learning. They differ in how strict the time limits are.","children":[]},{"text":"A **critical period** has a **discrete, “programmed” onset and offset**.","children":["The window opens and closes at fixed points in development.","Change in response to the environment, which is learning, is **strictly limited to this period**.","There is **no sensitivity to the environment before or after** the window, so experience outside it has no effect."]},{"text":"A **sensitive or “optimal” period** is a looser link between timing and learning.","children":["**Learning is possible throughout the life span**.","Learning is **optimal during particular windows**, when it is easiest or works best.","These windows can have a **gradual onset and offset**, and experience can itself change their timing."]},{"text":"The two ideas make different predictions for late learners.","children":["A critical period predicts **no learning** after the window, so any late improvement counts against it.","A sensitive period predicts that late learning is **harder but still possible**, with a lasting advantage for early learners."]},{"text":"So the difference is between impossibility and reduced effectiveness. Even the classic critical period in vision fits the term sensitive period better, and the same question arises for language.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-03":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is **poor vision in one eye despite normal peripheral function**. **Peripheral function** is the working of the eye itself. So the eye can be healthy while vision through it is poor. The problem lies in how the brain handles input from that eye.","children":[]},{"text":"Three conditions can lead to amblyopia.","children":["In **strabismus**, the eyes are misaligned, so they point in different directions and send the brain two conflicting images.","In a **cataract**, the lens of the eye is clouded, so light reaching the back of the eye is blurred or blocked.","In **anisometropia**, the two eyes differ greatly in focusing power, so one eye's image is much blurrier than the other's."]},{"text":"These conditions cause **periods of deprivation from normal visual input**. That deprivation can result in **poor vision long after the conditions themselves have been remediated**, meaning corrected.","children":["Straightening the eyes, removing the cataract or correcting the focus restores the input from the eye.","The brain may still fail to use that input well."]},{"text":"So a temporary problem early in life can leave a **lasting effect**. That is why the timing of the problem matters as much as the problem itself.","children":["A cataract removed early may leave little lasting damage.","A cataract removed late may leave poor vision even after the lens is clear."]}],"l1-critical-04":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** occurs because of **competitive dynamics between cortical inputs**. Both eyes send input to **visual cortex**. The two inputs **compete** to control the neurons there.","children":["When both eyes work normally, they share the cortical neurons evenly.","Abnormal vision in one eye can lead to **“colonization”** of the cortical representation of that eye by the other eye. The stronger eye takes over neurons that would have served the weaker eye."]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation** means blocking or degrading the input to one eye. In mice, it reveals how the takeover unfolds over time.","children":["Within **2 to 3 days**, there is **increased spine motility**. **Dendritic spines** are small projections where neurons receive connections, and they become unstable and move around.","Within **3 to 4 days**, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration**. Connections from the deprived eye disappear. Wallerian degeneration is the breakdown of nerve fibers cut off from their source.","After **more than a week**, there is **axon sprouting and new spine birth**. The open eye's fibers branch out and form connections in the space the deprived eye lost."]},{"text":"When normal function is restored to the deprived eye, it **remains at a competitive disadvantage**.","children":["The other eye now holds the connections.","Ordinary viewing with both eyes open keeps favoring the stronger eye."]},{"text":"That is why **normal cortical responses cannot be restored by normal experience**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-05":[{"text":"Treatments for amblyopia are **based on competition**. One of the most effective treatments involves **intermittently patching the “good” eye**.","children":["A patch on the stronger eye blocks its input for part of the day.","The brain then has to rely on the weaker eye."]},{"text":"Patching **restores the competitive balance between the eyes**. It then permits the **brain's own plasticity** to restore normal function. **Plasticity** is the brain's ability to change its connections in response to experience.","children":["With the strong eye covered, the weak eye's input can win back cortical connections.","The patch does not repair the eye itself. It changes the experience the brain receives, and plasticity does the rest."]},{"text":"The treatment is **most effective early in development**. It **may be ineffective by late childhood**, around 10 years.","children":["A young brain is highly plastic, so rebalancing the input leads to rapid reorganization.","As the visual system matures, the same treatment helps less and less."]},{"text":"So the timing of treatment matters as much as the treatment itself.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-06":[{"text":"**Levi and Polat (1996)** tested whether adults with amblyopia can still improve after the critical period. Adults with amblyopia are normally considered past the point where treatment works. Some of these patients were **in their 50s**.","children":[]},{"text":"The adults did **long-term, intensive training** on specific **psychophysical tasks** with the affected eye. A psychophysical task measures the relationship between a physical stimulus and what a person perceives. One such measure is the faintest contrast a person can detect.","children":["They practiced visual judgments with the amblyopic eye over many thousands of trials.","Training was counted in **kilotrials**, and one kilotrial is a thousand trials."]},{"text":"Improvement was measured as a falling **threshold**. A threshold is the smallest stimulus a person can reliably detect, so a **lower threshold means better vision**.","children":["Over the first **19 kilotrials**, the threshold **dropped steadily**, and this change was highly significant.","From about **20 to 35 kilotrials**, the threshold **leveled off** with little further change.","Around **36 kilotrials**, the threshold **dropped again** to a new, lower level and stayed there."]},{"text":"Training improved both **task performance** and **general visual function**, so the benefits spread beyond the trained task.","children":[]},{"text":"So **plasticity is not abolished entirely**, even well into adulthood. The adult visual system keeps some **experience-dependent plasticity**. The right kind and amount of training can still produce change.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-07":[{"text":"**Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)** tested video game play as training for **adult amblyopia**. Adults played an action video game with their amblyopic eye. The game was a first-person shooter set in a battle scene.","children":[]},{"text":"Playing video games **enhanced visual acuity and visual attention**.","children":["**Visual acuity** is sharpness of vision, the ability to see fine detail. Better acuity means clearer vision through the weak eye.","**Visual attention** is the ability to pick out and focus on the important parts of a scene. Targets in a busy battle are one such part."]},{"text":"Action games demand quick visual decisions and constant attention. So they force the visual system to use the amblyopic eye's input.","children":["These adults were long past childhood visual development, so their gains came from the training itself.","The gains came well after the age when patching usually works best."]},{"text":"So video game training adds to the evidence for **plasticity** in adult amblyopia. A demanding, engaging visual task can drive change in a mature visual system. That is why the closing of an early optimal window does not remove every chance of improvement.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-08":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is a classic example of a **sensitive period**. Three features of the early window support this.","children":["A **peripheral disturbance in early experience** can lead to **life-long disruption in visual function**. An early problem with the eye itself permanently affects vision.","The long-term loss of function is the **sequela** of a temporary deficit during **experience-dependent organization of the brain**. A sequela is a lasting result of an earlier condition. The brain organized itself around abnormal input, and that organization stays.","**Treatment is most effective during a limited developmental window**. Patching works best early and loses its power by late childhood."]},{"text":"However, new evidence suggests that the **right kind of experience can reverse deficits late into adulthood**.","children":["Intensive perceptual training improved vision in adults.","Video game play also improved vision in adults.","Late learning is harder and needs special conditions, but it does happen."]},{"text":"A strict critical period would rule out any late improvement. Amblyopia has a strong early window and some ability to change later.","children":[]},{"text":"That is why amblyopia fits a sensitive period better than a strict critical period.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-09":[{"text":"Two lines of work relate the vision evidence to **language**.","children":["The first is **Lenneberg's early attempts** to synthesize **developmental neurology** with the notion of a **“language acquisition device”**. Developmental neurology studies how the nervous system develops. A language acquisition device is a proposed innate capacity for learning language.","The second is **studies of early deprivation**. These use data from **neglected children** and from **Deaf children with limited input to sign**."]},{"text":"Lenneberg asked whether changes in the maturing brain could open and close a window for language.","children":["He linked language milestones to stages of brain development.","If such a window exists, language learning depends on the state of the brain as well as on the input."]},{"text":"Deprivation studies look at people who first met an accessible language unusually late.","children":["Neglected children were cut off from ordinary language input.","Some Deaf children have no access to sign language in early childhood.","Comparing them with typical learners tests whether the age of first exposure affects the final outcome."]},{"text":"So the two lines answer two questions. Lenneberg's work asks whether the brain has a window for language. The deprivation cases ask what happens when language input arrives late.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-10":[{"text":"In **Biological Foundations of Language**, **Lenneberg (1967)** tried to synthesize contemporary linguistics with what was known about brain development. Studies of language development were starting to find a fairly robust and **stage-like pattern**.","children":[]},{"text":"Data from Austrian, British and American children follow the same order between 8 and 36 months.","children":["**Words** come first, mostly between about 10 and 18 months.","**Two-word phrases** come next, mostly between about 18 and 26 months.","**Sentences of five words or more** come last, between about 28 and 36 months."]},{"text":"Lenneberg paired language milestones with motor milestones at each age.","children":["At **8 months**, infants stand holding on and pick up small objects. **Reduplicated** babbling, such as repeated syllables, becomes frequent. Its intonation patterns can signal emphasis and emotion.","At **10 months**, infants creep efficiently and pull to standing. Vocalizations mix with sound play such as gurgling and bubble-blowing. They try to imitate sounds and begin to tell words apart.","At **12 months**, infants walk with one hand held. Words such as **mama** or **dada** emerge. Infants show definite signs of understanding some words and simple commands.","At **18 months**, children have a vocabulary of more than three but fewer than fifty words. Much babbling continues, and comprehension progresses rapidly."]},{"text":"**Maturational milestones** are developmental achievements tied to physical growth. Language milestones line up with motor milestones, so the two seem to share one biological timetable.","children":[]},{"text":"That is why language milestones were seen as consistent with the **unfolding of a biological program**. Lenneberg proposed that brain maturation sets the pace for both.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-11":[{"text":"Data on typical brain development were **sparse** in Lenneberg's time. They were almost entirely based on **post-mortem analyses of tissue**, and post-mortem means after death.","children":["Researchers could not follow the same children's brains as they grew.","They had to piece development together from the brains of children who had died at different ages."]},{"text":"**Neuron size** was measured as the cell body volume of neurons in the cerebral cortex. Cortical layers III, IV and V were measured separately.","children":["Neuron size grows rapidly in the **first two years** and then levels off.","**No data were available between ages 2 and 18**.","That gap overlaps much of the period of language acquisition."]},{"text":"Lenneberg observed abrupt changes in neuron size and **packing density** before age two. He proposed that these changes might be **permissive** for the beginning of language acquisition.","children":["Packing density is how closely neurons are crowded together in the tissue.","Permissive means the changes make language acquisition possible without directly causing it."]},{"text":"So the **critical period seems to open after this initial period of rapid development**. The brain first goes through a burst of physical growth. Once that growth settles, the window for learning language opens.","children":["Lenneberg filled the gap in the data with a reasonable guess about how the brain matures.","Later research would need to test that guess directly."]}],"l1-critical-12":[{"text":"More extensive data came from studies of **recovery from stroke**. **Aphasia is surprisingly rare in children with brain lesions**. A **lesion** is an area of damaged brain tissue.","children":["In adults, damage to language areas often causes lasting aphasia.","In children, similar damage often has much milder effects on language."]},{"text":"In extreme cases, a **hemispherectomy** was performed. This surgery removes or disconnects one entire **cerebral hemisphere**, typically to relieve seizures in people with epilepsy.","children":["**Children** whose left hemisphere is removed still seem to **develop typical language abilities**.","**Adults do not recover language** after this procedure."]},{"text":"Lenneberg summarized hemispherectomy outcomes from **Basser (1962)** by the age when the lesion was acquired.","children":["For lesions **before the teens** with **left** removal, **49** had speech not affected or improved. Only **3** had permanent aphasia, and all 3 had aphasia before the operation.","For lesions before the teens with **right** removal, **38** had speech not affected or improved. Another 5 had permanent aphasia, and all 5 had aphasia before the operation.","For one lesion **during puberty** with left removal, there was **slow improvement**. Some aphasia remained to the end of life, 27 months after surgery.","For **adults** with **left** removal, **none** had speech unaffected or improved, and **6 had permanent aphasia**.","For adults with right removal, 25 had speech unaffected or improved, and none had permanent aphasia."]},{"text":"These findings, among other things, led Lenneberg to hypothesize what closes the critical period. He proposed that a key factor was the development of **adult-like cerebral dominance**. Cerebral dominance is the specialization of one hemisphere, usually the left, for language.","children":["In young children, language is not yet fixed in the left hemisphere, so the right hemisphere can take over.","Once dominance is established in adulthood, the right hemisphere can no longer take over. That is why removing the left hemisphere then leaves permanent aphasia."]}],"l1-critical-13":[{"text":"Systematic studies of children with **perinatal strokes** complicate the hemispherectomy findings. A perinatal stroke happens between the **prenatal period and 28 days after birth**.","children":[]},{"text":"Outcomes for these children are **clearly much better than for adults with similar injuries**. The children still show some effects of the injury.","children":["**Early language deficits** appear in **both right-hemisphere and left-hemisphere patients**.","In adults, language problems mainly follow left-hemisphere damage.","In young children, damage to either side causes early language delays.","**Some deficits are worse for children with right-hemisphere damage**, which is the opposite of the adult pattern."]},{"text":"Language outcomes are generally in the **low-normal range**.","children":["That suggests a **persistent delay** rather than a catching up.","The children recover far better than adults.","They often do not reach the level of children without injury."]},{"text":"So early plasticity is **real but limited**. A young brain can reorganize to support language. The reorganized system may still work less well than the original would have.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-14":[{"text":"**Curtiss and colleagues (1974)** studied the **effects of early deprivation** through the case of **Genie**.","children":["Genie was born in **April 1957**.","When researchers first met her, she was **13 years and 7 months old**.","She was so thin that she looked six or seven."]},{"text":"Her early life involved extreme isolation and abuse.","children":["**From the age of 20 months**, she was confined to a small room under increasing physical restraint.","Most of the time she was harnessed to an infant's potty chair. Otherwise she was confined in a homemade sleeping bag in an infant's crib covered with wire mesh.","She received minimal care from a mother who was rapidly losing her sight. She was fed only infant food.","Her father **physically punished her if she made any sounds**.","Her father and older brother did not speak to her, but at times they barked at her like dogs.","The home had no television or radio, and her contact with other people was extremely limited."]},{"text":"Genie was hospitalized for malnutrition. At that point she **could not stand up straight or chew food** and was **not toilet trained**. She **did not speak, cry or produce any vocal sounds**.","children":[]},{"text":"So her case tells what happens when first-language learning starts in adolescence. Her lack of language came together with severe physical, social and emotional deprivation. That is why the effect of missing language input **cannot be separated** from her other hardships.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-15":[{"text":"Genie's language was observed for **four years**, and she **learned quite a bit** in that time.","children":["She came to **comprehend hundreds of words**.","She came to comprehend some aspects of **morphology**, and she was **particularly good with plurals**. Morphology is the system of meaningful word parts, such as the plural ending “-s”.","She came to **comprehend some prepositions**, which are words for spatial relationships between objects."]},{"text":"A test of prepositions used **buttons and plastic boxes** of different colors and sizes. Genie had to move the buttons and boxes according to spoken instructions.","children":["A sample item was **“Put the red box behind the blue box.”**","The test covered **under, over, in, on, behind, in front of, in back of** and **next to**."]},{"text":"Between October 1972 and August 1973, she gave **more correct than incorrect responses for every preposition**.","children":["**In** had 37 correct and 4 incorrect. **On** had 36 correct and 9 incorrect.","**Over** had 11 correct and 2 incorrect. **Under** had 17 correct and 7 incorrect.","**In front of** had 20 correct and 6 incorrect. **In back of** had 7 correct and 3 incorrect.","**Next to** had 7 correct and 1 incorrect. **Behind** had 12 correct and 5 incorrect."]},{"text":"So **some language can be acquired late**. This learning took place after puberty, long after the window Lenneberg proposed. Her learning was uneven across different parts of language.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-16":[{"text":"Genie's **production lagged severely behind her comprehension**.","children":["**Some lag is typical of development**, because all children understand more than they can say.","Genie had also been **punished for making sounds** as a child, which could limit how much she spoke.","So her speech revealed less than she actually knew."]},{"text":"Her **grammatical abilities developed much more slowly than her vocabulary**. A test of **conjunctions** placed **five familiar objects in a row** in front of her. She was asked to point to one or more of them.","children":["The items included **“Show me the fork and pencil”**, **“Show me the crayon or the knife”** and **“Show me either the spoon or the crayon”**.","For **and**, the right response is to point to every named object.","For **or**, the right response is to point to just one, which depends on understanding a logical relationship."]},{"text":"Her responses changed over the testing, from October 1971 to December 1973.","children":["In **early responses**, she treated **or** sentences the same as **and** sentences, so she pointed to both objects.","In **later responses**, she told and from or apart, but she still did not understand **either/or**.","**And** sentences produced **100 correct**, 3 incorrect and 0 unanalyzable responses.","**Or** sentences produced only **7 correct**, **51 incorrect** and 6 unanalyzable responses."]},{"text":"So Genie understood and almost perfectly but failed or almost entirely, despite knowing hundreds of words. That is why her case fits the idea that **grammar depends more on early exposure than vocabulary does**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-17":[{"text":"Genie's case leaves **lots of questions** open.","children":["One question is whether Genie was **learning disabled**. She may have had difficulties from birth that would have affected her language even without deprivation.","Another question is what **residual impacts neglect and abuse** had on her social development. Severe trauma could affect learning in many ways beyond the missing language input."]},{"text":"**After four years, funding to study her was pulled**.","children":["So nobody can know what she might have learned after a longer time.","The evidence covers only what she learned within those four years."]},{"text":"Nonetheless, the **disparity between her vocabulary development and her grammatical development** is widely interpreted in one way. It is taken as suggesting that **grammar learning in a first language is specifically impaired after a sensitive period**.","children":["She learned hundreds of words but made little progress with grammar.","On this interpretation, vocabulary can be learned at any age, while grammar depends on early exposure."]},{"text":"Disability, abuse and late exposure could each have affected her grammar. One case cannot separate them. So her pattern stays suggestive, and it still became an influential piece of evidence for a sensitive period in first-language learning.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-18":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** studied deaf people who learned a first language late. Many deaf children **do not get sign language input** but have an **otherwise typical upbringing**.","children":["Unlike Genie, these children grow up in ordinary, caring homes.","Their only unusual experience is a lack of accessible language early in life. So they are a cleaner test of first-language timing."]},{"text":"Two groups of children learned **American Sign Language (ASL)** late.","children":["For children aged **9 to 15** learning a language for the **first time**, ASL was **difficult to learn**.","For a comparison group who **went deaf after first learning a spoken language**, ASL learning went **much better**.","Both groups learned ASL late, but only one group had an earlier language.","**These differences persist into adulthood**."]},{"text":"Two tasks measured the adult outcome.","children":["The first task was **recall of complex ASL sentences**. Deaf adults with **no early language** scored about **36%**. Deaf adults with an **early spoken language** scored about **78%**.","The second task was judging whether **complex English sentences were grammatically correct**, where chance is 50%. Deaf adults with **no early language** scored about **52%**, close to chance. Deaf adults with ASL in infancy scored about **80%**. Hearing adults with an early spoken language other than English also scored about 80%."]},{"text":"So the advantage comes from **having any language early**, whatever its type. Early ASL helped later English learning as much as an early spoken language did. That is why early accessible language, signed or spoken, is the foundation for learning languages later.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-19":[{"text":"The evidence bears on whether a first language (**L1**) has a **“critical period”** or a **“sensitive period”**. Three points come out of it.","children":[]},{"text":"**Recovery from stroke**, and certainly from **hemispherectomy**, looks **very different in childhood and adulthood**.","children":["Children can develop language after damage that would leave an adult with permanent aphasia.","So age strongly affects the brain's ability to reorganize for language."]},{"text":"**Deprivation of input does not appear to extend the critical period**.","children":["One might expect a child with no language input to keep the window open until input arrives.","Instead, Genie and the deaf adults who learned sign late have lasting difficulty, especially with grammar.","So the window seems to follow a maturational schedule, whether or not language has been experienced."]},{"text":"All of these data come from **highly unusual cases with lots of other things going on**.","children":["Stroke, brain surgery, abuse and neglect each bring complications beyond the timing of language input.","There is also a **lack of animal models**, because animals do not learn human language.","**Gene knockout experiments**, which disable specific genes to test their role, cannot be done with people."]},{"text":"That is why it is **difficult to reach firm conclusions**. The evidence supports age effects in first-language learning, but the exact nature of the window stays uncertain.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-20":[],"l1-critical-21":[],"l2-learning-01":[],"l2-learning-02":[{"text":"**Sensitive periods** for a second language (**L2**) are often taken to mean that infants, toddlers and children learn language better than adults. That idea should not be carried too far, because **babies are overrated** as language learners.","children":["A typical child by **18 months** uses at least **20 words**. The child also understands \"in and out\" and \"off and on\" and points to several body parts when asked.","The child answers simple questions such as \"Where's teddy?\" and \"What's that?\" with words or gestures. The child also has some pretend play, such as giving teddy a drink.","The child makes at least four different consonant sounds, such as b, n, d, g, w and h. The child enjoys being read to and points to pictures with one finger.","An adult who knew only about 20 words after 18 months of language instruction would count as a very poor learner."]},{"text":"**Adults initially learn more quickly** than children.","children":["One reason is **transfer from L1**, the first language. Adults build on what they already know about how language works.","The other reason is **better general cognitive skills**, such as memory, attention and reasoning."]},{"text":"**Adults do worse on ultimate attainment**, which is the final level of skill they eventually reach.","children":["Adults **rarely become native-like** in a second language.","That may also be due in part to transfer. The first language gives adults a fast start, but it can also keep them from native-like skill."]},{"text":"So early learning speed and final success are separate measures. Children and adults rank differently on each.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-03":[{"text":"Several toddler **language milestones** are really **general conceptual abilities**.","children":["Understanding \"in and out\" and \"off and on\" depends on the ability to deal with **spatial relations between objects**.","**Pretend play**, such as giving teddy a drink, depends on **event representations**. Event representations are mental representations of who does what in an event.","A toddler who meets these milestones is building new ideas about the world along with new words."]},{"text":"**Later learners of a second language** already have much more of this in place.","children":["An adult learning Spanish already has the concepts behind \"in\" and \"on\".","So the adult can focus on how these concepts are represented in the **target language**, which is the language being learned.","That is a smaller job than building concepts and words at the same time."]},{"text":"Later learners also **already know another language**. That knowledge affects how the second language is learned.","children":["The first language offers ready-made ways to express concepts.","Some of those ways fit the second language, so they help. Others clash with it, so they lead to errors."]},{"text":"So a later learner mainly has to learn how the new language expresses ideas the learner already has.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-04":[{"text":"**Transfer** is the use of native language (**L1**) processes and representations for a second language (**L2**).","children":["A learner brings L1 habits for hearing sounds, building sentences and finding words into the L2.","The learner comes to the L2 with a complete working language system already in place."]},{"text":"Transfer can have both **positive** and **negative** impacts on second-language learning and performance.","children":["**Positive transfer** happens when an L1 pattern matches what the L2 needs. Shared sounds, words and structures then give the learner a head start.","**Negative transfer** happens when an L1 pattern conflicts with the L2. Errors appear where the languages differ, because the learner's habits point the wrong way."]},{"text":"A cartoon gives a case of negative transfer in grammar. A man in a pub asks \"Do you not be happy with me as the translator of the books of you?\"","children":["The words are English, but the structure follows the grammar of another language.","Translating word by word carries over the first language's word order and phrasing."]},{"text":"So every habit built for the first language comes along into the second, whether it helps or hurts.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-05":[{"text":"**Transfer** affects **phonetic and phonological learning**, which is the learning of speech sounds.","children":["**Categorical perception** leads to **acquired similarity** for sounds in the same category.","Acquired similarity means that sounds a language groups into one category come to sound more alike with experience."]},{"text":"**Minimal pairs in Hindi** make the problem clear. A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in only one sound.","children":["Two Hindi words both sound like \"tap\" to an English speaker. One means hot, and the other means a kind of beat.","Two words both sound like \"dab\" to an English speaker. One means pressure, and the other means a kind of fruit.","Two words both sound like \"ari\" to an English speaker. One means saw (the tool), and the other means crooked.","In each pair, one word has a **dental** consonant, made with the tongue against the teeth. The other has a **retroflex** consonant, made with the tongue curled back toward the roof of the mouth."]},{"text":"Native English speakers tend to use the English **alveolar** category to interpret both dental and retroflex consonants.","children":["An alveolar consonant, such as English /t/ or /d/, is made with the tongue on the ridge behind the teeth.","English has only the alveolar category, so both Hindi sounds fall into it and seem the same."]},{"text":"So in this case **transfer makes learning the L2 contrast more difficult**.","children":["The English category was learned successfully, but it now hides a difference that Hindi uses to tell words apart.","The learner has to separate sounds that the first language taught them to treat as one."]}],"l2-learning-06":[{"text":"**Transfer** also affects **grammar learning**. **Classifiers** are a somewhat unusual grammatical feature across languages.","children":["A **classifier** is a word that must come between a number or demonstrative and a noun.","The classifier depends on the kind of thing the noun names.","The order is demonstrative pronoun, then nominal classifier, then noun. In **yí gè rén** (\"one person\"), gè is the classifier."]},{"text":"Chinese has many classifiers, and each one goes with a kind of noun.","children":["**Gè** is the general classifier. **Bǎ** goes with objects that have a handle. **Běn** goes with books and similar objects.","**Kuài** goes with objects that are divided into pieces. **Liàng** goes with wheeled vehicles. **Tái** goes with devices and gadgets.","**Xiē** marks an indeterminate amount, or goes with the plural of pronouns and demonstratives.","**Zhāng** goes with flat objects. **Zhī** goes with long, thin objects."]},{"text":"**Native English speakers learning Chinese have difficulty** learning classifiers.","children":["In English, saying \"a chair\", \"that dog\", \"the car\" or \"one book\" does not require much attention to noun meaning.","In Chinese, different nouns require different classifiers. So the learner must notice properties such as shape and function."]},{"text":"One way to think about this is that English speakers have **learned to ignore the cues** for classifiers as part of learning English grammar. That is why learning Chinese means paying attention again to cues that English taught them to disregard.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-07":[{"text":"**Grammatical gender** is another place where **transfer causes problems**. Grammatical gender sorts nouns into classes, often called masculine and feminine.","children":["Grammatical gender is different from social or cultural gender.","There is nothing \"male\" about a book or \"female\" about liberty. Yet French **le livre** (the book) is masculine, and **la liberté** (liberty) is feminine.","Other masculine nouns are le cheval (the horse), le chien (the dog) and le bruit (the noise).","Other feminine nouns are la colombe (the dove), la chemise (the shirt) and la maison (the house)."]},{"text":"Grammatical gender matters for **agreement**. Agreement means that related words must match the gender of the noun.","children":["Pronouns, adjectives and some forms of verbs all need to be coordinated with noun gender.","English nouns have no grammatical gender, so English speakers have no habit of tracking it."]},{"text":"French is famous for **synonyms with different genders**.","children":["**Auto** and **voiture** both mean car. One is masculine and the other is feminine.","**Rivière** and **fleuve** both mean river. Rivière is feminine and fleuve is masculine.","**Vélo** and **bicyclette** both mean bicycle. Vélo is masculine and bicyclette is feminine.","So gender has to be learned one word at a time."]},{"text":"**Word endings** give clues to gender.","children":["Masculine nouns often end in -age (le fromage, the cheese), -r (le professeur, the teacher), -t (le chat, the cat) or -isme (le capitalisme).","Feminine nouns often end in -ie (la boulangerie, the bakery), -ion (la nation), -ité or -tié (la fraternité, brotherhood) or -ance (la balance, the scales).","Feminine nouns also often end in -nne, -mme and -lle, as in la fille (the girl) and l’indienne."]}],"l2-learning-08":[{"text":"**Transfer is not all bad**. In some L1 and L2 situations, the similarity of words across languages helps **vocabulary learning**.","children":["French **auto** resembles English \"auto\" and means a car.","French **rivière** resembles English \"river\".","French **bicyclette** resembles English \"bicycle\".","Japanese **コロッケ (korokke)** means a croquette. Japanese **モノレール (monoreru)** means a monorail."]},{"text":"Sometimes the similarity comes from **shared etymology**. Etymology is the history of where a word comes from.","children":["Words with a shared origin are called **cognates**.","French rivière and English river both come from the same older word."]},{"text":"Sometimes the similarity comes from more recent **borrowing**.","children":["A **loan word** is a word that one language takes from another.","Japanese korokke and monoreru were borrowed from European languages. Their sounds were changed to fit Japanese."]},{"text":"Cognates and loan words give **positive transfer** for vocabulary.","children":["The learner already knows a similar form with a similar meaning.","A learner who knows river can link it to rivière right away. So the new word is easier to store and recall."]}],"l2-learning-09":[{"text":"Not all similar-sounding words are **cognates**. A **false friend** is a word that looks or sounds like a word in another language but means something different.","children":["Learners have a bias to interpret L2 words as related to similar-sounding L1 words.","That bias can be **very strong**, and sometimes the result is embarrassing."]},{"text":"The Spanish sentence \"Arnold está embarazado\" does not mean that Arnold is embarrassed.","children":["Spanish **embarazado** means **pregnant**.","Embarazado sounds like embarrassed, so an English speaker assumes it means embarrassed.","That speaker gets the meaning badly wrong."]},{"text":"The link to the familiar word forms before the learner checks the meaning.","children":["When the meanings match, the link speeds up learning, as with cognates.","When the meanings differ, the same link leads to errors."]},{"text":"So a helpful cognate and a misleading false friend differ only in whether the meanings really match.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-10":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** tested in the lab whether people use **cognates** in \"second language\" learning.","children":["They used **paired-associate learning** of made-up words. In paired-associate learning, people study pairs and later recall one member when given the other.","The participants were Dutch speakers. So Dutch stood in for the first language, and the made-up words stood in for a new second language."]},{"text":"The made-up words varied in two ways.","children":["They varied in their **similarity to their Dutch associates**. Some resembled the Dutch word, so they were cognates, and some did not.","They also varied in **concreteness**. A concrete word names something that can be seen or touched. An abstract word names an idea."]},{"text":"Crossing the two factors gave four kinds of pairs.","children":["The pair gitaar and gettar (\"guitar\") is concrete and cognate.","The pair tijger and duunze (\"tiger\") is concrete and non-cognate.","The pair domein and domaan (\"domain\") is abstract and cognate.","The pair herstel and groop (\"repair\") is abstract and non-cognate."]},{"text":"Participants were presented with pairs of Dutch words and their non-word associates and asked to learn the pairs.","children":["They were then tested **a week later on retention**. Retention is how well they remembered the pairs after a delay without study."]},{"text":"So the four kinds of pairs let the effect of each factor be measured separately.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-11":[{"text":"In **De Groot and Keijzer (2000)**, **cognates were learned more rapidly** and also **remembered more easily**.","children":["Recall was tested in three training sessions. It was tested again in session 4, after one week with no training."]},{"text":"Recall rose across the three training sessions for every kind of word, but the starting points differed.","children":["For **concrete cognates**, recall was 69.7% in session 1 and 93.4% by session 3.","For **concrete non-cognates**, recall was 43.7% in session 1 and 92.0% by session 3.","For **abstract cognates**, recall was 42.7% in session 1 and 86.0% by session 3.","For **abstract non-cognates**, recall was 19.0% in session 1 and 72.7% by session 3."]},{"text":"The week without training separated the kinds of words again.","children":["Concrete cognates dropped to 78.4%, and concrete non-cognates dropped to 69.0%.","Abstract cognates dropped to 68.4%, and abstract non-cognates fell sharply to **36.0%**.","Responses were also faster for cognates in every session."]},{"text":"So **cognate status** can contribute to the very earliest stages of word learning in a second language.","children":["The cognate advantage was largest in session 1, when learning had just begun. It reappeared after a week, when non-cognates were forgotten more.","**Concreteness** also had a large effect, and the two factors add together. Abstract non-cognates have neither source of support, so they were hardest.","One explanation for concreteness effects is **context availability**. It is the idea that concrete words are easier to link with a supporting context."]}],"l2-learning-12":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer** tested vocabulary in two directions, and that is what makes the experiment a good one.","children":["**Receptive** testing gives the made-up word and asks for the Dutch word.","**Expressive** testing, which the authors call **productive** testing, gives the Dutch word and asks for the made-up word."]},{"text":"A receptive test alone would not be very impressive.","children":["A cognate new word already resembles the Dutch word. So a participant could answer just by noticing the resemblance, without learning the pair.","The meanings of gitaar, tijger and domein could be guessed without any Dutch training. They resemble English guitar, tiger and domain."]},{"text":"The **concrete cognates** in the study are very close to their Dutch partners.","children":["Five pairs are broer and breur, brood and brodde, gitaar and gettar, jongen and jange, lepel and lippel.","Five more are lichaam and lechem, naald and nadel, paraplu and parredu, rivier and riffer, schaar and skare.","The last five are stier and stire, trein and trinen, varken and vark, viool and vialo, vrouw and vrauk.","Each made-up word is a slightly changed Dutch word."]},{"text":"Productive testing removes the shortcut.","children":["The learner must recall the exact new form, such as gettar, out of many similar possibilities.","So a cognate advantage in productive testing reflects **real learning of new forms**."]}],"l2-learning-13":[{"text":"The comparison of **productive** and **receptive** testing tells what participants actually learned. Recall was measured immediately after learning (session 3) and at retest a week later (session 4).","children":["The measure that matters is how much recall dropped over the week."]},{"text":"In **productive testing**, learners had to produce the new word.","children":["**Concrete** words fell from 92.7% to 73.7%, a drop of 19.0 points. **Abstract** words fell from 79.3% to 52.2%, a drop of 27.1 points.","**Cognates** fell from 89.7% to 73.3%, a drop of 16.4 points. **Non-cognates** fell from 82.3% to 52.5%, a drop of 29.8 points."]},{"text":"In **receptive testing**, learners had to give the Dutch word.","children":["Concrete words fell from 97.7% to 88.9%, a drop of 8.8 points. Abstract words fell from 91.5% to 64.9%, a drop of 26.6 points.","Cognates fell from 98.5% to 90.7%, a drop of 7.8 points. Non-cognates fell from 90.7% to 63.0%, a drop of 27.7 points.","Receptive scores were higher overall, because recognizing a familiar word is easier than producing a new one."]},{"text":"Cognates and concrete words were **forgotten less in both directions**.","children":["The cognate advantage held in productive testing, where the learner had to recall the new form itself.","So similarity to a known word helped learners form a **lasting memory** of the new word."]}],"l2-learning-14":[{"text":"A central research question is whether **similar L1 words are always activated** when a person processes L2. There have been many, many studies of this question.","children":["When a bilingual hears an L2 word, L1 words that sound similar might also become active.","That activation could happen automatically, even when the person is trying to use only the L2.","If it does, the two languages are never fully separate in the mind."]},{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** gave one clear example with a **visual world experiment**.","children":["In a visual world experiment, people hear spoken instructions while they look at a set of objects. Their eye movements are recorded.","Looks toward an object whose L1 name sounds like the spoken L2 word would mean the L1 word became active.","Eye movements reveal that activation without asking the person to report it. The person still goes on to choose the correct object."]},{"text":"So eye movements can answer whether L1 words are active during L2 listening.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-15":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** used the same kind of eye movement experiment to study categorical perception. They studied gradient effects of within-category **VOT** on **lexical access**.","children":["Participants saw a lamp, a ship, a bear and a pear. They heard a word from a continuum between \"bear\" and \"pear\".","Bear and pear differ only in their first consonant. The lamp and the ship are unrelated fillers."]},{"text":"**Eye movements** tell about **competition** between the possible responses that participants are considering.","children":["A participant can click on the bear after looking briefly at the pear.","Those looks mean that \"pear\" was active as a competitor."]},{"text":"The amount of competition between BEAR and PEAR depended on **distance from the category boundary**.","children":["Sounds closer to the boundary between /b/ and /p/ drew more looks to the competitor.","Sounds farther from the boundary drew fewer looks to the competitor."]},{"text":"So eye tracking measures how active an alternative word is, even when the final choice is correct. That is why the same method can ask whether a word in one language competes with a similar word in another.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-16":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","children":["Participants viewed a grid with pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","The display had a **beaker**, which was the target.","It had a **beetle**, which shares its beginning with beaker.","It had a **speaker**, which rhymes with beaker.","It had a **carriage**, which is unrelated."]},{"text":"The probability of looking at each object changed over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the target word began.","children":["Looks to the beaker and the beetle rose together early, because both fit the first sounds of the word.","Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 at around 500 milliseconds. They fell once the sounds that separate the two words arrived.","Looks to the beaker kept rising and approached 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller, peaking near 0.1. Its overlap comes at the end of the word.","Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"The **cohort effect** is this early competition from words that share a beginning with the target.","children":["Words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds.","They drop out when the input stops matching them."]},{"text":"So eye movements track the rise and fall of competition moment by moment.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-17":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey** examined the **cohort effect** with **cross-language competitors**. A cross-language competitor is an object whose name in the other language begins like the spoken target word.","children":[]},{"text":"They hypothesized that L1 words are always activated. If so, native Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the target is \"speaker\" in English.","children":["English **speaker** begins with the same sounds as Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches.","If Russian is active during English listening, the matches should draw looks."]},{"text":"For **English targets**, each target came with an English competitor and a Russian competitor.","children":["For speaker, they were spear and spichki (matches). For boot, they were book and buben (tambourine). For shark, they were shovel and sharik (balloon).","For chair, they were chess set and cherepaha (turtle). For marker, they were marbles and marka (stamp). For barbed wire, they were bark and barhat (velvet).","For plug, they were plum and platye (dress). For gun, they were gum and gaika (nut). For lock, they were lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish)."]},{"text":"For **Russian targets**, the roles were reversed.","children":["For spichki (matches), the competitors were English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles).","For marka (stamp), the competitors were English marker and Russian marlya (cheese cloth).","For platye (dress), the competitors were English plum and Russian plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The target and its cross-language competitor are tied only by **sound**.","children":["Speakers and matches share no meaning.","The overlap exists only for someone who knows Russian. So looks to the matches during an English task measure activation of spichki."]}],"l2-learning-18":[{"text":"Russian and English bilinguals first followed instructions in **English**. This tests **competition during L2 spoken word recognition**.","children":["The **between-language competitor** was an object with a similar Russian name.","The **within-language competitor** was an object with a similar English name."]},{"text":"The measure was the percent of trials with a look to each object.","children":["With only the between-language competitor present, participants looked at it on **18%** of trials.","With only the within-language competitor present, participants looked at it on **18%** of trials.","With both present, they looked at the between-language competitor on 13% of trials and the within-language competitor on 19%.","With no competitor present, they looked at a control filler object on only **7%** of trials."]},{"text":"When only one competitor was present, participants looked at within-language and between-language competitors **to the same extent**.","children":["The Russian name of the matches pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word did."]},{"text":"When both were present, participants looked at the within-language competitor more. They still looked at the between-language competitor a lot, about **twice as often as the control**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the **first language stays active** during second-language listening. Bilinguals still choose the correct object, but L1 words compete along the way.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-19":[{"text":"The same bilinguals then followed instructions in **Russian**. This tests **competition during L1 spoken word recognition**.","children":["Here the between-language competitor was an object with a similar **English** name."]},{"text":"The measure was again the percent of trials with a look to each object.","children":["With only the between-language competitor present, participants looked at it on **8%** of trials.","With only the within-language competitor present, participants looked at it on 14% of trials.","With both present, they looked at the between-language competitor on 8% of trials and the within-language competitor on 13%.","With no competitor present, they looked at the control filler on **5%** of trials."]},{"text":"**English words do not seem to be activated** when native Russian speakers do this experiment in Russian.","children":["The small numerical difference between the between-language and control conditions is **not statistically significant**.","A difference that is not statistically significant is within the range of chance variation.","The within-language competitor still drew extra looks, so the cohort effect appeared in Russian."]},{"text":"So the two experiments give an **asymmetric** result.","children":["Russian words became active during English tasks.","English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","That fits the idea that the first language is more strongly established and more easily activated than the second."]}],"l2-learning-20":[{"text":"**The Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**. Spreading activation means that activating one representation partly activates the representations connected to it.","children":["The model has an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a store of **concepts**, which are meanings.","**Lexical links** connect L1 words and L2 words directly.","**Conceptual links** connect each vocabulary to the concepts."]},{"text":"The **L1 lexicon** is assumed to be larger and more strongly connected to relevant concepts.","children":["For **L1 words**, the links to meanings are strong.","For **L2 words**, the links to meanings are weaker."]},{"text":"The **L2 lexicon** is assumed to have strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon.","children":["The lexical link from L2 to L1 is strong. The lexical link from L1 to L2 is weak.","So activation spreads more readily **from an L2 word to an L1 word** than the other way."]},{"text":"This happens because that direction is **emphasized in learning**.","children":["Learners usually learn each new L2 word by connecting it to its L1 translation. That builds a strong route from L2 words to L1 words.","The direct link from L2 words to concepts stays weaker, at least early in learning. It can grow stronger as proficiency rises."]}],"l2-learning-21":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains the Marian and Spivey results through **spreading activation** along links of different strength.","children":[]},{"text":"Consider the task in **L2**, with the English target \"speaker\".","children":["The concept for \"matches\" partially activates the Russian word for matches, spichki. This happens because L1 words are strongly connected to their concepts.","The sounds of English speaker also partly activate spichki, because the two words begin alike.","This activity feeds back to the conceptual activation for \"matches\".","Conceptual activation for \"matches\" leads to **eye movements toward the L1 competitor**, the matches."]},{"text":"Speaker and spichki are linked only by **sound overlap**.","children":["Speakers and matches share no meaning.","The extra activation of spichki strengthens the concept of matches, so the eyes are drawn to the matches."]},{"text":"Now consider the task in **L1**. The conceptual links with L2 might be **too weak** to create this kind of interference reliably.","children":["In the Russian task, an English competitor would need strong links from the English word to its concept.","In the model, L2 words have only weak conceptual links. Those links are too weak to drive looks to their objects."]},{"text":"That is why the effect appears in one direction and not the other.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-22":[{"text":"The Revised Hierarchical Model makes a **counter-intuitive prediction** even for **balanced bilinguals**. A balanced bilingual is a person who is about equally skilled in both languages.","children":["One might expect a balanced bilingual to work like \"two monolinguals in one\", with each language running separately.","The model predicts the opposite."]},{"text":"For a balanced bilingual, the **conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages**.","children":["Each concept strongly activates its word in both languages. So both vocabularies become active whenever either one is used.","**Competitive dynamics** will be engaged all the time. Competitive dynamics means the words of the two languages keep competing for selection.","**Inhibition** will also be engaged all the time. Inhibition is the suppression of the word from the language that is not in use."]},{"text":"This prediction has led to a lot of work on bilinguals' advantages in **non-linguistic cognitive control tasks**.","children":["**Cognitive control** is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","Bilinguals constantly practice selecting one language and suppressing the other. So they might become better at controlling attention in general, even on tasks unrelated to language."]}],"l2-learning-23":[{"text":"Second-language learning has so far been considered in one particular **sequence**.","children":["A person begins learning L1.","Later, the person learns L2.","The person also continues learning and using L1, so both languages stay in use."]},{"text":"A second sequence asks what happens when someone **stops using L1** after L2 is introduced.","children":["The person begins learning L1 and later learns L2, as before.","This time the person does not continue learning or using L1.","L1 is no longer heard or spoken, and L2 takes over."]},{"text":"The second sequence separates two things that usually go together. One is **learning a language early**, and the other is **continuing to use it**.","children":["If early learning leaves a permanent foundation, traces of L1 should remain even without use.","If continued use is needed, L1 might fade or disappear."]},{"text":"So comparing the two sequences tells whether early learning alone leaves a lasting mark.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-24":[{"text":"**Pallier, Dehaene, Poline, LeBihan, Argenti, Dupoux and Mehler** asked whether a **second language can replace the first**. Their study used brain imaging of language plasticity in adopted adults.","children":["They tested adults aged **20 to 32**.","These adults had been adopted into French homes from Korean orphanages between the ages of **3 and 8**.","They learned Korean as young children and then grew up in homes where only French was spoken.","They stopped using Korean entirely after adoption. So they are a natural case of L1 use stopping."]},{"text":"The researchers tested whether the adoptees had any **residual knowledge of Korean**. Residual knowledge is any remaining trace of the earlier language.","children":["Monolingual French speakers with no Korean experience served as the comparison group.","If the adoptees did better than the French speakers on Korean tasks, some Korean knowledge must have survived."]},{"text":"So any surviving Korean would reveal what early learning leaves behind once a language is no longer used.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-25":[{"text":"Korean has a **three-way stop contrast**. A **stop** is a consonant made by fully blocking the airflow and then releasing it, such as /p/, /t/ or /k/.","children":["English and French have two kinds of stop at each place in the mouth, such as /b/ and /p/.","Korean has three kinds at each place, which are **aspirated**, **plain** and **tense**.","An aspirated stop is released with a strong puff of air. A plain stop has a lighter release. A tense stop is made with a stiffened, tightened throat."]},{"text":"The contrast appears in three series, recorded in the UCLA Phonetics Archive.","children":["For the **bilabial** series, made with the lips, the words are pʰul (\"grass\"), pul (\"fire\") and p’ul (\"horn\").","For the **dental** series, made with the tongue at the teeth, the words are tʰal (\"mask\"), tal (\"moon\") and t’al (\"daughter\").","For the **velar** series, made with the back of the tongue, the words are kʰo (\"nose\"), kok (\"music\") and k’ok (\"exactly\")."]},{"text":"The three words in each series are made at the same place in the mouth. They differ only in the type of stop.","children":["French has no such contrast, so French speakers tend to hear the three Korean sounds as two.","Korean speakers hear three different sounds."]},{"text":"That is why the contrast is a good test of retained Korean. Adoptees who kept any Korean sound knowledge should hear these differences better than French speakers do.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-26":[{"text":"**Ventureyra and colleagues** found that **Korean adoptees in France were no better than monolingual French speakers** at the Korean contrasts.","children":["Listeners heard pairs of sounds and judged whether they were different.","Three groups were compared, which were adoptees, French native speakers and Korean native speakers."]},{"text":"Four conditions used Korean contrasts.","children":["For **plain and tense** p and k, Korean speakers said \"different\" on about 96% of trials. Adoptees did so on about 8% and French speakers on about 3%.","For **plain and aspirated** p and k, Korean speakers scored about 90%, adoptees about 19% and French speakers about 26%.","For **tense and aspirated** p and k, Korean speakers scored about 95%, adoptees about 23% and French speakers about 20%.","For **plain and tense** s, Korean speakers scored about 79%, adoptees about 26% and French speakers about 23%."]},{"text":"Two control conditions checked general performance.","children":["For a **vowel** contrast, all three groups said \"different\" on about 97 to 99% of trials.","For pairs of **identical** sounds, all groups rarely said \"different\", at about 3 to 13%."]},{"text":"So the adoptees could do the task. The difficulty was specific to the Korean stop contrasts.","children":["On those contrasts, the adoptees heard the differences at the same level as people who had never heard Korean.","Their early Korean experience left **no measurable advantage** for these sounds."]}],"l2-learning-27":[{"text":"In a more extensive study, **Pallier and colleagues** found that **adoptees were no better than monolingual French speakers** on three tasks. Each task tested a different kind of knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"The first task was distinguishing Korean from a collection of largely unrelated languages. It tested familiarity with the overall sound of Korean.","children":["Participants rated from 1 to 7 how much each sentence sounded like Korean.","Both groups rated Korean sentences around 4, and they rated Japanese sentences around 4 to 5.","Polish sentences got about 2, and Swedish and Wolof sentences got about 2.5 to 3.","Adoptees did not pick out Korean any better than French speakers did."]},{"text":"The second task was identifying the correct Korean translation of French words. It tested memory for vocabulary.","children":["Adoptees scored about 55% correct and French speakers about 50%. Both scores are close to chance."]},{"text":"The third task was deciding whether a short excerpt was repeated from a recorded sentence just heard. It tested sensitivity to sound sequences.","children":["For French sentences, both groups were right about 95% of the time.","For Korean, Japanese and Polish sentences, both groups scored about 80%. Adoptees had no advantage for Korean."]},{"text":"These are not standard psycholinguistic tasks. Pallier and colleagues were looking for **any remaining trace of Korean**, and they **did not find any**.","children":["The tasks were designed to catch even weak knowledge.","On every measure, the adoptees looked like people who had never learned Korean."]},{"text":"So the Korean the adoptees heard early in life left no measurable trace on these tasks.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-28":[{"text":"Pallier and colleagues also used **brain imaging**. In both groups, listening to French activated the **classical language network**, including **superior temporal** and **inferior frontal** regions.","children":["The superior temporal region is the upper part of the temporal lobe. It is involved in processing speech sounds.","The inferior frontal region is the lower part of the frontal lobe. It includes Broca's area."]},{"text":"The comparisons subtracted brain activity for another language from activity for French.","children":["The three comparisons were French minus Polish, French minus Japanese and French minus Korean.","Each was computed for the French group and for the Korean group, the adoptees.","In every comparison, French produced more activity in the language network than the foreign language did."]},{"text":"**Subtracting Korean from French** might have revealed a difference between the groups, if any residual processing ability for Korean were left.","children":["Suppose adoptees still processed Korean as a language. Then Korean would activate their language network more than it did for French speakers.","The French minus Korean difference would then be smaller in adoptees.","But the subtraction revealed **no difference** between the groups."]},{"text":"So the adoptees' brains responded to Korean like a foreign language, much as they responded to Polish and Japanese. The imaging agrees with the behavioral tests.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-29":[{"text":"Interactions between L2 and L1 can also be thought of in terms of **competitive dynamics**.","children":["Imagine that L1 is represented as a set of **connections** within and between different brain regions.","These connections are built and maintained through use."]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation** in vision, where one eye is deprived, follows this kind of competition. The same process could apply to language.","children":["In mice, after 2 to 3 days, the deprived eye's connections have **increased spine motility**.","After 3 to 4 days, there is **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration**. Wallerian degeneration is the breakdown of cut-off nerve fibers.","After more than a week, the other eye's fibers have **axon sprouting and new spine birth**. They take over the lost space.","So the eye that keeps receiving input wins the connections that the deprived eye loses."]},{"text":"If the L1 connections are never activated again, they could be **\"taken over\" by L2**.","children":["L2 is used constantly, so its connections strengthen and spread.","L1 is not used, so its connections weaken and can be claimed by L2."]},{"text":"So on this account, L2 replaces L1 by winning a competition for the same neural resources.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-30":[{"text":"A second language can **apparently replace** the first.","children":["The adoptees had no behavioral or brain sign of their childhood Korean.","As far as these tests could measure, French had completely replaced Korean."]},{"text":"The remaining question is whether **traces of L1** are left behind that can be **\"salvaged\"**.","children":["Salvaging a trace means recovering or using some leftover effect of the earlier language.","A trace might not appear on a test of current ability. It could still make relearning easier."]},{"text":"A test of current recognition and a test of later learning ask different questions.","children":["The first asks what the person can do with the language now.","The second asks whether new learning benefits from something left over from the past."]},{"text":"That is why **relearning** is the measure that could reveal such a trace.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-31":[{"text":"**Terry Au and colleagues** at UCLA examined whether traces can be salvaged. The paper is \"Overhearing a language during childhood\" by **Au, Knightly, Jun and Oh**.","children":["They studied undergraduates who **overheard Spanish** frequently as children but never learned to speak it.","All participants started classroom Spanish at around age 14. They were tested at the end of a second-year college Spanish course.","The overhearers were compared with **native speakers** and **typical late L2 learners**."]},{"text":"On **pronunciation**, the overhearers were far ahead of the late learners.","children":["For **voice onset time** in word-initial voiceless stops, native speakers had 19.3 milliseconds and overhearers had 19.6. Late learners had 36.2, a longer and English-like value.","For word-medial voiceless stops, the three values were 22.4, 23.4 and 31.2.","**Lenition** is a softening of a consonant, which Spanish applies to voiced stops between vowels. Word-initial voiced stops were lenited 43% by natives, 24% by overhearers and 1% by late learners.","Word-medial voiced stops were lenited 72%, 63% and 23%.","For **accent rating** on a 5-point scale, voiceless stops scored 4.3, 3.5 and 2.2. Voiced stops scored 4.4, 3.3 and 2.7."]},{"text":"On childhood slang, overhearers knew some that late learners did not.","children":["Production was 79% for natives, 17% for overhearers and 3% for late learners. Comprehension was 88%, 22% and 3%."]},{"text":"On **grammar**, the overhearers had little or no advantage.","children":["Gender agreement was 93% for natives, 70% for overhearers and 68% for late learners. Number agreement was 92%, 88% and 85%.","Grammaticality judgment accuracy was 94%, 64% and 63%. Reaction time was 1,042, 2,475 and 2,209 milliseconds."]},{"text":"So childhood overhearing helped **pronunciation but not grammar**. Early listening left traces in the sound system that were unmasked by later study.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-32":[{"text":"The data from **Korean adoptees** and the data from **overhearers** can be taken together.","children":["If L1 is removed from the environment entirely, L2 can **appear to completely replace it**. The adoptees had no trace of Korean on any test of current ability.","But this replacement is **not necessarily complete**. There may be some **residual knowledge** that can be \"unmasked\" with later learning.","The Spanish overhearers' pronunciation is the case of unmasking."]},{"text":"The two populations also differ in potentially important ways.","children":["The adoptees were tested on what they knew, with no new Korean instruction.","The overhearers were tested at the end of a second-year college Spanish course. That gave hidden knowledge a chance to surface."]},{"text":"So complete loss of exposure can make a first language seem to vanish, while traces may still remain. These and other differences make the two sets of results hard to compare directly.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-33":[{"text":"L2 can appear to completely replace an L1 that is removed from the environment, but residual knowledge may be unmasked later. Several **differences between the two populations** could explain why overhearers kept traces and adoptees did not.","children":["Korean is **rarely heard** or encountered in France, but Spanish is common in Los Angeles. So overhearers may have kept hearing Spanish throughout their lives.","A **heritage language** spoken by other family members differs from a language not spoken by anyone you know. Overhearers often heard Spanish from relatives, while adoptees lost all contact with Korean speakers.","**Cognates** are common between Spanish and English but rare between Korean and French. Shared vocabulary could help overhearers relearn.","A **shared writing system** is another difference. Spanish and English use the same alphabet, while Korean uses a different script from French."]},{"text":"This is a **general issue** with work on second language acquisition and bilingualism.","children":["Languages and learning situations differ in many, many ways that are hard, perhaps impossible, to control experimentally.","Researchers cannot assign people to grow up with particular languages in particular settings."]},{"text":"So separating these factors would take groups that differ in only one way. Real language histories rarely provide that.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-01":[],"l2-sensitive-02":[{"text":"**Second-language (L2) learning** works as a **model system** for research on the sensitive period. A model system is a common, easily studied situation that answers questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** proposed that the sensitive period comes from the development of **cerebral dominance**.","children":["**Cerebral dominance** means one hemisphere, usually the left, becomes specialized for language.","He also described it as developmental change in **language laterality**. Language laterality is how much language relies on one side of the brain instead of both.","On this theory, the window for learning language closes once language is fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence motivated the theory.","children":["The first was a rough alignment of **brain development** with **language milestones**. Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off. No measurements existed between ages 2 and 18. Lenneberg linked the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","The second was a review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery**. Children who lost the left hemisphere often developed language. Adults did not recover it."]},{"text":"So the theory is about the brain's **maturational state**, which is its stage of physical development.","children":["A young, less specialized brain can shift language to other tissue.","A mature, specialized brain has fewer options, so language learning becomes harder."]}],"l2-sensitive-03":[{"text":"Research on **first-language (L1) sensitive periods** relies mostly on **idiosyncratic cases** or relatively rare phenomena. Idiosyncratic means particular to one individual.","children":["One kind of case is children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere.","Another case is Genie, who was isolated from language until age 13.","Each case comes with other unusual circumstances, such as injury, illness or abuse. So it is hard to tell which factor caused the outcome."]},{"text":"**Second-language (L2) learning** gives a much larger comparison. Many people learn second languages at different times in their lives, often with somewhat **limited success**.","children":["L2 learners are common, so researchers can study large groups instead of single rare cases.","They start at every age from early childhood to late adulthood.","Their final success can be measured."]},{"text":"The **limits of L2 success** are used to reason about the **timing** of the sensitive or critical period.","children":["If later starters reliably reach lower levels, the age where the decline appears could mark when the sensitive period closes.","The **first language** complicates the comparison. Every L2 learner already knows a language, and that knowledge shapes how the new one is learned."]},{"text":"That is why L2 learning is used as a model system for sensitive period research.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-04":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested whether **critical period effects** reach **second-language learning**. Their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on the acquisition of English as a second language.","children":["**Lenneberg (1967)** hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period**. That period runs from early infancy until puberty.","In its basic form, the critical period hypothesis only has consequences for first-language acquisition.","If it also extends to second-language acquisition, young children should be better second-language learners than adults. They should also reach higher final proficiency."]},{"text":"They compared the **English proficiency** of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers**.","children":["The speakers had arrived in the United States between the ages of **3 and 39**.","By the time of testing, they had lived in the United States between 3 and 26 years.","A **grammaticality judgment task** tested a wide variety of structures of English grammar."]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage** over later arrivals.","children":["**Up to puberty**, test performance was **linearly related** to age of arrival. Each extra year of age at arrival meant a lower score.","**After puberty**, performance was **low but highly variable** and unrelated to age of arrival."]},{"text":"The age effect was not an accidental result of other differences between people.","children":["It did not come from amount of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","It appeared on **every grammatical structure tested**, although later learners mastered some structures far better than others."]},{"text":"So Johnson and Newport concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-05":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** devised a **grammaticality judgment test**, or **GJT**. A grammaticality judgment is a decision about whether a sentence follows the rules of the language.","children":["Participants listened to recorded sentences and said whether each sentence was grammatical or not.","The test was **untimed**, so participants could take as long as they needed."]},{"text":"Each item had a grammatical version and an ungrammatical version, and each pair tests **one rule of English**.","children":["**(1a) “The farmer bought two pigs at the market”** and **(1b) “The farmer bought two pig at the market”** test **plural marking** on nouns.","**(2a) “The little boy is speaking to a policeman”** and **(2b) “The little boy is speak to a policeman”** test the **progressive -ing** form of the verb."]},{"text":"Several pairs test articles and other **determiners**.","children":["**(6a) “Tom is reading a book in the bathtub”** and **(6b) “Tom is reading book in the bathtub”** test whether a needed article is present.","**(7a) “The boys are going to the zoo this Saturday”** and **(7b) “A boys are going to the zoo this Saturday”** test whether determiner and noun agree in number.","**(8a) “Larry went home after the party”** and **(8b) “Larry went the home after the party”** test a spot where English does not allow an article."]},{"text":"Other pairs test how **English questions** are built.","children":["**(23a) “When will Sam fix his car?”** and **(23b) “When Sam will fix his car?”** test **wh-question** word order, where the helping verb comes before the subject.","**(24a) “What do they sell at the corner store?”** and **(24b) “What they sell at the corner store?”** test the need for the helping verb “do” in a question."]},{"text":"So the test covers many different rules of English grammar, with one rule for each pair.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-06":[{"text":"The data relate each participant's score on the grammar test to **age of arrival**, or **AOA**. Each participant is one point, and the score is items correct out of 276.","children":["The participants are split at **puberty**, which is the end of the critical period in Lenneberg's proposal. Splitting there tests whether the age effect changes after puberty.","A **correlation**, written r, measures how closely two variables are related, from −1 to +1. A value near −1 means one variable falls steadily as the other rises. A value near 0 means little relationship."]},{"text":"For **early arrivals**, aged 3 to 15, the correlation was **−0.87**.","children":["Children who arrived between about 3 and 7 scored near the maximum, around 270.","Scores fell steadily with each later year of arrival, to around 210 to 230 near age 15.","The strong negative correlation means age of arrival **predicts much of the score** in this group."]},{"text":"For **late arrivals**, aged 17 to 39, the correlation was only **−0.16**.","children":["Scores were lower overall and widely scattered, from about 160 to 250.","Some adults scored around 250, while others of similar age scored below 180.","The weak correlation means age of arrival **predicts little** about which adults do well."]},{"text":"So the pattern has two parts, a **strong age effect in childhood** and **lower, highly variable performance in adulthood**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-07":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features.","children":["There is a **strong relationship between AOA and accuracy**. AOA is age of arrival, and earlier arrival goes with higher scores.","**Early learners**, up to about age 8, are often **near-perfect**, which means **native-like**. Their scores sit close to those of native English speakers.","**Late arrivals** have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy**. Among adults, age of arrival barely predicts the score."]},{"text":"The first two features describe learners who arrived as children.","children":["For the **youngest arrivals**, grammar reaches a native-like level.","For **arrivals between about 8 and 15**, scores are progressively lower. So the decline starts well before puberty.","For **adult arrivals**, scores are lower still, with wide differences between individuals."]},{"text":"A “nearly random distribution” has a specific meaning here.","children":["It means that age of arrival and score are only weakly related among late arrivals.","It does not mean guessing, because late arrivals scored well above chance.","Individual scores varied greatly, so two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]},{"text":"That is why near-perfect scores for the youngest arrivals are the clearest sign of an early advantage.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-08":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** argue that their data are consistent with a **sensitive period** for L2 learning. They themselves call it a **“critical period.”** Their argument has two parts.","children":[]},{"text":"First, **ultimate attainment** depends on AOA. Ultimate attainment is the final level a learner reaches.","children":["Scores fall steadily as age of arrival in childhood rises. That is consistent with a **loss of language-learning ability over development**.","The timing is **not entirely consistent with Lenneberg's hypothesis**. Lenneberg put the end of the critical period at puberty, but the decline here starts around age 7 or 8.","Lenneberg was working with a large gap in the data between early childhood and adolescence. He had no brain measurements between ages 2 and 18, so his timing was a rough estimate."]},{"text":"Second, attainment in late acquisition is **all over the place**. Adults who arrived at the same age reached very different levels.","children":["After the sensitive period closes, attainment depends on **idiosyncratic learning abilities**, not on the usual language acquisition processes.","Idiosyncratic learning abilities are individual differences in things such as memory, motivation or analytical skill.","**Children** all learn through the same natural acquisition process, so they reach similar levels at similar ages.","**Adults** no longer have that process, so they rely on their own abilities, which vary greatly between people."]},{"text":"So a steady decline in childhood and scattered adult scores both fit a sensitive period that closes.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-09":[{"text":"The **“critical period” hypothesis** comes in **two versions**. Both start from the same claim that early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The **exercise hypothesis** says the capacity must be used early to be kept.","children":["If the capacity is **not exercised** during this time, it will disappear or decline with maturation.","If the capacity **is exercised**, further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life.","So an adult who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child does."]},{"text":"The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used.","children":["So even a person with a normal L1 should learn an L2 less well as an adult."]},{"text":"Johnson and Newport (1989) see the **maturational state hypothesis** as more consistent with their data.","children":["All of their participants had a normal first language, so all had exercised the capacity.","Later starters still learned the L2 less well.","That result fits the maturational state hypothesis, which is the idea named in the title of their paper."]},{"text":"This changes how the **L1 critical period data** should be read.","children":["In cases like Genie, the capacity was never exercised. Both hypotheses predict poor learning for such cases, so those cases cannot separate them.","Only **second-language data** can separate the two versions. That is because only L2 learners exercised the capacity before learning another language."]}],"l2-sensitive-10":[{"text":"**Test design** raises a methodological issue for the grammaticality judgment task. The task is **very different from fluent language use**.","children":["In fluent use, people speak and understand quickly without stopping to think about rules.","In a grammaticality judgment task, people consider each sentence and decide whether it is correct."]},{"text":"One worry is the use of **“metalinguistic”** or **“explicit” knowledge**. **Metalinguistic knowledge** is conscious knowledge about language, such as knowing that English plurals add “-s.”","children":[]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** contrasts **implicit knowledge** and **explicit knowledge** on several characteristics.","children":["For **awareness**, implicit knowledge is intuitive awareness of linguistic norms. Explicit knowledge is conscious awareness.","For **type of knowledge**, implicit knowledge is **procedural**, which means knowing how to do something. Explicit knowledge is **declarative**, which means knowing facts that can be stated.","For **systematicity**, implicit knowledge is variable but systematic. Explicit knowledge is often anomalous and inconsistent.","For **accessibility**, implicit knowledge is reached through **automatic processing**. Explicit knowledge is reached through **controlled processing**, which takes effort.","For **use of L2 knowledge**, implicit knowledge is used during fluent performance. Explicit knowledge is used when a speaker is planning and having difficulty.","For **self-report**, implicit knowledge is **nonverbalizable**, so people cannot explain it. Explicit knowledge is **verbalizable**.","For **learnability**, implicit knowledge is potentially only learnable **within the critical period**. Explicit knowledge is learnable at **any age**."]},{"text":"So an **untimed GJT** may let people use explicit knowledge.","children":["A learner who consciously applies a taught rule gets the same score as one who knows the rule implicitly.","That is why the test may miss the implicit knowledge that the critical period is supposed to affect."]}],"l2-sensitive-11":[{"text":"One suggestion is that the **untimed GJT** measures explicit learning of **“metalinguistic” knowledge** better than it measures grammar acquisition. The reason is the task demands of the test.","children":[]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** compared the design features of five tests.","children":["**Imitation** and **oral narrative** rely on feel, are done under time pressure and focus on meaning. They require no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **timed GJT** relies on feel and is done under time pressure, but it focuses on form. It requires no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **untimed GJT** relies on rules, has no time pressure and focuses on form. It does allow metalinguistic knowledge.","The **metalanguage test** asks people to state rules. It relies on rules, has no time pressure, focuses on form and requires metalinguistic knowledge."]},{"text":"Ellis then related each test to **starting age** and to **years of formal instruction**. Only two relationships were significant.","children":["For the **timed GJT**, starting age had a correlation of **−0.23**.","For the **untimed GJT with ungrammatical items**, years of formal instruction had a correlation of **0.27**.","The other tests had only weak relationships with either measure."]},{"text":"Consistent with this, untimed GJT performance is correlated with **years of formal education in L2**.","children":["Formal classroom instruction teaches explicit rules, and explicit rules help on the untimed test.","Starting age matters for the timed test, which limits the use of explicit rules."]},{"text":"So an age effect found with an untimed GJT may partly reflect **explicit, taught knowledge** instead of natural grammar acquisition.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-12":[{"text":"**Transfer** is the use of first-language knowledge in learning a second language. It raises another methodological and theoretical issue for the age findings.","children":["Languages that are **typologically more similar** can benefit more from transfer. **Typological similarity** is similarity in how languages are structured, such as word order and word forms.","Cognates and loan words give learners familiar words to build on. The examples are French **auto**, **rivière** and **bicyclette**, and Japanese **korokke** and **monoreru**."]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested people with **English as L2** and **Chinese or Korean as L1**.","children":["Chinese and Korean are very different from English in grammar and vocabulary.","So these learners got little help from transfer."]},{"text":"If the languages were more similar, transfer would help much more.","children":["A learner whose L1 shares grammar and vocabulary with English can reuse much of what they already know.","Those learners might have a different relationship between age and attainment."]},{"text":"So the age pattern in Johnson and Newport's data may depend on the **particular languages involved** as well as on age.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-13":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** compared errors on **12 types of English rules**. They compared native speakers with five arrival groups. The groups arrived at ages 3 to 7, 8 to 10, 11 to 15, 17 to 24 and 25 to 39.","children":["The rule types are **determiners**, **plurals**, **verb subcategorization**, **past tense**, **pronouns**, **particles**, **auxiliaries**, **third-person singular**, **wh-questions**, **yes/no questions**, **word order** and **progressive -ing**.","**Verb subcategorization** is the set of rules for which kinds of words or phrases can follow a verb."]},{"text":"Not all aspects of the grammar are **equally difficult**.","children":["For **native speakers and the youngest arrivals**, errors are very few on every rule.","For **the latest arrivals**, errors are highest on determiners, around 65 to 68%, and on plurals, around 45 to 55%.","For **every group**, errors on word order and progressive -ing stay low, under about 10%."]},{"text":"Some of the pattern looks like the **order in which children learn language**.","children":["Progressive -ing and word order are easiest for late learners. They are also among the first structures children master."]},{"text":"Some of the pattern could be **transfer**.","children":["**Chinese has no determiners**, so Chinese speakers have no L1 category for “a” and “the.” That makes English articles especially hard for them.","Questions do not follow the child order. Children learn questions very late, but questions are among the **easiest structures for late learners**.","Adults may use general cognitive skills or L1 knowledge when they learn question forms."]},{"text":"So how hard a structure is depends on the structure itself and on what the learner's first language provides.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-14":[{"text":"**Birdsong and Molis (2001)** repeated the Johnson and Newport study with **Spanish speakers learning English**. The comparison is between two language pairs.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Chinese and Korean speakers**, the age effect was in childhood.","children":["Early arrivals, aged 3 to 15, had a strong correlation with age of arrival, **−0.87**.","Late arrivals, aged 17 to 39, had a weak correlation, **−0.16**."]},{"text":"For **Spanish speakers**, the pattern was almost the reverse.","children":["Early arrivals, aged 3 to 16, had only a weak correlation, **−0.23**. They scored near the maximum regardless of age, with only a slight decline for those arriving around 16.","Late arrivals, aged 17 to 44, had a strong correlation, **−0.69**. Scores fell from around 260 for those arriving near 18 to around 160 for those arriving after 40."]},{"text":"The results are a problem for the idea that AOA effects on GJT performance reflect **maturational constraints**. Maturational constraints are limits on learning caused by the brain's development.","children":["If a fixed maturational schedule controlled learning, the pattern should look the same for every first language.","Instead, the pattern changed with the learners' first language.","Spanish is much closer to English than Chinese or Korean is. So **transfer** may help Spanish speakers reach native-like grammar even when they arrive late in childhood."]},{"text":"So the age effect depends on the **language pairing** as well as on age.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-15":[{"text":"These findings may not even count as **“conclusions.”** One thing holds across studies. Overall, **L2 attainment is lower for older learners**.","children":[]},{"text":"How much lower depends on three things.","children":["It depends on **what constructions are tested**. Some structures, such as determiners, are much harder for late learners than others, such as word order.","It depends on **how they are tested**. Timed and untimed tests can give different results, because the untimed test allows explicit knowledge.","It depends on **language pairings**. Spanish speakers and Chinese or Korean speakers have different age patterns when learning English."]},{"text":"Three pairs of factors are **hard to dissociate**, which means hard to separate.","children":["**Age of acquisition** is hard to separate from the **amount and quality of experience**. People who arrive younger usually have more years of exposure and more immersion, such as schooling in the language.","**Transfer effects** are hard to separate from **L2 acquisition**. L2 performance reflects what was learned and also what carries over from the L1.","**Performance** is hard to separate from **competence**, generally speaking. Competence is underlying knowledge of a language. Performance is how well that knowledge is used on a particular task. A test score measures performance, so it may not reveal competence directly."]},{"text":"So the age effect in second-language learning is real, but it depends on many factors that are hard to separate.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-16":[],"review-54":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between **L1** and **L2** in terms of **spreading activation**. Spreading activation means that activating one representation partly activates the representations connected to it.","children":["The model connects an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a shared store of concepts.","**Lexical links** join words across the two languages. **Conceptual links** join each vocabulary to meanings.","L1 words have strong links to concepts. L2 words have weaker links to concepts."]},{"text":"Consider the **Marian and Spivey** task in **L2**. Russian and English bilinguals hear an English instruction to pick up the **speaker**.","children":["The concept for \"speaker\" partially activates the Russian word for \"matches\", which is **spichki**.","Speaker and spichki are linked only by **sound overlap**. They begin with the same sounds, but speakers and matches share no meaning.","The activity of spichki feeds back to the conceptual activation for \"matches\". This happens because L1 words are strongly linked to their concepts.","Conceptual activation for \"matches\" leads to **eye movements toward the L1 competitor**, the matches. The listener still picks up the speaker."]},{"text":"Now consider the task done in **L1**. The conceptual links with L2 might be **too weak** to create this kind of interference reliably.","children":["An English competitor would need a strong link from its English name to its concept to draw the eyes during a Russian task.","L2 words have only weak conceptual links in this model."]},{"text":"So interference runs from the first language into the second but not the other way.","children":[]}],"review-1":[],"review-2":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** is a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property. This continuum **keeps F1 constant** and **varies the F2 onset frequency linearly** from stimulus 1 to stimulus 13.","children":["The **F2 onset frequency** is the frequency at which the second formant begins its transition.","Keeping F1 constant removes one possible difference between stimuli.","Varying the F2 onset linearly means it changes by the same step every time. So **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"The F2 transition shifts gradually along the series.","children":["**For the early stimuli**, F2 starts well below the vowel frequency, so the transition rises.","**For the middle stimuli**, the onset is higher, so the rise shrinks until the transition is nearly flat.","**For the later stimuli**, F2 starts above the vowel frequency, so the transition falls."]},{"text":"Every step is physically equal, so the continuum is a fair test of whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories.","children":["Some neighboring pairs fall inside one consonant category, and other pairs straddle a boundary.","A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size.","If perception is categorical, steps within a category will sound alike and steps across a boundary will sound different.","So any advantage for boundary pairs comes from the boundary itself."]}],"review-3":[{"text":"An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond if perception worked according to one particular account. This simple version assumes that perception is **non-categorical**.","children":["Non-categorical perception tracks the acoustic signal gradually, with no sharp category boundaries.","The model is a baseline. Real data can be compared against it to see whether perception is more categorical than a gradual account allows."]},{"text":"The first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**. A **prototype** is the clearest example of a category.","children":["The endpoints of the continuum are /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","Between these points, **stimulus ambiguity should scale with the distance from the prototype**.","A stimulus farther from the /b/ prototype sounds less like /b/ and more like /d/.","**Stimulus 2** would be labeled **75% /b/ and 25% /d/**. It sits a quarter of the way from the /b/ prototype toward the /d/ prototype."]},{"text":"So under this account, **labeling** changes smoothly.","children":["Responses for one category fade out in a straight slope while responses for the neighboring category fade in.","The /b/ responses reach 0 at stimulus 6, where the /d/ responses peak at 1. The /d/ responses fall back to 0 at stimulus 10, where the /g/ responses reach 1.","Two neighboring labels are equally likely halfway between their prototypes. No label changes in a sudden step."]}],"review-4":[{"text":"The same simple **ideal observer** can assume that perception is **linear**. Linear perception means equal acoustic differences are perceived as equally large. This leads to a second prediction, that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/.","**Discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are the **same or different**.","These are separate tasks, even when they use the same continuum."]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**.","children":["There are three categories, and listeners are choosing between labels.","So each category’s responses rise toward its prototype and fall away from it.","These peaks come from the structure of the labeling task itself."]},{"text":"Discrimination works differently under the linear account. Pairs are compared two steps apart, from **1 and 3** through **9 and 11**. Every pair has the same physical separation.","children":["If perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, discrimination accuracy would also be constant.","So accuracy would stay level for every pair, at about 0.8, even where labeling changes.","A listener can be unsure which label to choose and still hear that two sounds differ."]},{"text":"So the two possible outcomes mean different things. A **flat** pattern would mean category boundaries play no role in hearing differences. A **peaked** pattern would mean the boundaries make some differences easier to hear.","children":[]}],"review-5":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** measured actual labeling and discrimination along the /b/, /d/ and /g/ continuum. Their results departed sharply from the non-categorical, linear predictions, and the pattern is called **categorical perception**.","children":["Listeners labeled each stimulus.","Listeners also judged pairs of stimuli that were **one step**, **two steps** or **three steps** apart."]},{"text":"Labeling changed **sharply** from one consonant to the next.","children":["**Stimuli 1 through about 3** were labeled /b/ close to 100% of the time.","**Around stimuli 4 and 5**, /b/ responses dropped steeply and /d/ responses climbed to nearly 100%. /d/ stayed dominant through about stimulus 8.","**Around stimuli 9 and 10**, /d/ responses dropped steeply and /g/ responses rose to near 100%.","These switches are far more abrupt than the gradual slopes the ideal observer predicted. The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**.","children":["Accuracy peaked at the /b/ to /d/ boundary and again at the /d/ to /g/ boundary.","Pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance**, which is the level expected from guessing.","Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries."]},{"text":"Liberman and colleagues also predicted discrimination from the labeling data. They assumed listeners can tell two sounds apart only when they give them different labels.","children":["The predictions captured much of the real pattern.","Real accuracy was often a little higher than predicted, especially within categories.","So listeners keep some sensitivity to acoustic differences beyond the category labels alone."]}],"review-6":[{"text":"The bear and pear eye-tracking result is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing** in speech perception. A **thresholded model** has a hard time explaining it.","children":["The number of looks to the other member of the **minimal pair** increases gradually toward the category boundary.","A minimal pair is two words that differ in only one sound, such as bear and pear.","The increase holds even when the final response is the same. So it comes from graded activation within one category."]},{"text":"**In a thresholded model**, graded feature activity never reaches the phoneme level.","children":["Feature activation keeps swinging between +voiced and −voiced.","Phoneme activation stays flat until /b/ suddenly switches on.","So the phoneme level cannot know how close a sound came to the boundary.","Every token in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","Word-level competition would then be the same for every /b/. Nothing would produce gradually changing competitor looks."]},{"text":"**In a cascaded model**, graded information reaches the word level.","children":["More support for the competing phoneme passes along more support for its word."]},{"text":"That is why the gradual rise in competitor looks favors cascaded processing.","children":[]}],"review-7":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also includes **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**. Activity runs up from phonemes to words and back down from words to phonemes.","children":["**Lexical feedback** means that an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","An uncertain sound can partly activate a word. That word can then strengthen the reading of the sound that fits it."]},{"text":"So influence runs in both directions.","children":["Acoustic evidence changes which words are active.","Active words change the balance between possible phonemes while the phoneme decision is still forming.","This helps listeners recover from noisy or ambiguous input."]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**.","children":["**Cascading** passes incomplete evidence forward, from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words.","**Feedback** sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","An interactive model can include both."]},{"text":"Some experiments motivate adding this kind of feedback. In them, knowledge of words changes how sounds are heard, as in the **Ganong effect**.","children":[]}],"review-8":[{"text":"A **late “correction” process** is one prediction for Ganong’s continua. In this account, **categorical perception functions as usual**, but there is a **bias to report having heard a word**.","children":["The listener first categorizes the sound with no help from word knowledge.","At the response stage, the listener leans toward the option that forms a real word."]},{"text":"Three kinds of continua are compared.","children":["**For word-nonword continua**, the voiced end forms a word.","**For nonword-word continua**, the voiceless end forms a word.","**For neutral continua**, neither end has a lexical advantage."]},{"text":"This account predicts a **general reporting bias**. A bias added after perception pushes responses toward the word by the same amount everywhere.","children":["The word-nonword responses are shifted up everywhere. At the long VOT end, voiced responses stay near 0.25 and do not fall to 0.","The nonword-word responses are shifted down. At the short VOT end, voiced responses start near 0.75 and not at 1.","So even a clearly voiceless sound would sometimes be reported as voiced when the voiced version makes a word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** is the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions.","children":["In this account, the lexical effect is **the same size at every step**, near 0.25.","So perception and word knowledge stay separate, and word knowledge only nudges the reported answer."]}],"review-9":[{"text":"A second prediction is that **word knowledge shifts the categorical boundary**. In this account, word knowledge changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other.","children":["Near an ambiguous onset, the interpretation that forms a real word gets extra support.","So the effect changes categorization itself, not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"This account predicts that the lexical conditions differ only in the middle of the continuum.","children":["**At both ends**, the sound is clear, so every condition gives the same answer. Voiced responses start near 1 at short VOT and end near 0 at long VOT.","**Near the boundary**, the word-nonword condition crosses later and the nonword-word condition crosses earlier."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** forms a **peak**.","children":["It is near 0 at the endpoints and rises to about 0.35 for the items closest to the boundary.","The effect is **strongest for the items closest to the category boundary**. Those are the stimuli for which lexical information can help resolve ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","At the endpoints, strong acoustic evidence already settles the consonant, so word knowledge has little room to help."]},{"text":"So where the effect occurs matters. A **flat effect** points to a reporting bias. A **peaked effect** points to a shift in the boundary itself.","children":[]}],"review-10":[{"text":"**Ganong’s results** are strikingly consistent with the **second model**, which is the boundary-shift account.","children":["**At the short and long ends** of the VOT continuum, the word-nonword and nonword-word conditions give nearly the same answers.","**Near the boundary**, the two conditions separate the most.","The **lexical effect** rises from about 0.1 at the endpoints to a peak near **0.3** at the boundary."]},{"text":"A peak at the boundary rules out a simple reporting bias. Word knowledge matters most where the acoustic evidence is weakest.","children":["At the boundary, the word that fits tips the balance.","At the endpoints, the acoustic evidence decides the answer alone."]},{"text":"Ganong raised a number of possibilities for how this could happen.","children":["He focused on different kinds of **guessing strategies**.","A guessing strategy would let listeners use word knowledge only when they were unsure about the sound."]},{"text":"Later models emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes.","So the whole-word representation can affect how an ambiguous part of the word is interpreted during recognition."]}],"review-11":[{"text":"A **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model** tracks **phoneme-level and word-level activations** as the input unfolds.","children":["The input begins with a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/**.","Then come /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/, which together make the word **plug**.","Activations are recorded at four moments, called frames."]},{"text":"**In the first frame**, only the ambiguous first sound has arrived.","children":["/b/ and /p/ are both weakly active at about the same level, near 0.15 to 0.2.","Word activation is still very low."]},{"text":"**In the second and third frames**, /l/ and the vowel arrive.","children":["Partial /b/ evidence supports words beginning with bl. Partial /p/ evidence supports words beginning with pl.","**Plug**, **blood** and **blush** gain activation together, because all three fit the beginning heard so far.","Phoneme activity for /p/ starts to pull ahead of /b/."]},{"text":"**In the fourth frame**, the final **/g/** arrives.","children":["The /g/ fits plug but does not fit the endings of blood or blush.","**Plug** becomes the most active word, at about 0.45, while the alternatives fall away.","So **/p/ ends up more active than /b/** at the phoneme level."]}],"review-12":[{"text":"In the TRACE simulation, **activity accumulates for “plug”** as the input unfolds over time. The biggest gain comes when the **final /g/** arrives.","children":["The /g/ is highly inconsistent with “blood” and “blush”, which were also activated in Frame 3.","Blood and blush fit the early part of the input, so they were plausible candidates for a while.","Once /g/ arrives, they lose support because their endings do not match.","Plug wins because only plug matches the full sequence of sounds."]},{"text":"The model explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** in terms of **top-down processing**. Top-down processing means that higher-level knowledge influences lower-level analysis.","children":["**Activity from the lexical level produces feedback to the phoneme level**.","The feedback gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**.","Plug is the winning word, so /p/ receives extra support."]},{"text":"So the ambiguous first sound comes to be heard as /p/. Information that arrives at the end of a word can affect how an earlier sound is interpreted.","children":[]}],"review-13":[{"text":"**Syntactic explanations** of the garden path effect start with a **parser**. The parser is a mental process that builds a **syntactic tree** for each sentence we hear or read.","children":["A syntactic tree records how words group into phrases and how those phrases relate to one another."]},{"text":"**In the correct tree** for the horse sentence, the subject is a large noun phrase, or **NP**. It contains “the horse” plus a reduced clause meaning “that was raced past the barn”.","children":["“That” and “was” are silent in the actual sentence. The clause also has an empty subject position.","Inside the clause, raced takes the prepositional phrase, or **PP**, “past the barn”.","The main verb phrase, or **VP**, contains only **fell**."]},{"text":"**In the failed parse**, “the horse” is an ordinary NP. The main VP is “raced past the barn”, with raced as the main verb.","children":["**Fell** is then left with nowhere to attach, so the parse breaks down."]},{"text":"The parser runs inside a cognitive system with **limited memory** and **time constraints**.","children":["Words arrive quickly, so the parser cannot wait for the end of the sentence before building structure.","It can keep only a limited number of structures available at once."]},{"text":"That is why an account of parsing has to explain how the parser handles an ambiguous sentence under these limits.","children":[]}],"review-14":[{"text":"The **grammatical version** of the horse sentence is much more complex syntactically than the failed parse. It has **many more nodes**.","children":["A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping, such as an NP, a VP or a sentence.","The correct tree needs extra nodes for the embedded clause and for the silent “that” and “was”.","The failed parse makes “raced past the barn” the main VP, which takes far fewer nodes."]},{"text":"**“Minimal attachment”** is a strategy in which the parser **initially prefers smaller trees**.","children":["The parser fits each new word into the existing structure with the fewest possible new nodes.","Fewer nodes means less structure to build and hold in memory at that moment, so the strategy saves space."]},{"text":"In the horse sentence, minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb**, because that analysis needs the fewest nodes.","children":["The reading where “raced past the barn” modifies horse needs an embedded relative clause. So the parser passes it over at first.","When fell arrives, it has no place in the small structure. The parser has to switch to the larger structure and rebuild the tree."]},{"text":"So the smaller tree lowers the immediate load, but it leads the reader down the garden path.","children":[]}],"review-15":[{"text":"**“Late closure”** is another parsing strategy in this framework. It is a bias to **keep the current clause “open” as long as possible**.","children":["Keeping a clause open means adding incoming words to it instead of closing it and starting a new structure.","So new words attach to the phrase currently being built."]},{"text":"**Frazier (1987)** contrasts two structures for “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite”. The phrase “with the dynamite” wants to associate with the big **verb phrase** starting with “blew open”.","children":["**In the preferred structure**, the PP attaches to the **VP**. This is **verb-phrase attachment**, and it makes the dynamite the **instrument** the burglar used to blow open the safe.","**In the unpreferred structure**, the PP attaches to the **NP** “the safe”. This is **noun-phrase attachment**. It makes the phrase describe which safe was blown open, the one that had the dynamite."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, but they express different relationships.","children":["Late closure keeps the verb phrase open, so the incoming phrase joins it.","Minimal attachment prefers the same attachment, because it needs fewer nodes than building an extra NP around “the safe with the dynamite”."]},{"text":"So both strategies favor the VP attachment. For this sentence the preferred parse is also the sensible one, so readers reach the right meaning easily.","children":[]}],"review-16":[{"text":"Late closure can be seen at work in a sentence that needs the **“unpreferred” parse** to be sensible. The sentence is “The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels”.","children":["Jewels are an implausible tool for blowing open a safe.","The sensible meaning is that the burglar blew open **the safe that contained the jewels**.","That meaning requires attaching “with the jewels” to the noun phrase “the safe”, which is the unpreferred NP attachment."]},{"text":"Careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements** find that people are **slower** to read sentences where the sensible reading requires the unpreferred parse.","children":["Reading time measures how long readers spend on each part of a sentence.","Eye movement recordings track where readers look and when they jump back to reread earlier words.","The extra time appears at the point where the parse has to change."]},{"text":"Readers **momentarily entertain the idea** that the jewels were used to blow open the safe. Then they have to **retrace their steps**.","children":["The parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase, just as it did for the dynamite.","The meaning of jewels conflicts with the instrument reading, so the parser moves the attachment to the noun phrase."]},{"text":"So the final meaning is easy to understand, but reaching it carries a measurable **processing cost**.","children":[]}],"review-17":[{"text":"This syntactic explanation rests on a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure** of the sentence. It parallels the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception.","children":["In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","In the same way, the parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without consulting meaning or context.","Meaning is worked out afterward from the finished structure."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**.","children":["It wants to make the simplest tree possible, which is **minimal attachment**.","It accomplishes this partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**.","Both preferences limit how much structure must be built and held in memory at once."]},{"text":"This is a very efficient way of dealing with ambiguity. The parser **ignores ambiguity until it becomes a problem**.","children":["The parser commits to one likely structure and builds only that one. Most of the time the first choice is right, so no extra effort is needed.","When a later word does not fit, the structure has to be revised. That revision is the source of garden path difficulty."]},{"text":"So garden paths are the price of an efficient strategy.","children":[]}],"review-18":[{"text":"**“World knowledge” can be very local**. It can come from the visual scene in front of the listener as well as from general knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"**Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested this with a visual world task.","children":["Participants saw real objects, which were **a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple sitting on a towel**.","They heard the instruction “Put the apple on the towel in the box”. They carried it out while their eye movements were recorded.","The unambiguous comparison instruction was “Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”."]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” would temporarily seem like the end of the clause “put the apple on the towel”.","children":["In that reading, “on the towel” names the **destination**, the place the apple should go.","In the intended reading, “on the towel” tells **which apple** to move, and “in the box” is the destination."]},{"text":"People **do look at the empty towel** while planning their movements, as though they are considering it as a target.","children":["**For the ambiguous instruction**, a typical sequence of looks goes from the apple to the empty towel and then to the box. The look to the apple comes near 1,000 milliseconds, and the look to the box comes near 2,000 milliseconds.","**For the unambiguous instruction**, the looks go from the apple straight to the box."]},{"text":"So the listener recognizes every word correctly but briefly takes the location phrase as the destination. That briefly changes the planned action.","children":[]}],"review-19":[{"text":"A **two-referent context** is a display that contains **two apples**. One apple sits on a towel, and the other sits elsewhere. The empty towel and the box are still present.","children":["Now “the apple” alone does not say which apple to move.","The phrase “on the towel” becomes useful, because it picks out one apple from the two."]},{"text":"In this context, participants usually look first at the apple on the towel and then at the box.","children":["Looks to the empty towel are much less common.","The **sequence and timing of eye movements**, measured relative to the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the ambiguous and unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"The ambiguous instruction is processed just like the explicit instruction with “that’s on the towel”.","children":["With two apples present, the listener needs to know which apple, so a phrase that picks one out is expected.","Listeners treat “on the towel” as a description of the apple from the start. The destination reading never takes hold."]},{"text":"So the same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context is different.","children":[]}],"review-20":[{"text":"With **two apples** in the display, it becomes **felicitous** to give more information about which apple the speaker means. Felicitous means appropriate and natural in the situation.","children":["**With one apple**, there is no need to say which apple. So “on the towel” seems like extra information and is easily taken as a destination.","**With two apples**, “on the towel” has a clear job, which is to identify the right apple."]},{"text":"So people **do not look at the empty towel as much**. The measure is the proportion of trials with eye movements to the incorrect destination, the empty towel.","children":["**In the one-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on about **55%** of trials. Unambiguous instructions led to almost none.","**In the two-referent context**, ambiguous instructions led to such looks on only about **17%** of trials. That is close to the roughly 12% for unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"The words of the instruction were identical in both contexts. So the visual context alone changed how often listeners were misled.","children":["This is an effect of **referential context**, which is the set of objects a phrase could refer to.","Referential context influences which grammatical relationship listeners assign while the sentence is still being heard."]}],"review-21":[{"text":"An attractive **dichotomy**, a two-way division, contrasts the two aphasias.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Wernicke’s aphasia**, speech is **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**. **Syntax is roughly intact**.","children":["“Jabberwocky” is Lewis Carroll’s poem of grammatical sentences filled with nonsense words.","Asked about his health, one patient said “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds to keep me from mind and up to the ear which can be to find among ourselves.” Kriendler and colleagues (1971) reported this answer.","Asked about his poor vision, another patient said “My wires don’t hire right.” The sentence frame is normal, but the words do not express the problem with his eyes.","Asked if he was a doctor, an aphasic physician said “Me? Yes, sir, I’m a male demaploze on my own. I still know my tubaboys what for I have that’s gone hell and some of them go.” **Demaploze** and **tubaboys** are neologisms.","One patient called a **fork** “a need for a schedule” and a **spoon** “How many schemes on your throat.”"]},{"text":"For **Broca’s aphasia**, speech is **“telegraphic.”** There is **difficulty with word order and grammatical particles**, with **relative sparing of important content words**.","children":["Asked what brought him to the hospital, one patient said “Yes … ah … Monday … ah … Dad and P.H. (the patient’s name) and Dad … hospital. Two … ah doctors … and ah … thirty minutes … and yes … ah … hospital. And, er Wednesday … nine o’clock and eh Thursday, ten o’clock … doctors. Two doctors … and ah … teeth. Yeah … fine.” Badecker and Caramazza (1985) reported this answer.","Content words such as Dad, hospital, doctors and teeth carry pieces of the story.","The grammatical words and endings that would connect them are largely missing."]},{"text":"So the two patterns look like mirror images. Wernicke’s patients have **fluent grammar with wrong words**. Broca’s patients have **meaningful words with little grammar**.","children":[]}],"review-22":[{"text":"The dichotomy can be stated by what is damaged. For **Wernicke’s aphasia**, there are **problems with activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon**. For **Broca’s aphasia**, there are **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words.","**Activation** makes a candidate word available.","**Inhibition** suppresses competing words that should not be chosen. So weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"This description is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**. Paul Broca described **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan.”**","children":["Tan **could only produce one jargon syllable**, “tan.”","He **seemed otherwise to be in possession of his senses**.","He **seemed to understand what was being said**."]},{"text":"Broca’s description raises three questions.","children":["It is critical that Leborgne’s other senses were intact. Intact senses mean the loss was specific to speech, which makes it a **dissociation**.","A “capacity for articulate speech” seems a more plausible function for a brain region than “criminality” or “philoprogenitiveness,” a phrenology term for love of one’s children.","Examining Tan today would mean measuring his deficits at the **levels of description used in modern linguistics**, such as phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic."]},{"text":"So there is a confusion. **Broca’s actual patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca’s aphasics** today.","children":["They were arguably **unable to produce speech at all**, apparently because of **motor control problems**.","They would be classified as having **apraxia of speech**, a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak.","Apraxia of speech can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"review-23":[{"text":"The dichotomy treats Wernicke’s aphasia as a lexicon problem and Broca’s aphasia as a grammar problem. It is interesting partly because it **aligns well with a sharp distinction between the grammar and the lexicon** in late 20th-century formal linguistics.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences.","The **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky’s formulation of the computational problem of language learning**.","children":["We recognize a new item as a sentence **not because it matches some familiar item** in any simple way.","We recognize it because **it is generated by the grammar** that each person has internalized.","So a sentence never heard before can be understood, because the listener can work out how the grammar builds it."]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also gave **compelling demonstrations of the independence of syntax and semantics**.","children":["Neither “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” nor “furiously sleep ideas green colorless” has ever occurred in an English speaker’s past experience.","For “colorless green ideas sleep furiously,” the meaning makes no sense, but the word order follows English grammar. So it is **grammatical**.","For “furiously sleep ideas green colorless,” the same words come in an order the grammar does not allow. So it is **not grammatical**.","Both sentences are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless. So the difference between them comes from **grammar as a separate system**."]},{"text":"That is why the aphasia dichotomy lines up with this split. Wernicke’s aphasia would be a disorder of the lexicon, and Broca’s aphasia would be a disorder of the grammar.","children":[]}],"review-24":[{"text":"**Psycholinguistic tests** are needed to observe grammatical difficulties in comprehension. This is because **in conversation, there are lots of redundant cues to what a speaker intends**.","children":["**Redundant** cues are several different sources of information that all point to the same meaning.","“Man bites dog” is the newspaper example of an unexpected event."]},{"text":"“The dog bit the man” offers **many ways to get the right meaning**.","children":["A listener can form the **most likely combination of DOG, BITE and MAN** into an event representation, **with or without syntax**. Dogs bite people much more often than people bite dogs.","The sentence follows **canonical word order**, which is agent, then verb, then undergoer, or subject, then verb, then object.","The **agent** is the doer of the action. The **undergoer** is the one the action happens to. In English, the first noun is usually the agent.","A lot of comprehension also happens in a **discourse context**, the surrounding conversation, which clarifies a great deal."]},{"text":"Broca’s patients’ comprehension might depend on these **compensatory heuristics**.","children":["A **heuristic** is a mental shortcut. A compensatory one makes up for a weakness elsewhere.","A patient might assume the most plausible event, or assume the first noun is the doer.","Either shortcut gives the right answer for “The dog bit the man” without any grammatical analysis."]},{"text":"That is why the test sentences must defeat the shortcuts, as in “The dog that the man is biting is angry.”","children":["Here the **man** is doing the biting, even though the dog comes first and dogs are more likely biters.","So only working out the grammatical relationships gives the right answer."]}],"review-25":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif** devised a list of exactly this kind of sentence, along with a set of **control sentences**. The materials have four groups of eight sentences each.","children":[]},{"text":"For **control sentences**, group A, the sentence is simple and in canonical order.","children":["Three of them are “The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird” and “The boy is riding a new bicycle.”"]},{"text":"For **center-embedded sentences with semantic constraints**, group B, meaning makes the roles obvious.","children":["A **center-embedded** sentence has a relative clause placed in the middle of the main clause.","Three of them are “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green” and “The balloon that the clown is holding is round.”","Apples cannot eat boys, so meaning alone tells who did what."]},{"text":"For **reversible center-embedded sentences**, group C, either participant could plausibly do the action.","children":["Three of them are “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown” and “The girl that the boy is tickling is happy.”","Meaning gives no help, so only grammar reveals the roles."]},{"text":"For **improbable center-embedded sentences**, group D, the event goes against expectations.","children":["Three of them are “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” “The man that the horse is riding is fat” and “The policeman that the robber is chasing is skinny.”","Common sense points to the wrong answer. Worms do not usually eat birds, but here the worm is the eater."]},{"text":"In every embedded sentence, the first noun is acted on and the doer is inside the relative clause. So the structure stays the same across groups B, C and D while the help from meaning changes.","children":["A listener who relies on meaning should succeed on groups A and B and fail on groups C and D.","A listener who parses normally should succeed on all four."]}],"review-26":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task**. Participants heard a sentence and chose which picture matched its meaning.","children":["Accuracy reveals whether the listener understood who did what to whom, without requiring a spoken answer.","Healthy listeners and patients with Broca’s, conduction and Wernicke’s aphasia heard all four sentence types.","The types were control, semantically constrained, reversible and improbable sentences."]},{"text":"For **healthy controls**, accuracy was **more or less equally good on all stimulus types**, near the maximum score.","children":[]},{"text":"For **Wernicke’s patients**, accuracy was **poor overall**.","children":["Their scores were low even on the simple control sentences.","This is probably a result of their **comprehension deficits**, which affect understanding of words as well as grammar."]},{"text":"For **Broca’s patients**, there was a particular difficulty keeping **“who did what to whom”** straight when **syntax conflicted with common sense** or with the probable semantic roles for nouns.","children":["Their scores were **high** on control and semantically constrained sentences, where meaning supplies the answer.","Their scores **dropped** on reversible and improbable sentences, where only grammar can reveal the roles."]},{"text":"So this **specific comprehension deficit** was taken as evidence that **Broca’s aphasia impacts syntax specifically**.","children":["The deficit appeared in understanding as well as speaking, which places it in the grammar itself.","The paper’s title is “Dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes.”","Broca’s patients lose the **algorithmic** process of grammatical analysis. They keep the **heuristic** shortcuts based on meaning."]}],"review-27":[{"text":"There are **problems with this dichotomy**. **Dick and colleagues** had participants listen to **four types of sentences** and **indicate which animal is doing the action**.","children":["Two pictures appeared on a computer screen, one of a dog and one of a cat.","Participants pressed a button on a response box to choose the animal.","The event was always a dog biting a cat, but the sentence structure changed."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the doer first.","children":["For an **active** sentence, the wording is “The dog is biting the cat.”","For a **subject cleft**, the wording is “It’s the dog that is biting the cat.”","A **cleft** sentence splits a simple sentence with “It’s … that” to put focus on one part. In a subject cleft, the focused noun is the doer.","For both types, choosing the first animal mentioned gives the right answer."]},{"text":"Two sentence types put the receiver of the action first.","children":["For an **object cleft**, the wording is “It’s the cat that the dog is biting.” The focused noun is the one being bitten.","For a **passive**, the wording is “The cat is bitten by the dog.” The receiver becomes the grammatical subject.","For both types, choosing the first animal mentioned gives the wrong answer."]},{"text":"So a listener who always picks the first animal succeeds on actives and subject clefts and fails on object clefts and passives. Only a listener who **parses the grammar** succeeds on all four.","children":[]}],"review-28":[{"text":"When **aphasia patients and healthy aging controls** did this task, a pattern emerged **much like what Caramazza and Zurif observed**. Patients were grouped by aphasia type using the Western Aphasia Battery.","children":[]},{"text":"Each group had its own profile across active, subject cleft, object cleft and passive sentences.","children":["For **elderly controls**, accuracy was close to 100% on all four types.","For **anomic** patients, whose main problem is word finding, accuracy was about 89% on actives and subject clefts. It dropped to about 69% on object clefts and about 73% on passives.","For **conduction** aphasia patients, accuracy was about 75% on actives and subject clefts. It fell to about 42% on object clefts.","For **Wernicke’s** patients, accuracy was low on every type, around 43% to 50%."]},{"text":"For **Broca’s aphasia patients**, there was **particular difficulty with the syntactically complex stimuli**, the object clefts and passives.","children":["They scored about 61% on actives and 65% on subject clefts.","They dropped to about 42% on object clefts and about 54% on passives.","These are **noncanonical** structures, meaning they depart from the usual doer-first order."]},{"text":"Their performance was **overall not so great**, however.","children":["Even on simple active sentences, Broca’s patients were far below the elderly controls.","So the pattern combines a **general drop** in performance with an **extra cost** for complex structures.","The groups differ in both overall accuracy and the size of the drop for complex sentences."]}],"review-29":[{"text":"The same task was given to **healthy undergraduates** under **conditions that stress the language system** in different ways.","children":["The stress conditions included **speeding up the speech**, adding noise, and **asking people to remember lists of numbers as a secondary task**.","**Compressed** speech is speech played faster than normal.","A **low-pass filter** removes the higher frequencies from speech, which makes it sound muffled.","**Auditory digits** and **visual digits** are number lists that participants had to remember while listening, which uses up memory resources."]},{"text":"For **single-stress conditions**, accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts.","children":["Object clefts suffered most. With noise, accuracy fell from about 97% on actives to about 86% on object clefts.","Passives also dropped under noise, to about 91%."]},{"text":"For **dual-stress conditions**, which combine two stressors, the effects grew larger.","children":["With low-pass filtering plus compression, accuracy was about 91% on actives and 90% on subject clefts.","It dropped to about 66% on object clefts and 68% on passives."]},{"text":"The general rule is that **object cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all manipulations**. This is the same pattern seen in Broca’s aphasia.","children":["These students have no brain damage and no loss of grammatical knowledge.","So stressing their processing system is enough to produce an aphasia-like profile."]},{"text":"That is why the comprehension deficits in Broca’s aphasia **may be a “performance” issue after all**.","children":["Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so any reduction in resources hurts them first.","The patients may still have the grammatical knowledge, or competence, but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"review-30":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked whether **Broca’s aphasics’ comprehension deficits are language-specific**. Patients had to **match an action to an object**.","children":["For the **non-linguistic** version, a drawing had a boy pantomiming an action, such as licking.","Participants chose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","For the **linguistic** version, the action was given in words instead of by pantomime."]},{"text":"Controls were nearly perfect in both versions, and aphasic patients made more errors in both.","children":["For the non-linguistic version, controls scored **98.5%** and aphasic patients scored **93.4%**.","For the linguistic version, controls scored **99.4%** and aphasic patients scored **87.5%**.","So **Broca’s aphasics had difficulty with this task whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**."]},{"text":"Accuracy also depended on the type of wrong choice offered, called the **distractor**.","children":["For **unrelated** distractors, controls scored 99.8% and patients scored 93.3%.","For **semantic** distractors, which are related in meaning to the correct object, controls scored 99.2% and patients dropped to **86.3%**.","For **affordance-based** distractors, controls scored 97.7% and patients scored 91.6%. An **affordance** is an action an object allows, such as a different object that can also be licked."]},{"text":"The non-linguistic version removes language from the input but keeps meaning in the task.","children":["A pantomimed action has to be understood and linked to the right object, just as a verb is linked to its object.","A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on the non-linguistic version.","So difficulty on that version points to a **broader problem with action meanings**, shared with language."]}],"review-31":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of this task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area**.","children":["Lesion maps mark the brain areas linked to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation**.","The maps cover three horizontal slices of the brain. Colored regions mark where damage was associated with poorer performance.","The hottest colors, red and yellow, mark the strongest relationships.","The strongest associations fall in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca’s area."]},{"text":"The task did not require producing or parsing language.","children":["Participants only had to interpret a pantomimed action and choose an object.","They did not have to produce a sentence.","They did not have to work out the grammar of a spoken instruction."]},{"text":"So damage to Broca’s area **affects more than syntax**.","children":["Patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved.","Broca’s area supports action understanding as well as grammatical processing.","That is why any account of Broca’s area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"review-32":[{"text":"Infant studies measure behaviors that change when an infant notices something new. **Head turns** and **sucking** reveal what an infant hears without the infant saying anything.","children":[]},{"text":"The **conditioned head turn procedure** trains an infant to turn toward a sound when it changes. It is used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab.","children":["The infant sits on a parent's lap. An experimenter keeps the infant's attention forward with toys.","When the sound changes, a correct head turn earns an interesting visual reward, such as a lighted, moving toy.","Once the infant has learned this link, a head turn after a sound change means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** uses the infant's natural interest in something new.","children":["The infant sucks on a pacifier connected to a pressure recorder. Strong sucks make a sound play.","As one sound repeats, the infant gets used to it, so sucking slows down. Getting used to a repeated sound is called **habituation**.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has **noticed the change**."]},{"text":"Neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound. The experimenter controls exactly which acoustic difference is presented. So both methods test whether infants hear the same distinctions that adults hear.","children":[]}],"review-33":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **4-month-old infants** with **high-amplitude sucking**. The sounds varied in **voice onset time (VOT)**. VOT is the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["With a VOT of +100 milliseconds, voicing starts much later after the release than with a VOT of +10 milliseconds."]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable repeatedly until their sucking declined. Then the sound either changed or stayed the same. There were three conditions.","children":["In **20D**, the two sounds differed by 20 milliseconds of VOT, +20 versus +40 milliseconds. They came from **different adult phonemic categories**.","In **20S**, the two sounds also differed by 20 milliseconds, either −20 versus 0 or +60 versus +80 milliseconds. They came from the **same** category.","In **0**, the **control** condition, the same sound continued throughout."]},{"text":"In every condition, sucking first rose above the baseline rate and then declined before the change.","children":["**For 20D**, sucking **jumped back up** after the change, to around 50 responses per minute.","**For 20S**, sucking showed **little recovery** and kept falling.","**For the control**, sucking kept declining, down to about 15 responses per minute."]},{"text":"The two changed conditions were matched in acoustic size, so the difference comes from crossing the category boundary. The control establishes what happens with repetition alone. So infants responded to a change that **crossed an adult phoneme boundary** but not to an equally large change **within a category**.","children":[]}],"review-34":[{"text":"Eimas's result was **a big deal** because of what categorical perception was thought to do. Categorical perception was considered the **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format** that later linguistic processes could use.","children":["A **symbolic** format represents a sound as a category, such as /b/.","Finding this ability in infants suggested that the starting point for language processing was already in place."]},{"text":"**Four-month-olds** are really little.","children":["Their **gross motor milestones** include manipulating objects with two hands and holding their head and chest up while prone. Prone means lying face down. Gross motor skills are large body movements.","Their **color vision** and general **visual acuity** are still \"in progress.\" Visual acuity is the sharpness of vision.","They are just starting to produce **vowel-like sounds** instead of creaky whines. They cannot yet produce speech sounds well."]},{"text":"This is very, very early in development for an apparently **language-specific** ability to emerge.","children":["Infants sort speech sounds into categories before they can produce them.","They do this while their other abilities are still immature."]},{"text":"So speech categorization ran far ahead of other skills. That is why the result **seemed like evidence for innateness**.","children":[]}],"review-35":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** for speech categories is the decline in sensitivity to sound distinctions that the child's language does not use. At around **six months**, English-learning infants discriminate speech contrasts they have **never heard before**. As development goes on, the ability to perceive these contrasts **declines**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants on two non-English contrasts.","children":["The **Hindi contrast** is a distinction between two \"ta\"-like sounds.","The **Salish contrast** is a distinction between \"ki\" and \"qi\" from Nthlakampx, a Salish language.","They measured the percentage of infants at each age who reached a **discrimination criterion**."]},{"text":"The percentage fell with age for English-learning infants.","children":["**At 6 to 8 months**, about 95% discriminated the Hindi contrast and about 80% discriminated the Salish contrast.","**At 8 to 10 months**, about 67% and about 52% did.","**At 10 to 12 months**, about 20% and about 10% did.","**Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants** discriminated their native contrast close to 100% of the time."]},{"text":"This is **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**.","children":["Without experience of a contrast, English-learning infants lose much of their ability to hear it by about their first birthday.","Infants who hear the contrast in their language keep it, so the decline **depends on language experience**."]},{"text":"Perceptual narrowing is related to categorical perception through the native categories.","children":["As infants form the categories of their native language, sounds that the language groups together become harder to tell apart.","So a nonnative distinction inside one native category comes to be heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"review-36":[{"text":"A **very compelling narrative** was built from these findings. It has three parts.","children":["Speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a **special property of the language system**.","These abilities are present **very early in development**, as the infant studies suggest.","Children start off as **\"citizens of the world.\"**"]},{"text":"A citizen of the world can hear **all contrasts**.","children":["This means children have **innate, language-general knowledge** about speech. Language-general knowledge applies to the speech sounds of every language.","So a newborn is ready to learn any human language.","Children then **rapidly tune** their perceptual systems to the specific language in their environment."]},{"text":"In this narrative, the role of learning is to **select** among the many possible innately specified phonetic contrasts.","children":["Learning narrows down the **inventory** to the contrasts that are meaningful in the child's native language. The inventory is the full set of phonetic contrasts the child can perceive.","Learning removes the contrasts that the native language does not use.","Learning keeps the innate categories that the native language needs."]},{"text":"So learning is a process of elimination, and the native language decides which innate categories survive.","children":[]}],"review-37":[{"text":"**Induction** is reasoning from particular examples to a general rule. In word learning, the rule is the meaning of a word. The **inductive problem of early word learning** is that a single experience fits many possible meanings.","children":[]},{"text":"A child hears a new word while watching a cat.","children":["The word might name a category, such as **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**. These are categories at different levels.","The word might be **FELIX**, the name of this particular cat.","The word might name a property, such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**.","The word might name an action, such as **WALKING**.","The word might name a part, such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings together make up the **hypothesis space**.","children":["Each candidate fits what the child sees.","Nothing in one scene singles out the meaning the speaker intends."]},{"text":"This is a form of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**.","children":["A linguist studying an unknown language hears a speaker say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by.","“Gavagai” might mean rabbit. It might also mean undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else."]},{"text":"Children meet this problem with every new word and still learn words fast. So something must narrow the possibilities. Joint attention, cues to the speaker’s intention and default strategies do that narrowing.","children":[]}],"review-38":[{"text":"**Joint attention** is sharing an attentional focus with another person. It is one way children acquire word meanings.","children":["Two people look at the same object.","Each person knows that the other is attending to it too."]},{"text":"**Gaze following** is looking where someone else is looking. It **emerges around 9 months of age**.","children":["An adult looks over at an object.","The infant follows the adult’s eyes to that object."]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**.","children":["The infant grasps that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific.","If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent."]},{"text":"So joint attention shrinks the **hypothesis space**, which is the set of meanings a word might have. It ties the word to what the speaker is attending to.","children":[]}],"review-39":[{"text":"**Joint attention optimizes word learning**. **Baldwin and colleagues** tested this with two groups of infants who heard a novel word while playing with an object.","children":[]},{"text":"The two groups differed in who said the word.","children":["In the **joint attention group**, an adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with.","In the **no joint attention group**, a voice from behind a screen labeled the object the baby was playing with."]},{"text":"The two conditions matched closely.","children":["In each one, the word came while the infant was focused on the same object.","They differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant and the object."]},{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group**. They did **not** learn it in the no joint attention group.","children":["The hidden voice gave the same word at the same moment. So the difference in learning came from the visible, engaged speaker.","Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough."]},{"text":"So infants need evidence that a speaker means the word to refer to that object.","children":[]}],"review-40":[{"text":"An **intentional cue** is a signal of what the speaker means to refer to. Children use **intentional cues from the speaker** to narrow the **hypothesis space** for new words.","children":["**Eye gaze direction** is one such cue.","When a speaker looks toward one toy, the direction of the eyes marks the object of the speaker’s attention."]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to infants of **16 to 17 months** and infants of **18 to 19 months** in two conditions.","children":["In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy the infant was focused on while saying the new word. Both cues pointed to one toy.","In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket while saying the new word. That toy was different from the infant’s toy."]},{"text":"The discrepant condition pulls the infant’s focus apart from the speaker’s focus.","children":["Infants who attach a new word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy.","Infants who follow the speaker’s gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket."]},{"text":"So comparing the two conditions reveals whether infants treat the speaker’s gaze as evidence of meaning.","children":[]}],"review-41":[{"text":"**Intentional cues from the speaker** include **affective and behavioral cues**. **Affective cues** are emotional expressions. **Behavioral cues** are the speaker’s actions.","children":["An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was searching for.","An action, such as reaching for something, can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"**Akhtar and colleagues** found that infants **used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":["The adult’s emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one meant.","Infants linked the new word to the object that the adult’s expression picked out."]},{"text":"Several objects may be present when a word is spoken. The speaker’s reaction can single out one of them.","children":["For a **satisfied** expression, the speaker has found the desired object.","For a **disappointed** expression, the object is not the one the speaker meant."]},{"text":"So word learning involves grasping the goal behind the interaction.","children":[]}],"review-42":[{"text":"**Word learning strategies** are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**. A **default** is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":["These strategies **function to narrow the hypothesis space for new words**.","They give children a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The **whole object assumption** is one such strategy. It leads a child to expect a new word to name **a whole object**.","children":["The child does not expect the word to name a part of the object, its color, its texture or something it is doing.","A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will favor “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"The assumption rules out many unlikely meanings at once. It also fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":["It is only a starting preference, so later evidence can override it.","Children do eventually learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way."]},{"text":"So the whole object assumption speeds learning by removing many unlikely meanings before the child considers them.","children":[]}],"review-43":[{"text":"**Mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**, is another word learning strategy. It says that, **as a default, objects can have only one name**.","children":["The implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","Mutual exclusivity **emerges during the second half of the second year**, roughly between 18 and 24 months."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view.","children":["The child hears a new word.","The dog already has a name, so the child takes the new word to mean the parrot.","No gaze cue or emotional cue is needed for this choice."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity combines with the **whole object assumption**.","children":["The whole object assumption says a new word names an object.","Mutual exclusivity says it names an object that has no name yet."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity is a default. So later learning can override it when an object turns out to have two names.","children":[]}],"review-44":[{"text":"**Second-language (L2) learning** works as a **model system** for research on the sensitive period. A model system is a common, easily studied situation that answers questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** proposed that the sensitive period comes from the development of **cerebral dominance**.","children":["**Cerebral dominance** means one hemisphere, usually the left, becomes specialized for language.","He also described it as developmental change in **language laterality**. Language laterality is how much language relies on one side of the brain instead of both.","On this theory, the window for learning language closes once language is fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"Two kinds of evidence motivated the theory.","children":["The first was a rough alignment of **brain development** with **language milestones**. Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off. No measurements existed between ages 2 and 18. Lenneberg linked the end of fast brain growth to the start of language learning.","The second was a review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery**. Children who lost the left hemisphere often developed language. Adults did not recover it."]},{"text":"So the theory is about the brain's **maturational state**, which is its stage of physical development.","children":["A young, less specialized brain can shift language to other tissue.","A mature, specialized brain has fewer options, so language learning becomes harder."]}],"review-45":[{"text":"Research on **first-language (L1) sensitive periods** relies mostly on **idiosyncratic cases** or relatively rare phenomena. Idiosyncratic means particular to one individual.","children":["One kind of case is children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere.","Another case is Genie, who was isolated from language until age 13.","Each case comes with other unusual circumstances, such as injury, illness or abuse. So it is hard to tell which factor caused the outcome."]},{"text":"**Second-language (L2) learning** gives a much larger comparison. Many people learn second languages at different times in their lives, often with somewhat **limited success**.","children":["L2 learners are common, so researchers can study large groups instead of single rare cases.","They start at every age from early childhood to late adulthood.","Their final success can be measured."]},{"text":"The **limits of L2 success** are used to reason about the **timing** of the sensitive or critical period.","children":["If later starters reliably reach lower levels, the age where the decline appears could mark when the sensitive period closes.","The **first language** complicates the comparison. Every L2 learner already knows a language, and that knowledge shapes how the new one is learned."]},{"text":"That is why L2 learning is used as a model system for sensitive period research.","children":[]}],"review-46":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested whether **critical period effects** reach **second-language learning**. Their paper is about the influence of **maturational state** on the acquisition of English as a second language.","children":["**Lenneberg (1967)** hypothesized that language could be acquired only within a **critical period**. That period runs from early infancy until puberty.","In its basic form, the critical period hypothesis only has consequences for first-language acquisition.","If it also extends to second-language acquisition, young children should be better second-language learners than adults. They should also reach higher final proficiency."]},{"text":"They compared the **English proficiency** of **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers**.","children":["The speakers had arrived in the United States between the ages of **3 and 39**.","By the time of testing, they had lived in the United States between 3 and 26 years.","A **grammaticality judgment task** tested a wide variety of structures of English grammar."]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage** over later arrivals.","children":["**Up to puberty**, test performance was **linearly related** to age of arrival. Each extra year of age at arrival meant a lower score.","**After puberty**, performance was **low but highly variable** and unrelated to age of arrival."]},{"text":"The age effect was not an accidental result of other differences between people.","children":["It did not come from amount of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","It appeared on **every grammatical structure tested**, although later learners mastered some structures far better than others."]},{"text":"So Johnson and Newport concluded that a critical period for language acquisition **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**.","children":[]}],"review-47":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features.","children":["There is a **strong relationship between AOA and accuracy**. AOA is age of arrival, and earlier arrival goes with higher scores.","**Early learners**, up to about age 8, are often **near-perfect**, which means **native-like**. Their scores sit close to those of native English speakers.","**Late arrivals** have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy**. Among adults, age of arrival barely predicts the score."]},{"text":"The first two features describe learners who arrived as children.","children":["For the **youngest arrivals**, grammar reaches a native-like level.","For **arrivals between about 8 and 15**, scores are progressively lower. So the decline starts well before puberty.","For **adult arrivals**, scores are lower still, with wide differences between individuals."]},{"text":"A “nearly random distribution” has a specific meaning here.","children":["It means that age of arrival and score are only weakly related among late arrivals.","It does not mean guessing, because late arrivals scored well above chance.","Individual scores varied greatly, so two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]},{"text":"That is why near-perfect scores for the youngest arrivals are the clearest sign of an early advantage.","children":[]}],"review-48":[{"text":"The **“critical period” hypothesis** comes in **two versions**. Both start from the same claim that early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The **exercise hypothesis** says the capacity must be used early to be kept.","children":["If the capacity is **not exercised** during this time, it will disappear or decline with maturation.","If the capacity **is exercised**, further language-learning abilities will remain intact throughout life.","So an adult who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child does."]},{"text":"The **maturational state hypothesis** says the capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whether or not it has been used.","children":["So even a person with a normal L1 should learn an L2 less well as an adult."]},{"text":"Johnson and Newport (1989) see the **maturational state hypothesis** as more consistent with their data.","children":["All of their participants had a normal first language, so all had exercised the capacity.","Later starters still learned the L2 less well.","That result fits the maturational state hypothesis, which is the idea named in the title of their paper."]},{"text":"This changes how the **L1 critical period data** should be read.","children":["In cases like Genie, the capacity was never exercised. Both hypotheses predict poor learning for such cases, so those cases cannot separate them.","Only **second-language data** can separate the two versions. That is because only L2 learners exercised the capacity before learning another language."]}],"review-49":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**. A **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","children":["Participants viewed a grid with pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions.","The display had a **beaker**, which was the target.","It had a **beetle**, which shares its beginning with beaker.","It had a **speaker**, which rhymes with beaker.","It had a **carriage**, which is unrelated."]},{"text":"The probability of looking at each object changed over the first 1,000 milliseconds after the target word began.","children":["Looks to the beaker and the beetle rose together early, because both fit the first sounds of the word.","Looks to the beetle peaked near 0.2 at around 500 milliseconds. They fell once the sounds that separate the two words arrived.","Looks to the beaker kept rising and approached 1 by about 1,000 milliseconds.","Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller, peaking near 0.1. Its overlap comes at the end of the word.","Looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"The **cohort effect** is this early competition from words that share a beginning with the target.","children":["Words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds.","They drop out when the input stops matching them."]},{"text":"So eye movements track the rise and fall of competition moment by moment.","children":[]}],"review-50":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey** examined the **cohort effect** with **cross-language competitors**. A cross-language competitor is an object whose name in the other language begins like the spoken target word.","children":[]},{"text":"They hypothesized that L1 words are always activated. If so, native Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the target is \"speaker\" in English.","children":["English **speaker** begins with the same sounds as Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches.","If Russian is active during English listening, the matches should draw looks."]},{"text":"For **English targets**, each target came with an English competitor and a Russian competitor.","children":["For speaker, they were spear and spichki (matches). For boot, they were book and buben (tambourine). For shark, they were shovel and sharik (balloon).","For chair, they were chess set and cherepaha (turtle). For marker, they were marbles and marka (stamp). For barbed wire, they were bark and barhat (velvet).","For plug, they were plum and platye (dress). For gun, they were gum and gaika (nut). For lock, they were lobster and lak dlea nogtei (nail polish)."]},{"text":"For **Russian targets**, the roles were reversed.","children":["For spichki (matches), the competitors were English spear and Russian spitsi (knitting needles).","For marka (stamp), the competitors were English marker and Russian marlya (cheese cloth).","For platye (dress), the competitors were English plum and Russian plashch (raincoat)."]},{"text":"The target and its cross-language competitor are tied only by **sound**.","children":["Speakers and matches share no meaning.","The overlap exists only for someone who knows Russian. So looks to the matches during an English task measure activation of spichki."]}],"review-51":[{"text":"Russian and English bilinguals first followed instructions in **English**. This tests **competition during L2 spoken word recognition**.","children":["The **between-language competitor** was an object with a similar Russian name.","The **within-language competitor** was an object with a similar English name."]},{"text":"The measure was the percent of trials with a look to each object.","children":["With only the between-language competitor present, participants looked at it on **18%** of trials.","With only the within-language competitor present, participants looked at it on **18%** of trials.","With both present, they looked at the between-language competitor on 13% of trials and the within-language competitor on 19%.","With no competitor present, they looked at a control filler object on only **7%** of trials."]},{"text":"When only one competitor was present, participants looked at within-language and between-language competitors **to the same extent**.","children":["The Russian name of the matches pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word did."]},{"text":"When both were present, participants looked at the within-language competitor more. They still looked at the between-language competitor a lot, about **twice as often as the control**.","children":[]},{"text":"So the **first language stays active** during second-language listening. Bilinguals still choose the correct object, but L1 words compete along the way.","children":[]}],"review-52":[{"text":"The same bilinguals then followed instructions in **Russian**. This tests **competition during L1 spoken word recognition**.","children":["Here the between-language competitor was an object with a similar **English** name."]},{"text":"The measure was again the percent of trials with a look to each object.","children":["With only the between-language competitor present, participants looked at it on **8%** of trials.","With only the within-language competitor present, participants looked at it on 14% of trials.","With both present, they looked at the between-language competitor on 8% of trials and the within-language competitor on 13%.","With no competitor present, they looked at the control filler on **5%** of trials."]},{"text":"**English words do not seem to be activated** when native Russian speakers do this experiment in Russian.","children":["The small numerical difference between the between-language and control conditions is **not statistically significant**.","A difference that is not statistically significant is within the range of chance variation.","The within-language competitor still drew extra looks, so the cohort effect appeared in Russian."]},{"text":"So the two experiments give an **asymmetric** result.","children":["Russian words became active during English tasks.","English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","That fits the idea that the first language is more strongly established and more easily activated than the second."]}],"review-53":[{"text":"**The Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**. Spreading activation means that activating one representation partly activates the representations connected to it.","children":["The model has an L1 vocabulary, an L2 vocabulary and a store of **concepts**, which are meanings.","**Lexical links** connect L1 words and L2 words directly.","**Conceptual links** connect each vocabulary to the concepts."]},{"text":"The **L1 lexicon** is assumed to be larger and more strongly connected to relevant concepts.","children":["For **L1 words**, the links to meanings are strong.","For **L2 words**, the links to meanings are weaker."]},{"text":"The **L2 lexicon** is assumed to have strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon.","children":["The lexical link from L2 to L1 is strong. The lexical link from L1 to L2 is weak.","So activation spreads more readily **from an L2 word to an L1 word** than the other way."]},{"text":"This happens because that direction is **emphasized in learning**.","children":["Learners usually learn each new L2 word by connecting it to its L1 translation. That builds a strong route from L2 words to L1 words.","The direct link from L2 words to concepts stays weaker, at least early in learning. It can grow stronger as proficiency rises."]}],"review-55":[{"text":"The Revised Hierarchical Model makes a **counter-intuitive prediction** even for **balanced bilinguals**. A balanced bilingual is a person who is about equally skilled in both languages.","children":["One might expect a balanced bilingual to work like \"two monolinguals in one\", with each language running separately.","The model predicts the opposite."]},{"text":"For a balanced bilingual, the **conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages**.","children":["Each concept strongly activates its word in both languages. So both vocabularies become active whenever either one is used.","**Competitive dynamics** will be engaged all the time. Competitive dynamics means the words of the two languages keep competing for selection.","**Inhibition** will also be engaged all the time. Inhibition is the suppression of the word from the language that is not in use."]},{"text":"This prediction has led to a lot of work on bilinguals' advantages in **non-linguistic cognitive control tasks**.","children":["**Cognitive control** is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","Bilinguals constantly practice selecting one language and suppressing the other. So they might become better at controlling attention in general, even on tasks unrelated to language."]}]}};
